      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CYCLING AND CYCLISTS.</head>
        <p>CYCLING AND CYCLISTS.</p>
        <p>No. lI.—THE HUMOURS OF</p>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>BE H. J. HOLMES (EDITOR OF THE " WHEELMAN")</p>
        <p>Humour in cycling is born young. It dies hard. Its birth generally occurs when the learner takes his first lesson; its death only when the pastime is relinquished. The fun begins when the novice, with a confideni air and the aid of a patient friend, skips into the saddle. Most people think it is an awfully easy matter to master the balancing trick. That's before they have tried it. They generally change their minds afterwards. The humour to be got out of a really first-class novice is immense—the novice himself does not appreciate the fun. Yet he never says so. In fact,</p>
        <p>the more he flounders round; the of tener the pedals kick him viciously on the shins; no matter how frequently he sits down with ghastly and hurtful promptness in the middle of the road, he continues to remain apparently unimpresied. He wouldn't let the bystanders notice that he is not enjoying the circus just as much as they. He adopts a sort of stereotyped grin, which all the pains of the cycling novitiate fail to tear from his features. TUB UNFORTUNATE FRIEND who has, in a weak moment, volunteered to give him "a shove off" generally appears to be the only wild man in the crowd. The humour of the situa- tion does not appear to appeal to his idea of the article.</p>
        <p>When his pupil is not grabbing him round the neck, that young man is most likely trying all he knows to gouge out his teacher's eye, as he lounges out to grab something—it doesn't matter what—to save himself when the front wheel begins to be irre- sponsible in its actions. And, mark you, even at this trying moment the learner's stereotyped grin is</p>
        <p>still there, its cast-iron intensity undisturbed—nay, not even the sarcasm of the modern butcher's boy who drives his horse and trap within inches of the martyr's handle-bar can dim that expression of horrible happiness. That smile is invariably main- tained until the moment of its evolution into the</p>
        <p>genuine hysterical chuckle of satisfaction which announces to the world—at least, the bystanders— that another tyrant wheel has been conquered, that one more bicycle balance—even though it be but a wobbling balance withal—is 2, n fait accompli I I think</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>IT WAS 30IIN BURNS</head>
        <p>IT WAS 30IIN BURNS</p>
        <p>who once stated publicly that the men who taught so many stout ladies in the West End to balance themselves a-wheel, unler the fond delusion that they were learning how to become cyclists, would not have been overpaid at the rate of a guinea an hour. John is an observant man and a shrewd one. At a West End cycling school I once had a chat with a perspiring five-feet-two of humanity. He had just finished half-an-hour's "turn " with a very stout dowager, who had ambitions cyclewards. He spoke strongly and feelingly about the sort of thing he had to enclurl for the modest stipend of R.l a week—with "chant-es." Thn chances apparently were "ghosts." I have taken the liberty of putting kis cycle teacher's ronaarits into rhyme, and I call it</p>
        <p>TUE WAIL OF THE CYCLE TEACHER. Yer torks abart yer 'eroes Wot's called Old England's sons, Wot fell by &amp;shin' 9abres, An' Martin 'Erxery guns. Yer prates aban the 'earties Wot 'olds th• lifboat oar, An' dashes thr nigh, so.a mountings All in the teropest's roar. But abart us f ust-class 'eroes Yer never wa.gs yer cheek— We teaches 'eavy lydies At twenty bob a-week!</p>
        <p>The reasonable cycling creature is content to enjoy his country spin at a mAximum rate of ten miles an hour. Ile never dreams of riding more than sixty miles in twenty-four hours. I am one of these reasonable creatures myself, and I am aware that this confession will ~.au-.e a scornfully amused smile to flicker across the features of those of my readers who happen to be regarded as "scorchers." But I don't mind it a bit. I Mean this article to be amusing. Yet I am certain that I, as "a mere worm "in the way of speed, am in the majority. I am equally certain that the study of</p>
        <p>'I7IE SCORCIIKU " LISto HIS WAYS affords a great deal of amusement to me—and to the majority of wheelmen. Take the Sunday scorcher. I have endeavoured on sundry occasions to wrestle with this problem of humanity—and have failed every time. I have met several young men who apparently look forward to the first thy of the week solely as a splendid opportunity to promote perspiration. I have asked them why they do so, and they say: "My boy! Nothing iike it i Get up at nine, mount, and ride two hundred or so in the day. You won't know yourself in a month." I quite agree with that. I know I shouldn't know myself, and probably my friends and my favourite haunts would know me no more. Besides, I'd hate to do it. The idea of seeking enjoyment in scooting over so many miles of ground in a day is one of those absurd conundrums we usually give up without a moment's Gonsideration. I can quite understand a man mounting his bike with the aim of getting plenty of fresh air add seeing new scenes after the toil of the week. But I feel very much inclined to smile when I hear men gloating over tt.tir Sunday performances. On Monday morning they appear limp, flabby lengths of humanity, with a By-Jove-I-do-feel-baked glare in their eyes what time they assert that they have enjoyed themselves in quite a high-class fashion. Runny, very. Then take</p>
        <p>THE MAN `.MO THIRSTS</p>
        <p>after records. Ilia idea of life is to gratify hi.mger for the accomplishment of almost unheard-of velocity. Do anything with him; drop him down the side of a precipice, and when he reaches the bottom he will be looking out for someone to time his descent. Blow him up with dynamite, and the moment he touches the earth he will ask for a rub down and to he sent c,ti again without delay. He doesn't know it, but he is very amusing. Touring is one of the most prolific sources of amusements connected with the pastime. Imme- diately the new cyclist considers himself capable of keeping erect on his machine for a certain length of time, he resolves to tour !</p>
        <p>If he is very new, and therofore very enthusiastic, he invests in a cycling road map and route book. Now, lam convinced that the compilers of most cycling route maps un the market at present are humorists of the mo.;t:pronounced type. If they were not humorists, they should entitle their literary compilations "Puzzles for Pedallers" or "Problems for Promoting Impatience." Suppose you live in Rugby and wish to ride to Bournemouth rid Bristol. With the exquisite con- fidence of a novice you seize your route book and find Rugby. The fun begins after that. If you succeed in getting on the trail of Bournemouth within five hours' earnest study, under the brain.. cooling influence of a wet towel wound round youZ brows, you are a lucky man. Probably you will have tired of the hunt in two hours—and then you may have chucked tlke road book into the fire, mean- while yearning for ten minutes' conversation with the author. But it is</p>
        <p>ONLY A PITIABLE NOVICE who ever dreams of taking the trouble of consulting a road book when he wants a cut-and-dried route. The man who knows something acts quite sensibly. Be Writes away to the editor of an imluential Cycling organ requesting briefly the favour of a detailed route from Rugby to Bournemouth lid Bristol, with full particulars as to the state of every mile of the road, the present condition of the same, whether they have been relaid' and if so the best by-ways to avoid the had hits; also how many hills, the gradient of each; likewise the names of hotols tlus s - ; &gt;l-'4 a "4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
