      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>ASK 70FclICHR FOR MCDOUGA I I'S PATENT SELF-RAISING IeLOU R.</head>
        <p>ASK 70FclICHR FOR MCDOUGA I I'S PATENT SELF-RAISING IeLOU R.</p>
        <p>deal of other valuable information. That eaitor will stand the request like a lamb You see, he hasn't</p>
        <p>6do that kind of hack-work himself. He Cionny ands the letter over to a subordinate, who, atel several hours of torture, will produce a nice type' written statement about a yard long, and which ill immediately despatched to the querist, who perhaps laying in the meantime changed his mind: throws the carefully compiled route into his fire and writes again for a fresh one, say, from Rugby to Lowestoft vid Chelmsford!</p>
        <p>THE INTENDING Tommy having started on his journey, his next humorous experience will probably be a punctured tyre. No properly conducted tyre has ever failed to puncture on its first tour</p>
        <p>In all probability the new tourist knows as much about repairing a punctured tyre as of building an ironclad. For the first time in his existence, per- haps, he realises how very important air is.to the proper enjoyment of life. The subject becomes enthralling. Here I should like to say a few words on the subject myself, as it is certainly not uncon- nected with the humours of cycling. Air is a subtle fluid which surrounds the rims of modern cycles. It is captured, but not always successfully, however, in india-rubber tubes. I know, of course, that air is a substance, because the substantial exertions required to force a little of it into a tube are something enormous. The force with which air weighs upon every square foot of the earth is equal to a weight of 2,1601 b., yet we find that a bicycle with a 12st. rider will pirouette almost sky-high off a brick or any such-like surface that fate may leave in its way. A man whose surface is about fourteen square feet—not to speak of the two fiat ones— sustains a weight of atmospheric pressure equal to 30,240.1 b. ; yet mark how a cyclist, who has been carrying that load of 30,2401 b. about for a whole lifetime, will claw around his bike for half-a-day, taking off a pair of mud-guards weighing, say, 21b. Ile doesn't seem to mind the 30,2401 b. a bit, but he absolutely refuses to stand the extra 21b. on his machine!</p>
        <p>TO ALL LADIES the general adoption of the bike was a boon: to those of the fair sex who pined for emancipation it afforded a glorious opportunity of throwing off the thraldom of the skirt. The "rational " dress move- ment received an impetus through the wheel such as it never before enjoyed, even in the days of the great Mrs. Bloomer. The "rational " dress is only associated with humour when it is donned by a lady whose figure does not look as if it were exactly called upon by Nature to meet the requirements of the case. Thus: Mrs. Stoutly De Shorty, a matron of forty, adopted the "rational" dress, but the critics said: "Fie! You're a positive guy!" Her costume was not a success. But young Miss De Muir, when going on tour, decided that skirts mustn't fetter her. So knickers she aired, and critics declared: "She looks simply charming," &amp;c. Figure and face altered tits case.</p>
        <p>Were the donning of the "rational" costume con- fined to the young, slim-built and pretty, it might have succeeded in "catching on." But alas we find it mostly bulging around such massive struc- tures of womankind as to bury any grace it might have owned. Else, it is seen hanging pensively upon the bony limbs of that attenuated lankiness almost invariably associated with maiden ladies of uncertain age, who object to mankind on principle. Such are a few of the humours of cycling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>NEEDFUL TO KNOW THE ROAD.</head>
        <p>NEEDFUL TO KNOW THE ROAD.</p>
        <p>When John Gilpin started out on that famous ride of his he WU delayed a little by a chance for business,and had a bit of bad luck afterwards, buz he knew the road te the Bell at Edmonton, and fi ally arrived there ac- cording to the programme. And that is the main thing. The best of us may break down on the way, but so long as we are dead sure it is the way we are not likely to fail wholly in our purpose. The principle I am Blast retmg scarcely reeds it. When we employ others to work for us,for exomple, it is plea- cant to observe that they understand what they are hired to do,and are making a nest and workmanlike job of it. Especially is this s if the job is important and we are in something of a hurry about it. And above all is it true when it is one of relieving present pain and arresting the progress of dangerous disease. A doctor's j rb,of course, this would b. ; attd we are intensely coa- cerned that the man who undertskes it should, in the highest sense of the phrase, 1.-.40w his busines v. That the raid he must travel is often hard to reek out ; that his task is vexatious and discouraging ; yes, we must admit it is so. Nevertheless, there is no alternative. We are obliged to trust him,to look to him,to hope in his know- ledge and 6kill,to place our weak and sufferiae selves in his hands as lambs nestle in the arms of the shepherd whom they know. If he is equal to his task all is well ; if not—then what? o For some 35 years," says one who is able to speak intelligently on ttis point, " EiOCO I was a woman of 22, I have suffered from dyspepsia and weaknees, I was always weary, languid, and tired. I was seldom free from giddiness and pains in the head. I had no relish for food, and no matter how light and simple it might be I had pain at the chest and under the shoulder blades. I was frequently sick, vomiting a bitter fluid. I had also a gnawing sensation at the stomach which nothing relieved. Year after year I continued in this weakened state of health. As time passed by I was attacked with rheumatism, which affected my arms chiefly, although I had pain in a lesser degree all over me. I was quite crippled with it, being unable to dress myself without ass,stance.. I had my arms wrapped in cotton wool and did all I could to eaee the pain ; but got little sleep or rest, night or day. "My limbs felt heavy, and the act of moving about WAS painful to me. I got extremely weak and life was a misery to me, but I fought against this feeling as well as I could. I consulted one doctor after another,and saw several of our leading physicians wh gave me the best of medical treatment, but with no better result than to relieve me temporarily. They did not seem to yet to the bottom of mv ailment, and this was probably the reason why their medicines and applications were of no avail. "In October, 1892 a small book was sent me by polt, in which I read of cases like mine being cared by Mother Seigel's Syrup. Thinking it mieht he pme I bought a bottle at Magor's Drug Stores, Corporation-street. In a short time I felt it was doing me good. Nly appetite improved and food gave me leas pain. So I continued with it, and gradually the weakness and rheumatism left me, and ultimately I was again well and strong. Of course, it took some time to accomplish this result, but I never looked back, feeling better and stronger every day. My friends wondered at the change and asked what remedy I had finally used to cause it. I replied, "Mother Seigel's Syrup, and that alone." You can pub- lish this statement and refer anyone to me or to my husband."—(Signed) (Mrs.) G. Wilson, 73, St. Clement's Road, Neehell'e Park Road, Birmingham, October 27, 1897. Mr. and Mrs. Wilson are well-known and highly res- pected in Birmingham,and the lady is slid to be particu- larly earnest in praising and commending the medicine to which she is indebted for her recovery. The reason why Mother Seigel's Syrup succeeded while other modes of treatment failed is suggested by the werds of Mrs. Wilson herself : "It went to the bottom of her ail- ment." That is, it proved a specific for her true and only disease, chronic dyspepsia from which the rheuma- tism arose, and of which it was virtually, a symptom. Had the doctors used it they would of course have cured her with it and obtained the credit for so excellent a result. But good work calls for the proper tools ; and he who would reach his destination must know the way.</p>
        <p>Note GENIHNE Makers st prently recl-u.ceNdiPArNienes°Rl4eTNormE UE VIO S. UNS, &amp;C. ARCHER AND SONS'. €,Se'sbSyTOCK. Also BANJOS. Of T 45-49. LORD-STRERT. LIVEFPOOL Established 448 lelephcrie 5615. -</p>
        <p>, 7 ITALIAN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>"CAIITAL" CHAT.</head>
        <p>"CAIITAL" CHAT.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon 'the death occurred of the Right Hon. Spencer Horatio Walpole, the oldest of Mr. Gladstone's Parliamentary contemporaries. at Ealing, aged ninety-one year.. tie ha,l two great crises in his life. One was when he refuse,i to have anything to do with the Tory Reform Bill,the other when,under the lead of Mr. Edmund Beale,the rails of Hyde Park were pulled down during the Reform Agitation. Then he wept. He was a fine old man, with more principles than most modern Conserva- tives. and Mr. Gladstone always had a great regard for him, though they were, as it happened, never colleagues in a Government. Mr. Gladstone was once the hope of "the stern, unbending Tories" ; Mr Warpole remained a stern, unbending Tory all his life. However, his distinguished son, who is Secretary to the Post Office, followed Mr. Glad- stone's lead so far as he showed himself in politics. Mr. Walpole's seconi son is Assistant-under- Secretary at the Inaia Office, and follows his father's party. The family are direct descendants of two Prime Ministers—Walpole, who became Earl of Orford. and Spencer Parceval, who was ass- assinated by Bellingham in the Lobby of the House of Commoiv, May 11, 1812. Three years older than Mr. Gladstone, Mr. Warp ole, so long as be was seen in public, retained the fine profile and appearance which made him one of the most distinguished- looking men who sat in our days in the House of Commons.</p>
        <p>It must be admitted that the arrangements for Mr. Gladstone's funeral,have given rise to consider- able disappointment among members of Parliament. It is expected that the result of fixing the funeral for Saturday instead of deferring it until after the holidays will be to diminish the attendance of members of Parliament by at least one-third. A large number of members have made arrangements which they find it impossible to cancel, for the end of the present week, in the belief that nothing would happen to call them back to town. Some or these engagements were made after the announce- ment that a State fureral would take place, and in the conviction that the ceremony would be of a character which would necessitate protracted pre- parations. Another drawback is that the shortness of the time and the express wishes of the family di,spose of the idea of an imposing procession such as many members desired and the responsible offi- cials were quito willing to organise, There is no doubt, however, that the proceedings at the funeral will be interesting and imposing, but they will not be all that was looked for, on the other hand, one can well understand that there were strong reasons for delaying the ceremony longer thau was ab- solutely necessary; whilst it is generally recognised that the stipulation for simplicity made by the family is quite in accordance with all that is known of Mr. Gladstone'a own views on these matters. It is obvious that any prolonged interval before the funeral would necessarily have been a period of most painful suspense for those most nearly con- cerned ; and I believe I am right in saying that the arrangements actually ad3pted have received ap- proval in the very highest quarters.</p>
        <p>According to the standard of the auction-room, one of the most fluctuating of barometers, Sir Edward Burne-Jones has now attained the meri- dian of his fame. At Christie's his masterpiece, "The Mirror of Venus," was sold to Mr. Fairfax Murray at no less a price than s,4so:gulneas—the largest sum ever paid for a work by this artist, and almost the largest ever paid for any modern pic- ture durin..: the lifetime of its painter. Examples of Mr. G. F. Watts's art were flattered at the same sale by the keen comptition of buyers who judge a picture by its commercial worth, while some woiks by Rossetti were shown to have exactly tripled in value since last they changed hands, twelve years ago. For example, a replica on a smaller scale of the great picture in the Walker Art Gallery at Liverpool, "Dante at toe Bier of Beatrice," was bought at 1,000 guineas, Mr. Agnew willing gave 3,000 guineas for it. The former " record" Or maximum price for a Rossetti picture was .£1.207, obtained for the "Beata Beatrix " in 1880. Even in the market, thAretore, the famous "P.R.8." is justified of its children.</p>
        <p>There has been quite a run on penny lives of Mr Gladstone, and thousanfis have been suld in the streets of London. Some memorial badges are now on sale at a penny each. These consist of a small photograph of the lamented statesman,set in a metal frame, over which are placed two crossed axes. The publishers also find there is a call for the more ex- pensive histories of Mr. Gladstone's career, and some of those firms who have not hitherto issued such a volume are now hastily prenaring one. To the coming "Contemporary" Mr. G. W. E. Russell contributes an article on Mr. Gladstone's theology.</p>
        <p>The latest report received at the Foreign Office of the progress of the " Cape to Cairo "telegraph is that it has reached Kota Kota, en the western shores of Lake Nyassa, and it was hoped that next month it would reach Abercorn, at the south end of Lake Tanganyika. The British Commission at Zomba appears to anticipate great good to the Cen- tral Africa Protectorate from the portion of the line already laid by giving a much needed stimulus to the trade of the country and to its settlement. It will be remembered that the line starts from Um- tali, then through Portuguese territory via Tati, whence it joins the existing line to Blantyre. From Blantyre it goes by way of Mponda and the west shore of Lake Nyassa to Abercorn. Hence, if it has luck, it should go by the west shore of Lake Tan- ganyika, through the territory of the Congo Free State, to Uganda, and so on, through Madi Lado and the Nile watershed, to Khartoum. The latter per- tion of the route will be the worst to deal with,and the most sanguine Rhodesian hardly expects to see the line completed before the end of the century.</p>
        <p>Very high praise is due to the Royal Horticul- tural Society for the magnificent show of flowers and fruit which was opened on Wednesday in the Temple Gardens. Almost every product of the gar- den, the greenhouse, and the orchard was to be seen in perfection. That has been true of every show in the Temple Gardens for years past. What chiefly is to be noted is the great improvement in the ranging of the exhibits with a view to effect and the avoidance of overcrowding. The setting out of banks of suberb caladiums was a case in point. Nothing better in the way of discreet grouping could be imagined. To convey an ade- quate idea of the beauty of Mr. Leopold de Roth- child's miniature orchard of trees in lots is not pos- sible. It included cherries, plums, peaches, apricots greengages, ripe in some cases and in others approaching maturity; and. gracefully overhang- ing the rest, a few well-laden vines. Of course one looks for novelty on these occasions. It was pro- vided by the " Acalypha Sanderi," a tall, shrub-like plant, whose broad green leaves are plentifully diversified by long, Bleeder, crimson tassels, not unlike those of the old fashioned cottage garden plant known as" Love lies bleed- ing." From a decorative stand plint it was interest- ing. The orchid-grower was well to the fore.</p>
        <p>There is a curious rumour afloat to the effect that the Government ars already considering the advisa- bility of abandoning Wei -Ilai•Wei. I give it with all reserve,but it is pretty certain that the opinion of experts tends to the conclusion that the advan- tages of the place will not repay the unavoidable cost of occupying it. This view is shared by some influential supporters of the Government in the Lio. use of Commons,and the speeches of prominent Miniaterialists who are expected to appear en the Platform during the Whitsuntide recess may be found worth perusal as far as they deal with this subject. At all events, the recent developments of the Government policy in the Far East are likely to come in for a good deal of candid criticism from member. on their own side of politics.</p>
        <p>Thirteen measures were mentioned in the Queen's Speech at the opening of the current Parliamentary session. We are now more than half-way on the road to the prorogation, yet not one of the bills enumerated has been passed into law. Several of them—notably the London Municipality Bill, on which the County' Council election was fought— have not even been introduced. Of those that are likely to find a place on the statute book the Irish Local Government Bill and the Prisons Bill are the most important. Only by making a heavy draft on the time of the future was it found possible on Wednesday to get the Local Government Bill through Committee. Consideration of many of the new clauses was postponed to the report stage, so that although the measure has already occupied more than three Parliamentary weeks, it may still be expected to make large demands on the attention of members.</p>
        <p>CADBURY'S CoCla has a world-wide reputation ass. deli- cious, strengthening beverage ad a valuable nutritive fond The ..Lane,t" says it representB "the standard of hlgheat purity.,' Always insist on having CADBURY'S—soId only in Packets and Tins—as other Cocoas are often substituted for the sake of eatra. proSt.-4dat (2.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
