      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Gh NE RAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>Gh NE RAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Tuesday wee the 79th anniversary of the Queen's birthday. It all the eggs of fish came to maturity, the ocean would be choked with their bodies.</p>
        <p>Dr. Conan Doyle Is about to come out as a poet with a book to he called " bongs of Action." Two miners were tiled on Monday by a cage accident at Aston Hall colliery. on the if awarden estate Mr. Edward Bellamy. author f " Looking Backward,' died on Sunday at Chicopee Falls, Massachusetts. Galileo suffered imprisonment on account of his devotion to science. His old age was darkened by great poverty. Three men were killed on Monday morning In the low main seam of the Kelloe Pit, Bast Ireton, Durham, by the fall of a stone.</p>
        <p>People should not eat when tired, much troubled or imme- diately after severe mental labour. At such time digestion is very imperfectly performed. A physician asserts that the pain of neuralgia, if superfi- cial, can be relieved by throwing a beam from a bright arc light upon the affected part. One of the latest quasi-scientificlfads is the adoption of a non starchy diet. The devotees restrict tli-ir foods to olive oil, fruits, eggs and milk. _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Spencer Horatio Walpole, the oldest of Mr. G:adstone's Parliamentary contemporaries, died at Ealing on Sunday afternoon, aged ninety-one years. Near the Caspian Sea there are several " eternal fires„' so-called by the natives. whEre natural has la.ues from the ground, and has been on fire for ages. The lowest human habitation is said to be that of the coal miners in Bohemia, some of whom make their dwellings at a point otrer 2.00Gf t below the level of the sea. An explosion of fire-damp occurred at Mons on Wednes- day at the Cracket Plcquery C II cry, belonging to the Char- bonnages Beiges. Over twenty mess were killed. A Minter's telegram, dated Kherson, May 21, states :-136 Jews have arrived here on their return to their former Russia homes from the late Baron Hirsch's colony in Argentina. The final composition of the French Chamber is as to:lows t-- Republicans 254, Rad teal a , 104. Radical bocialists 74. Socialists 57, Rallied 38, Reactionaries 44, Nationalists 10.</p>
        <p>A statement issued by the United States Geological Survey shows that the output of tha country's coal mines last year amounted to 198 250,000 tons—of 2,0001 h. each—this being the highest aggregate on record. East, Clausen, bomenc tles,Arnesby Brown, and the other artists whose works have been purchased at the recent Academy exhibitions for the Preston Art Gallery, are well represented at Burlington House this year. Bngineers and r.ilway men are much interested In the results of tests now being made ab.oad with mats of felt which are designed for laying under the rails of street r dlway lines, for purposes of protection and sound- deadening. At the Glulldhall, London, on Monday. Isaac S. Parker, bookmaker, was fined £5O and costs for using the p Nate bar, of his brother's public house the White Horse, Farm atreete fur betting. The hro,her was tine I £lO, and had the liaanc endorsed.</p>
        <p>A Vicuna 'Correspondent telegraphs that the Archduke Lepold, CCIIIIII of the liznperor of Austria, died on Tuesday at the ago of .eve' tv-five the deceased served in the army Up to the year 1884, holding the office of Inspector-General hngiueers</p>
        <p>I:he po-w-er of Niagara according to Lord Kelvin, says an American interviewer, even when wholly utilise i, would barely suffice for propelling a hundred Atlantic liners by electricity, This shows the gr at alyorption of energy nec-ssary for quick ocean travel, rather than the feebleness of the river's flow.</p>
        <p>_ Thomas Cheek, barber of Leroy-s rest. Londcn, was at Southwark Police Court on Wednesthy lined £lOO for per- mittinc his brerrises to be used for betting,and Charles Edgar for using the same was fined £lOO. An assistant was dis- charged. Detective-Inspector Freeman. who made the raid, was c =mended for his tact by the Bench. A shocking accident occurred at the dye works of Peter Reid and Sons, Limited in Whit-lane, Pendleton. About nine o'clock the engineer, Frederick Bates, of Egerton-s..ret Pendleton, VMS turning on the steam, when a valve burst, and he was bloa n into a cistern. He was got out of the ci,tern as soon as possible, but too late to owe his life. The first standing army of which there is any record was organized by King Saul in 1693 8.0 The army of Xerxes, in invading Greece, numbered 1,700,000 foot and 80,000 hnsee (480 8.C.) The first standing army of moiern times was maintained in France by Charles VII. (1445). Standing armies were first established in England by Charles I. in 1633.</p>
        <p>_ A. • Dilly Mail" Sierra Lone telegram says :—The garri- son at Kwulu has been relieved by Colonel Woodgatea column. which reached there without having encountered any serious opposition. It Is reported that an attack has , been made on Mabang, a village on the Itibbi river, and successfully repulsed by the newly-formed %Vest African Corps. In the action brought by the well-known actress Miss Geraldine Ulmer against the New London and Suburban Omnibus Company, for damages for personal injuries sus- tamed in a cellitdon with one of the defendants' omnibuses, a special jury in the Queen's Bench Division on Tuesday re- turned a verdict for the plat tiff, and assessed the damages at £750. In the Queen's Bench Division, on Monday. a married WOrMIII named Mary Simper recovers I £lOO .galust the North London Hallway Company as compensation for per- Bond injuries she had sustained whilst travelling on the de- fendant's line, the accident being caused by the negligf nee rf the defendant's servants in starting a train whilst she Was alighting. Rudyard Kipling's line, " Lest we forget," was found on the Nelson statute on last Trafalgar Day. It is also found at the foot of a coloured c rtoon In an American paper showing a sailor with " Thi Maine" on his cap. A contempwary suggests that this proves cmclusively that Mr. Kipling is th Poet laureate de facto, democratically chosen, of the Anglo- Saxon race. .bait el-vec o'clock on Tuesday morning a men named Dawson. a chief ganger in the employment of the Great Central Railway Company, was inspecting a length of lino near Wrawby Junction which his gang Was repairing. when he was struck by a train and killed. His body was removed to Barnetbv, where he resided. Dawson was only promoted to the position of chief ganger about three weeks ago. According to Dr. W. Harris, in " Brain." some Persons completely or partially blind in one eye are subject to visual hallucinations. They see animals or persons moving the blind field of vision, and occasionally take them for real objects. One patient continually SAW in the blind field of view a man standing with two lighted candies in his hands.- Another used to bunt for the spectres in corners or cup boards.</p>
        <p>A Tory Islander, named James Curran. has been arrested on a charge of wife murder. The woman was found dead on Monday morning in her husband's house, and on examina- tion the both was found to bear marks of very considerable violence. There being no police on the island. a district inspector and several constables left the mainland t‘, take Curran into custo4y The man was for three years an inmate of Letterkenny Asylum. The Vienna co-respondent of the "Standard" says that at an afternoon performance of 'Romeo and Juliet 3' in the Bohemian National Theatre at Prague a sharp dagger ins'ead of the usual stage weapon was handed to the actress who played the part of Juliet. and it pierced her dress and stave and entered her body. The actress, Madame Befoul, was bleeding profusely, hut the doctors declare that her condition Is not dangerous The matter will be investigated. . Thomas Dodoson, bricklayer, Parliament-street, More- c-mbe, was cycling down Wyresthle-road, past Lancaster Park on Sunday afternoon with his feet on the rests, when he lost cordrol of leis brakeless machine, and was carried down Minorgate.a long. unridable hill, at a deadly pace finally colliding with a stone wall. He was picked up unconscious and bleeding, and conveyed to the Lancaqter Infirmary, where it was ascertained that his skull was fractured. At Chester, on Saturday, Patrick O'Neill was sentenced to SIX months' Imprisonment for assaulting Lily Jardan, a young lady, in a train between Warrington arid Cheater Prosecutria who resided in Dublin had been taking an Invalid sister to Sheffield. On returning she was joined in the car- riage at Warrington by the prisoner, who grossly assaulted her. She seized the communication cord, and got outside. holding to the handle of the door until she reached Trod- sham.</p>
        <p>O Sunday, at Upper Mafylebone-street, London, lirredeeck Reeves aged about 38, an ex ari Merman, employed es COMMISSIOnAire at a large drapery establishment in the West end, fired at his young wife with a revolver, woundin, ber In different parts of the body and then committed suicide by shooting hlmse fln the mouth Mrs. Reeves was removed to Middlesex Hospital, where she died on Monday. The Duties had lived apart, and the tragedy Is attributed to jealousy.</p>
        <p>A new industry is about to be started by an enferprithig American farmer, He intrnds stocking a farm with a thousand Isla!,k cats, and five thousand rats on wh'ch to feed the cats, estimating th d, the cats will increa.e thousand in two years. Their skins are worth ft d dim! (4s. 2d.) each. The rats will multiply five 'lmes RS fa.t " the cats and will be used to feed the oats, while the skioned , cats will furnish food for the rats. Thus has perpet !al motion been discovered at last. At the Salford County Court on Monday the Salford Cor- poration were summoned for noncompliance with an order made in 1895 in respect of pollution of the river Orwell and the Manchester Ship Canal. The proceedings were i 115,1- tuted by the Mersey and I•well Joint Committee. The matter was before the Court last July. The Judge inflicted a fine of 4100. and th.eatened that unless better progress was made with the Corporation's scheme he would have to Impose the maximum penalty of £5O a day. s A correspondent at Caine, Wiltshire telegraphs that what I su Oposed to be a case of murder and suicide has oe'urrel at Bowood Park. the Marquis of Lansdowne" seat. Rlclisri Lynford end his wife, stated to h ye come from Bedford, enterel the park on Sunday and were missed until Wednes- day nidht when a hat was found on the bank of the lake, which was then dragged, Lynford's body was re: overetand his wife's was found under an adjacent tree with the throat cut. Lynford, who had recently undergone an operation in Wiltshire had relatives la Caine. The menthly °An :rirative statement of pauperism in Bog- gland and Wales has been published, the month under review being March last, when there was, as is usual, a alight decrease as compared with the preceding month. ha Lancashire and Cheshire there were in the first week of the month 3,098 more plupers. or 3'7 per cent more than in the corresponding month of the preceding year. In the second week of the month there were 3,065 more, or 36 per ,•ent In the third week there was again a rise, the figures going to 3.085 or 3 7 per cent, as in the first week, while In the fourth week the figures actually rose to 3.516. or an increa,e of no less than 4 2 per cent absolutely the highest return in Eng- land or Wales by over two per cent " The electric fluid "is reported to have been "discovered " by one Rychnowbki, an electrici-.n living at Lemberg, "Blec- trold "Is the name given to the matelal —for maerial it seems to be -and all kinds of extraordinary attributes are said to appertain to it, It is not identical with the term "ale tdcity." It "produces" light and causes Geissler tubes to fluoresce. It ' works photo-chemically." It, will I, tote Mjects in mid air," and preduce whilpools in water." When la vessel full of water is charged With electroid, the effect Is !an overflow. After making due allowance for the unusual : terms selected for defining the qualities of electroid, its like- ness to plain, o d-fashiOned electricity is certainly striking. The difference. if any, seems to be that, in electroid. M. Rychnowski has ,4at dried electricity separate from any of the usual material objects witn which it has hitherto been con- sidered •,e inseparably associat.d. o!her electricians, so far, have been compelled to Id -ratify tha workings of electricity through it effect on something palpable ; electricity, though spoken of as a fluid, was never considered as capable of being poured from cup to cup or drawn from a tap like Water.</p>
        <p>VUR.—SKIN RUGS Dressed. Cleaned, and Lined For alterations of every desoriotlon. B. COOK Manufacturiog Furrier, 21, Renehew-atreet, Liverpool. lis• talsashtd 24 yam.).</p>
