      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CHILDREN OF CUBA: A TALE OF LOVE AND WAR</head>
        <p>CHILDREN OF CUBA: A TALE OF LOVE AND WAR</p>
        <p>BY GRAHAM REDPATH, Author of "The Baffled Press Gang," "When Things Went Wry," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: Dr. Jacca Cardonna, who has lost money in speculation, is leaving Spain with his wife and two daughters, Castilia and Coria, to make his fortune in Cuba. The girls both leave sweethearts behind them—Leon and Castello.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>CHAPTER 111. FROM SPAIN TO CUBA.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER 111. FROM SPAIN TO CUBA.</p>
        <p>The first stage of the journey to Bilboa was passed with but little conversation to enliven such a monotonous route, where for miles they passed through a mountain path to Brievisca, driving from thence to Frias, on the banks of the Ebro. The ascent of the Biscavan mountains, however, was enlivened by the ordinary incidents and good humour of travelling. After a short halt at Ordunna they pressed on to their destination, reaching Bilboa, from whence they sailed, late in the evening. The rugged grandeur of the moun- tains and the placid beauty of the valleys called forth much admiration, and raised their spirits considerably. So by supper time all four were quite convivial, for travelling induces high spirits. Next day, at noon, when business on the quay at Bilboa was at its height, they boarded the full- rigged barque San Justo, and when the tide began to ebb the sailors heaved the anchor ; and with jib, mainsail, and stern-quarter sail set they passed slowly down the Zbaizabal before a gentle breeze. There were no friends to bid them adieu, with lifted hats and waving handkerchiefs. The sky was clear, and the sea was placid, being one grand stretch of blue. Soon all sail was crowded on, and the lightly-laden vessel, with slanting keel, sped onward to the west. Besides the Cardonnas there was another pas- senger—a young man of medium height and slim figure. His hazel eyes were set in a face which seemed to frequently change its expression. He was as unconcerned as if he were part of the ship, and sat on the quarter-deck reading a book. He might have been seated at his own fireside, so much at home did he seem. Castilia and Coria discussed their fellow passenger, and concluded from his nonchalant manner and careless air that he was chiefly bent on pleasure. Soon he rose, and, closing the book, went below.</p>
        <p>The Cardonnas looked towards the land till it disappeared, and leaning over the starboard they watched the golden sun change to crimson and dip into the horizon, creating a long vista of molten glory athwart the surface of the ocean.</p>
        <p>Leon and Castello enjoyed their stay at Burgos, but, after parting from their lovers, they hastened to their homes, where grief might be more readily forgotten among friends and in resumption of life's duties.</p>
        <p>A few days later a smart, stalwart soldier, booted and spurred, with facings of gold braid, strode through the streets of Toledo, and people looked up in admiration as lie passed. When off duty he spent his leisure at his mother's home perusing the life history of certain painters, poets, tale- tellers and musicians. liesperian literature and art claimed most of his attention, and examples of Murillo, Spagnoletti, Velasquez, Cano, Ribalto, and even Rubens and Titian formed part of his valuable collection. Poets taught him chivalry, and painters endowed him with that deep devotional feeling which is characteristic of the Spanish lyrists and artists alike.</p>
        <p>Leon was an only child, the pride of an invalid mother, who was a pale, sweet-faced woman, and whose beauty years of suffering had not faded much, and whose sunniness of temperament pain had not obscured. Her eyes brightened at his ap- proach and his heart kindled towards her as lie performed acts of almost womanly tenderness and devotion. His life had been smooth and sorrow- less, but for his mother's fragile constitution and his father's sudden death, through a carriage accident. Besides occupying himself with floricul- ture, Leon overlooked two farms and a vineyard— the cultivation of the purple grape being his especial delight. Round the southern side of the city the Tagus wound itself, and this portion being built on a precipice was practically impregnable. It had stood as a tower of strength, the home of warlike rulers when bitter internecine warfare had raged about it.</p>
        <p>But now the place is a mass of narrow, crooked, dirty, unoccupied streets, with a population of twenty-five thousand, in place of two hundred thousand when silk and steel blade making were carried on. A unique reminder of faded glory and fallen pride remains, viz., the half-ruined palace, with its Corinthian columns and stairways no longer guarded by brave soldiers, and galleries no longer crowded with gay and gallant courtiers. Seville lies in the sunny south, in softer climes, in the midst of fertility and fruitfulness, at the mouth of the Guadalquiver. The adjoining hills are clothed with vines, and the valleys are filled with fields of waving wheat, with the warm winds of Africa breathing beneficially over all. Valleys are deeper, mountains higher, rivers broader, and tablelands lower here than in the northern provinces._</p>
        <p>When Spring comes forth in her flowery vest- ments of snowy blossoms, the fragrance of the orange groves sweetens the air for many a mile; and when the time comes for glittering fruit to enliven the dark foliage, the admiration of even the natives is excited at the beauty of the scene.</p>
        <p>The Spanish sailor has given a Syrian aspect to the plains by the tropical vegetation he has brought and planted in a climate where frosts are unknown. The streets and lanes are narrow and dirty. The birthplace of Geber, the astronomer who studied the stars from the cathedral tower, and of Murillo, the prince of Spanish painters, unmistakably tears the mark of the mailed and despoiling hand of the invader.</p>
        <p>Castello lived in the most fashionable quarter of the town, leading a gay life when not called to duty on distant seas, and wasting the golden hours of life's seed-time. To see and enjoy life was the chief article of his creed ; fearless, generous, and impulsive as he was, acts of folly were the natural Consequence. Occasional cruises with his vessel served to recruit and sustain him on his reelFless Course.</p>
        <p>Like early adventurers on unknown sea's,- lured into the snowy brenicers beating fatefully on' do shores of a land of illusionary delight, he was enchanted by the beauty and fashion of the time into the vortex of unhallowed bliss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>CUA 1"11;R IV.</head>
        <p>CUA 1"11;R IV.</p>
        <p>111 HAVANA.</p>
        <p>Favoured by fair wind the San Justo made good headway till about half of the voyage was accom- plished. In mid-ocean she encountered a terrific tempest, and she hardly confirmed in actual ex- perience the hopes which her outward appearance inspired. Though trim and tidy, she seemed bluff and tough enough to weather any storm; but she wofully belied her sailing qualities, dipping her prow whenefer a heavy wave was met.</p>
        <p>"Sailing ships are as fickle as women," said the man at the wheel on daY, by way of comment on the vessel's behaviour during the voyage. - -</p>
        <p>Fortunately for everyone, the ihurricane gave early, and sufficiently clear, warning to allow the skipper of the San Justo to prepare and make things snug above and below. The sails were furled or stowed away in the sail-lockers, leaving top-yards and poles bare to the coming blast; and everything moveable was securely lashed.</p>
        <p>Still, prepared as she was, so great was the fury and, force of the gale that the vessel's timbers quivered like the muscles of some living thing in pain, and the masts groaned as they, bending in the wind, tugged at their fastenings. She reeled, rolled, leered and lurched; spars and masts snap- ping like match-wood as she rose and fell in the turbulent seas. labouring heavily before the gale. Never was ship more tempest-tossed or more at the mercy of wind and wave ; heaving awkwardly as a log, and being driven helplessly along. Below, the scene was painful and pitiable enough. Though battened down, the cabin floor became flooded, and the passengers 'were overcome with the stifling atmosphere, fear and fatigue. - - - -</p>
        <p>They were not likely to forget that awful night, with its vivid lightning flashes, its heavy and deafening thunder-peals rolling along the realms of space, and re-echoing in the billowy valleys of moving clouds ; nor the ceaseless and tumultuous war of seelhing and tossing waters as they massed</p>
        <p>in thew 11:1WIt. In Meet lllelsurenn blast or bell to swell its monoronous moan.</p>
        <p>Trembling like an aspen leaf, sensitive and responsive to 3ver3i fitful blast, the San Just° panted, and lurched, and creaked in every joint upon the angry waters. To the squealing gull and flying albatross she was a mere black speck on the dark and dismal deep. Ere daylight added its silver-grex and intenser tints, the storm had abated, and was followed by a heavy, sickening, sweeping, swell, much to the dismay, discomfort, and.ZlStress of the passengers.</p>
        <p>Though badly damaged, the ship was not beyond repair. It was a mere question of time and op- portunity. The San Justo had survived the storm, which was the main thing, and for which the skipper praised Santa Maria. She had drifted considerably out of her course. Her helm was rendered useless, her topmasts were now the playthings of the waves, her mainmast was almost gone by the board, and her bulwarks had started.</p>
        <p>Repairs were not only of first importance, but they were absolutely necessary to the continuance of the voyage. After the more urgent of these had been effected, the vessel's head was brought round, and oace more the San Justo stood out on her course, with a human hand to guide her, under jibs, mainsail, and quarter-sail. Her rigging was a mere apology, and she presented a most odd sight. Other work was done at leisure, which was ample, owing to the absolute calm which softly stole over sky and sea, as though the combatants in the elemental strife had quite exhausted their strength. And the long-winged birds rose on their snowy, slowly-flashing pinions till they disap- peared from view in the bright blaze of blue.</p>
        <p>It was then that the stranger, who had proved a friend indeed, became intimate with his fellow passengers. Huesca Ferrol had shewn his mettle during the storm, cheering his companions, and attending to their needs and comforts as though he had been the ship's steward. Through his kindness, a debt which the Cardonnas could only repay by gratitude, a sincere friendship was begun.</p>
        <p>"It sprang up with a gale of wind,; said Huesca to himself, "but it will not go down so suddenly. I think it is destined to grow. If I only have good luck when I land, I may yet claim Coria for my wife."</p>
        <p>Castilia and Coria, reclining in a shady nook of a beautiful garden, observed the San Just° sail out of Havana Harbour with their messages of love to those whom they had left behind in far-away Spain. The shelf of sable rock on which they reposed was partly veiled by luxuriant creepers laden with yellow and crimson flowers. The air was deli- ciously cool, and they enjoyed the scent of the flowers and the sight they beheld seawards; but it was a pensive pleasure. From the top of the rock hung a sheltering curtain of old-man's-beard, convolvulus, and other weeds, like torn and tattered tapestry; whilst at its base. grew ferns and mosses. A rivulet ran through the garden, beneath a netted mass of palm, bamboo, and fern, enlivened by the bright- nese of blossomed trailing plants. This was nature in a wanton mood—in her prodigality designing a carious piece of crazy patchwork. Beautiful and fragrant flowers delighted the senses, and whilst,Castilia and Coria fell chatting over old times a gaily plumaged bird chanted to them sweet melody; and then even it ceased so that it might listen to the lullaby of the gurgling brook. Through a labyrinth of path, stem and blossom stood the new home—a pretty building of large, airy, but scantily-furnished rooms, with a light wooden verandah and balcony adorned with flowers, commanding a capital view of the harbour and sea. . The windows were hidden by the coloured sunblinds. The Cardonnas' new /wine stood in one of the picturesque squares of villa residences, a perfect poem of architecture.</p>
        <p>Havana then, as now, possessed an admirable fortified harbour, the entrance to which was dangerously narrow. The view of the capital of Cuba from the sea was impressive, for its lofty and numerous spires peeped out from a forest of foliage. The churches were mostly of magnificent design, and clothed with costly ornaments; and the broad thoroughfares were crowded with waggons and merchandise—the sure signs of industry and pro- sperity. The population was indeed mixed, the upper class being whites and the middle mulattoes, or freed slaves, the lower strata being submissive labourers or degraded slaves. In this city Dr. Cardonna established a profitable practice—one far beyond his own anticipations— and he pursued his profession with a new zest. He also invested a good portion of his earnings in a flourishing silver mine at Villa Clara, which turned out well. Before long he was also the head of a copper mining directorate, for his .geological knowledge was invaluable to the promoters of mining and kindred undertakings. With retrieved fortune came new hope, and the doctor spent many happy, if laborious, days in Cuba. By the knowledge and perseverance of the doctor the Cardonna family soon became rich, and they began in no vague way to dream of the day when they should return to their old home.</p>
        <p>Soon Ether the departure of the Cardonnas Leon and Castello 'visited England, the former desiring to becolie more fluent in the language and more familiar with the people, with whom he had buiiness connections. On the other hand, Castello was impelled by mere motives of curiosity. He wished to have a peep at the life of the great English capital.</p>
        <p>Though -their aspirations were diverse, their objects differed, and their ideas varisd, they had one bond of sympathy—they loved two sisters, which was not a proof that they had Much in common; but the separation was a real bond of sympathy between them. All went well for the first two or three weeks, till they began to enjoy each other's company Jess, and found amusement in their own peculiar way. A visit to the Lake District, however, reconciled them. Whilst sailing up the nine-mile stretch of Ulleswater, with its high encircling hills and Helvellyn, wreathed in mist, frowning down upon them from a grey sky, they suddenly came face to face with danger. The wind changed, the water became choppy, and a long streak of lightning flashed over the lake, the heavy brattle pealing from shore to shore, reverberating among the hills and thundering aown the valleys.</p>
        <p>Fortunately the boat withstood the first shock of the squall, for Castello instantly released the main sheet; but the sails 'flapped like the torn wings of a storm-beaten bird. Many a crested wave dashed its spray into the boat; once the wooden shell was pitched so violently over that the water rushed over the gunwale; but Castello and the boatmen struggled and strained manfully at the oars till a landing was effected, and shelter found beneath a huge boulder. The wind howled, the torrents roared, and the thunder rolled along the heavens in a manner that terrified the two Spaniards, to whom a storm of such violence was a new experi- ence.</p>
        <p>The storm subsided as suddenly as it began, the calm water reflecting the peaceful hills; and the voyage was accomplished without further mishap. Next day, alpenstock in hand, the Spanish visitors ascended Helvellyn from Patterdale, via Striding-edge. It was a venturesome thing to do in April, but that ia why they did it. They smiled at the misgivings of the guides, and spoke of their exploits on their own sunny mountains. The weather remained fine, and they were re- warded with a splendid view from the summit; but darkness came so quickly that they were taken un- awares, and to the horror of being lost was added the fury of a Lakeland snowstorm.</p>
        <p>But intelligence had reached Grasmere, and the belated travellers were rescued by a party of plucky guides at a time when Leon and Castello were nigh overcome with fatigue and lack of food.</p>
        <p>Soon they were taken to a cottage, where the guides upbraided them for their folly, and their vehemence did not cease even when silence, one would have thought, had been purchased. After breakfast the following morning they saun- tered down the road—a pair of sorry figures in the ill-cut woolly clothing of the shepherd—to the place where they had, at the risk of their lives, crossed the stream on the pr.evious night. How changed everything seemed</p>
        <p>_ The air was sweet and genial, playful and caressing, 14inging the blood back into the cheeks of the swarthy men. From the clear blue sky a full flood of light fell upon the mountain, bathing stone and rock in richer reds and browns than they usually possessed. Bright plush-like mosses shone in the sunshine, and the blades of grass flashed emerald tints or blazoned forth like bur- nished shafts of steel. There was a peaty smell from the nut-brown stream which flowed strongly down, eddying round stone or stump, and foaming over the groups of boulders which impeded it. The quiet lovely scene invited reflections. . they ,e 1904 they were reminded somewhat of</p>
