      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <p>MR. GLA.DTONE.</p>
        <p>TtIE FUNERAL ARRANGEMENTS.</p>
        <p>REMOVAL OF THE BODY TO WESTMINSTER. .The coffin 'containing Mr. Gladstone's remains was conveyed from Hawarden Castle on Wednes- day morning to the village church, where it lay until evening, and was viewed by thousands of people. It was a quarter to seven when the coffin was brou,zht by a number of tenantry from the study and out by the " Golden Wedding " porchway. It was placed on a wheel bier; the white and gold pall over it worked for Archbishop Benson. The journey to the church was at once made. It was a very simple procession that was formed, consisting only of members,of the family. Behind the bier were Mr. 11Pnry Gladstone, Mr Herbert Gladstone, .Master Willie Gladstone (heir o the estate), Mrs. W. H. Gladstone, and Miss Helen Gladstone. Following them were Mr. and Mrs. Drew and the Dean of Lin- coln. Mrs. Gladstone and her daughter. Mrs Wick- ham remained behind to drive later in a pony phaeton to the church, with two young dau2hters. By many a fine beech and sycamore tree the proces- sion dipped down into the most, passed under the bridge built over it, and entered the village of Hawarden at its lower end,just under the Institute. The village street, was without sign of life. Blinds were drawn in the houses, and people, save for two or three labourers, remained within doors. When the church was reached the family, and the family only, pnised inside. Mrs. Gladstone joined the group, and the Rev, Stephen Gladstone concluded the Communion Service. About nine o'clock the members of the family left, some for the Castle, some for the Rectory, and Master Willie Gladstone, Mies Evelyn and Miss Constance Gladstose, and their mother, Mrs. W. H. Gladstone, for their home at Hawarden House, best known as the "Red House." . .</p>
        <p>The lying in state in liawarden Church began at eleven o'clock. A little belore ten strangers began to arrive and collect in the village and in the path to the church. Many came by train,hut soon after- wards there began a procession of brakes from Cheerer and miscellaneous carts from the neigh- w boing villages, which continued with little in- terruption during the rest of the day. A great many i were dressed n black; almost all had some show Of meurning ; and many cyclists had put into their saddle-bags a pair of black gloves or a sleeveband and wore them in the village as their contribution to the universal sorrow and respect. Shortly after the appointed time the door of the church was opened, and the first in the long rank passed into the building. The people were directed along the church at the rear of the pews and out at a door exactly opposite to that by which they entered. There was some little disappointment that a nearer view of the ' coffin could not be obtained, for it lay beneath the arch which divides the nave from the chancel, and therefore some distance from the appointed path- waY• The coffin lay between the pulieit and the reading desk near to a place which will ever ba famous 08 that in which Mr. Gladstone wee accus- tomed to sit for a better hearing of the. sermon. The coffin was closed ; the foot of it pointed towards the altar, se that the head was nearer the western win- dow of the church. In the evening, when the body of Mr. Gladrone was carried from the parish church • to Broughton, there was awonderful and most touching demonstra- tion. People had come from all parrs of the country. I They had filled the Hawarden street to ovetfltwing; and in the park they assembled-- ta countless multi- tude,—all quiet, reverently and deeply sinpathetic. In strange contrast with the quietness of the family procession in the morning from this Castle to the church, on the walk through the park and alone the hushed highway was the farewell mutely said by fifty thousand lips, before darkness fell on the village. The choir led the way from the church, it gold cross being held aloft in front. The coffin was wh, eled out into the churchyard, followed by Mrs. Gladstone, her children and grandchildren. When the choir reached the end of the road a halt was made that a hymn might be sung—" Hark, my soul, it is the Lord." It was a hymn not an much sung as whispered by the children. Mrs. Gladstone listened to it as she lay back in her seat with tran- quil and composed expression. In the main street the people stood deep on either side, everyone with bored head. Again there was a pause at the Castle I I' gate, where there was a densely packed throng. The great gateway was opened, and the mile-long avenue,right up to the Castle was lined with people 1 and all sang "For ever with the Lord ; amen, so let it be." &amp; last pause was made at the lodge in Broughton Park. The notes of the cornet which had led the singing throughout were again hcard, this time to accompany " Praise to the holiest in the height "—the hymn Mr. Gladstone so liked to , sing. At the close ot the hymn the coffin passed to ' the highway, and another stage of the journey was begun. Broughton Church was reached at half- past seven. There was a great crowd at Broughton Hall station. Every tree and roof which afforded a view of this,the closing scene was occupied. There is a level crossing at Broughton Hall,and when this was reached the funeral procession was at an end The train was drawn up, and all was ready, There were two saloons and two carriages. One of the saloons was prepared for the reception of the coffin. The coffin was lifted in by the last of the relays who had drawn it from Hawarden. The doors of the van were closed and locked, and the general at- tention was transferred to the saloon where Mr. Henry Gladstone, Mr, Herbert Oladstone, and Dr. Biss were seated. The engine was painted a dead black, On the side was its name "Gladstone," and the lettering was the one dash of colour it possessed. There was some conveNation, some final directions were given, then suddenly and without noise the train moved away. Mrs. Gladstone watched it,and then her carriage turned and drove back to the Castle through the gloom.</p>
