        <p>RECEPTION OF THE BODY IN LONDON.</p>
        <p>In the silence of the night a small company awaited at Westminster the arrival of the body of tke illustrious, statesman. In Parliament ?num the workmen had been busy all day surrounding the 'statues of the statesmen which adorn that site with white deal boards to protect them from the coming crowds. Curiously enough.the only figure that had been finished was that ot the Earl of Beaconsfield which had been swathed in these white cerements up to the neck, and the head alone stood out. Tne precise time at which the special train conveying the body ate • med into the Underground station was four minutes past one o'clock. The Duke of Nor- folk, Canon Wilberforce (chaplain to the House of CoMmona), and a very few other persons were ad- mitted to the platform. After an interval of some minutes,during which the sp,ctators occupying the approaches to the station remained uncovered, procession was seen to be ascending the winding staircase. It was beaded by the Duke of Norfolk. as Earl Marshal, and the offic ale of the College cf Heralds, and immediately behind them was Canon Wilberforce,who had donne I his white surplice and red hood. The coffin, covered with a white silk pall,was borne by six stalwart bearers. On the right of the coffin was Mr. Herbert Gladstone, and on the left Mr.lienry Gladstone,both looking pale and hag- gard after their sore trouble. Slowly and reverently the body was borne urVer the covered way leading from the station and beneath the cloisters in Palace , Yard. This was all the spectators assembled in the station were permitted to see,but crossing from the I Underground station into the road they found as- sembled in front of Palace Yard some hundreds of persons. It was a cold and even bleak night, but I the people had all reverently uncovered, and they remained bare-beaded until the procession had slowly made its way through the cloisters to the archway by the old Star Chamber leading into West- minster Hall. Thence the body was conveyed by the members' door into the great hall. It was then placed on the bier prepared for its reception at the upper end of the Hall. At the bier bad been erected a brass crose, and by the aide of the coffin were several lighted tapers. About forty gentlemen have volunteered their services as watchers. On Wednes- day night Canon Wilberforce, Mr. George W. Rus- sell, Mr. Arthur G. Symonds. the Rev. J. Andrew I Reece (the Rector of Lambeth), and the Rev. R. W. Wilberforce performed this duty. They remained until six o'clock in the morning, when they were relieved by a fresh relay of watchers. THE GRAVE</p>
        <p>The excavation of the grave was completed at 7-30 on Wadnes lay evening. Mr. Gladstone's re- mains will rest between walls of virgin soil. The total depth of the grave is Bft 4in, so that ample room will be available for two interments,the laver of which will still be at a depth greater than the average of Abbey burials.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, _FRIDAY, MAY 27, 1898.</p>
        <p>mony that the great Eaglishrnan who now lies awaiting his burial has been, to use the orceful, accuraie phrase of Lord Salisbury, ' a great Christian man.' That ma- jestic) and kingly life, that noble and intrepid spirit,that pure and blameless character have been an exhibition of what it may be, even amid earthly lirnitatione to be a child and an heir of G )d." In the evening,Mr.ionge's text was 2 Tim. iv, 6 8, " I am now ready to be oftmiecl and the time of my departare is at hand : I have fought a good fight : I have finished my curie : I have kept the faith." In closing the sermon, Mr. Yonge said : "These words of Paul might have well befitted that great Christian who yonder at Hawarden awaits hie burial. Every sentence in it is true of him. And who can doubt that the life so nobly and truly lived has re- ceived from " the righteous Judge" ifs appropriate re- ward P' The organir. Mr. W. 11. Payton, played the Dead March in " Saul "at the close of , the service, the congregation standing. It may las added that a me- morial service will be held in Wycliffe Church to- niorrow(Sunday) evening.</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>holy religion, his regular attendance at the house of prayer, his deep interest in religious work, his defence of what he conceived to be the truth. the ideals he set before him, the motives by which he was influenced, his humility of spit it, gentleness of disposition, and uniform kindness to friend and foe excited admiration and awoke their gratitude. During the last few days letters of con- dolence had been sent to Hawardsn Castle by all sorts and conditions of men. Some had been seat from viii lye clubs and some from Royal palaces. They were not a wealthy or an iofiuential congregation, but they would like, he thought, to express their sympathy with Mrs. Gladstone and family in their great bereavement and say that they solicited on their behalf Divine consola- tion. If they thought a letter of condolence should be sent they would perhaps stand up. The whole of the congregation, th n rose, and while they were standing the organist, Mr. F. Podinore, played the Dead M arch from Saul. The service was a most impesesive one.. CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL, WARRINGTON. At the above place of worship. on Sunday the Re*. J. E. Odgers, M.A. (Hyde), alluded to the death of Mr. Gladstone. Be said that personally he did not agree with Mi. Gladstone in politics but he pointed out that Mr. Gladstone had raised the whole tone of politics _ since his entry into Parliament. REFERENCE BY THE REV. W. H. DRITMNION D.</p>
        <p>At Gatacre Chapel on Suoday morning, the ft pr. W. H. Drummond referred in the following terms to the death of Mr. Gladstone at the close of a sermon on national character: Have lin any degree succeeded in expressing part of the message of the great life which has just closed, in interpreting the meaning of the mighty emotion which has swept through the heart of the English race during the past week ? It has been a revelation of the unity of character which underlies all diversities of thought and pJlitical creed, a Common reference for moral greatness, a confession of the heart to the things which make'a people great. The time has not come for assigning to Mr. Gladstone his special place among the statesmen of the world; but even greater than his constructive reforms and his contributions to political thought has been the personal influence which I has gone down to the roots of the national life,the power of speaking to the moral imagination of rnen,his success infusing the English character into closer unity and find- ing channels for its expression, not by appeals to selfish Interest, but by matchlesi effect of his own faith in freedom and conscience and God. NOW our heads are bowed with a sense of almost personal bereavement,but they will soon be lifted up into the light of a great glad- ness ; for this national mourning is also our re-baptism into national faith. To have felt the moulding force of this mighty heart and intellect has now become an im- perishable part of our national privilege. We are filled with a new sense of the greitnese of our gifts and the magnificence of our opportunities. It is with fresh courage and quickened hope that we take up our task, seeking to fulfil our destiny as a people "towards which time leads us and the will of heaven. REFERENCE BY THE REV. G, HARBOTTLE.</p>
        <p>The Rev. George Harbottle conducted a service in me- moriam in the Lymm Wesleyan Chapel on Sunday even- ing. The text was Acts xiii. 38... After that he hal served his own generation according to the will of God, be fell asleep." Every one had felt it to be unspeakably s•td he said, that the closing months of Mr. Gladstone's life should have been spent in acute pain, but good had come out of that. The whole nation bad been raised into a mood not only of sympathy, hut almost of prayer and worship. No one could estimate the good of this widespread interest and deep feeling, in the awakening of sympathy, in the strengthening of faith, and in the irresistible drawing of men's minds to those questions about death and futurity which are of undying Interest, but are apt to be let lie asleep amid the cares and en- deavours of busy life. The deep feeling aroused, won- derful and yet not wholly unexpected, was tribute, con- scious or unconscious, to one of those events whit% can. only happen at long intervAls of time, the passing away ot one of the world's great men. To us as Christian men, the main interest lies in the fact that from first to ' last he was a genuine Christian, a great man and a great statesman, yet a humble follower of the Lord Jesus. Though a High Churchman th re was nothing in him of the narrowness and bigotry often associated with the holding of High, Church opinions. That he held views we repudiate is undeniable, butthat he looked with a friendly eye and made friendly recog- nition of Nonconformists is alike undeniable. If he looked with a friendly eye on Nonconformists they re- paid him with love and trint. There has never been man who has touched the imagination, kindled the ad- miration, and won the hearts of the Nonconformists in anything like the measure in whicb he did it. His deep religious character was the main element of Mr. Glar'stone's influence,and in the confidence which he inspired. He trusted tht people. They might be misled, but the verdict of their conscience might be trusted. They repaid trust with trust. No man serves his gener- ation without hard work,and in this again Mr. Gladstone is almost without a peer. When at last he could no longer work he pathetically said " I feel like a log." Like a tired labourer he fell asleep, posed, as his doctor said,from sleep to sleep. But not to sleep in uncon- sciousness and inactivity, but, as he fervently believed, in the fuller activity of a better life, a life in Christ.</p>
        <p>RESOLDFION BY THE WARRINGTON BOARD OF</p>
        <p>The 11,!ve. T. R. Matthews, of Earlestown, at the meet. in of the Warrington Board of Glardians on Wednesd day said some reference should be made by the Board to the loss the country had recently ustained by the drath of that eminent statesman, William E wart Gladstone. Be would thergore move that the Clerk he instrocted to forward a vote of condolence on lolhalt of the Bard to the family of the late Mr. Gladstone.—The Chair- man seconded the resolution, an] Mr. Ashcroft sup- ported, and it was carried nnanimously.</p>
