      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>" TOUbEIINGLY BEAU CIFUL "</head>
        <p>" TOUbEIINGLY BEAU CIFUL "</p>
        <p>Sir William Richmond, h a been making sketches of Mr. Gladstone with the wiAw of painting a picture. Much interest has been vpressed as to Mr. GlAdstone's appearance in death. Tnis was hardly a matter on which the members of the family could gratify curosity. Accordingly they asked Sir William Richmond no Mon- day if he would prepare a statement. He has done so. It is as follows:—" The great statesman, so splendid yet so simple in his life, lies without any adornments about his noble head. Not OVP,I flowers are there. This dignity and severity are in keeping with a ch drAnt r of Homeric type. There is no trace of recent suffering. The ex- pression as one watches the beautiful face seems almost to attain mobility—almost j )yons. At times ond is re- minded faintly of his brillant smile in life, but chiefly it partakes of a divine severity. It b,ars no evidence that the spirit his departed. It appears rather to have found rest. I have never seen anycning so grand or so touch- ingly beautiful as the dead face of the great champion of Liberty."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>LOCAL TRIBUTES.</head>
        <p>LOCAL TRIBUTES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARIIa cHURCH:</p>
        <p>The Theorem (Canon Willis), preached at the Parish Church, Warrington, on Sunday from the text "Sithey after the Lord had spoken unto them, he was received up into heaven, and sat on the right hand of God. And they went forth, and preached everywhere. the Lord working with them and c nfirming the word with signs following. Amen." Tivr Roctor alluded to three great lives. the life of Jesus Christ. the life of Moee, and the life of Mr. Gladstone. Is concluding his sermon he said : You well know who is meant by the third life, that of William Ewart Gladstone. I want you to think for a few moments of his life, and then of his death, Throughout the whole, er almost the whole of this cen- tury, he has bean one of the forem,st men I Wal im- mensely struck in reading the soePches in the House of , Commons, and in the House of Lords, with the opinion of the members as to his greatness. Truly he was a great statesman, whether all mm agreed with his policy or not. Ha was a great stateoman, a wonderful orator. Who but be could have drawn by the magic of his vvice, and kept enormous Crowds of his countrymen spell- bound as he went from plaee to place ? Re wt 4 agr at scholar—a double first at Oxford in classics and loathe. matics—a great theologian, and a great and true clinrch- man. Think of that t. For few men knew better than Ihe did the catholic truth. By that I mean the faith of Jesus Christ. He knew it well. He may not have cared , for the connection with the State,bait he knew the teach- ing of the Church and he loved it, and it is well that we should think M that. Fle,the greatest intellect that Gol perhaps has ever given mon, wis devoted to the Church And cl arly understood her welfare. He was one of the greatest of men,a true man, a brave man. Everyboly agreed that he had the courage of hie convictions. He was never afraid to speak or do what be thought was right. That is tire mark of a true man, never to he afraid or ashamed to say or do that which is right. He was a leader and a fricnri of men be loved, nri would not have any distinction or tile himself, hut preferred to remain throughout life plain Williom E wart Glad- stone. And now think of lais death. For months past he had known that the end was near, and he had looked forward with courage, ptlier,ce, and faith to it, and on Ascemion Day,at five o'cloek.when people were aosemb- ling for the celebration of Holy Communion in Ilawarden Church, he passed away while the Rector was reading the commendatory prayer. Let us try to imitate him in standing up for the right and never be afraid to confess our faith or to do our work. At the conclusion of the sermon the Volunteer band played the n ,ad Nfareh in " Saul," the large congregation standing in the mean- time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WYCLIFFE CEIURCFL</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE CEIURCFL</p>
        <p>A passing allusion was made at WVClifh Church on Sunday to the late Mr. Gladstone. Preaching on Sun- day morning, from Romano viii, 17. "If children. then heirs, heirs of God, and joint heirs with Christ," the Ray. J. Yonge stated that Mr. Gladstone showed in his splendid life what it is to be an heir or God," an in- heritor of His Nature, His Character, His Life. "Neva' before," he remarked," In my day, hardly ever in his- tory has there been such a unanimous accord of testi-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>On Sunday, at the annual meeting of the above, the President, the Rev. John Yonge, said that a suggestion had been sent in to him by a member of the brotherhood, that it would be eminently clAsirable, and would meet the wishes of every member, if in some brief but earnest way they were to pabs a resolution of profound sympathy with Mrs. Gladstone and family in their great sorrow and- affliction. So much had been said—and well said—in honour of the deceased statesman, that it would not be an easy thing to -ay anything that would be as much to the point, as what they had been reading from day to day in the news- papers. Whatever differences of opinion might be represented in that bropherhood—and lie knew they had i members of all parties n politic!) as well as.rnembers of all churches in matters of religious faith—he thought they would all aamit that England never had a worthier son than William Ewart Gladstone; that it never bad a statesman who so thoroughly and conscientiously bad striven to do his duty in all the high offices Providence had called him tb fill. He did not think these was a man there that afternoon who would utter a discordant note in the matter of sending a message of sympathy and respect to Mrs. Gladstone and family in this hour of I sotrow which was felt throughout the kingdom and 'throughout the world. He (Mr. yonge) would be glad if some member would propose that a letter to that effect be sent.—The resolution was proposed and carried. HOLY TRINITY CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Special references were made on Sunday to the death of Mr. Gladstone, in the morning by the Rev. 11. R. Wilde, and in the evening by the Rev. E. C. E. Carleton. Mr. Percy Randall, the organist, played at the opening Chopin's "Funeral starch." and at the conclusion Han- clel's Dead March in "Saul," both morning and evening. FaVOlifite hymns of Mr. Gladstone were sung by the choir. ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CaIIRCII.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning at this church, the Ray. J. War- nock, in concluding his sermon, said, for a long period. longer than most of them had lived, longer than any of them could remember,to have mentioned Mr Gladstone's name from the pulpit would have been to introduce poll. tics. That was not so that day. They could join their countrymen all the world over, as well as multitudes in other lands, in mourning a great loss. for the voice of political controversy was hushed. To speak of Mr. Gladstone that day was to remind them that statesman- ship important though it migbt be, was among the things that would pass away, and they must turn their thoughts to themes that could never die. It was to the great honour of the deceased statesman that in all he did he was actuated by the highest motives. On that point those who differed from him in the affairs of State were not less generous in their appreciation than those who sympathized with him. Of the public services, of the diversifiod gifts and acoomplishmests of the deceased statesman it would not become him to spoak. But ho thought it was very significant that wheu four years ago be practically took leave of public life, he devoted him- self to the study of theology. That after all was the permanent theme. However much they might be pre- occupied with other matters now, a time would come when they would be interested in the kingdom that could not be moved. For there were things Visit were permanent, though thoy might not think so. Person- ality was more pronounced than achievement. When everything else passed away character remained, and that, as they knew, was largely moulded and influencod by the fleeting things of time. BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The Rev. William Potts, took his text from Matthew xxii. 32," God is not the God of the dead but of the living." After dwelling on the teaching of the passage that those who had passed from earth have not ceased to exist, but have gone to a fuller life, a higher develop- ment of spiritual being, Mr. Potts referred to Mr. Glad- stone as the greatest statesman of modern times. Men of all parties were agreed as to the loftiness of his char- acter and the breadth and humanity of his aims. Lord Salisbury's pronouncement would be generally accepted, that he was a great Christian statesman and a great Christian man. Two years ago Mr. William Watson, in some of the noblest lines which this century has pro- duced, summoned Mr. Gladstone to what proved the last effort of his public life : "Lift up thy VOlO9 once more. The nation's heart Is old as Anatolia's mountain snows. Oh. from these alien paths of base repose Call back thy England ere thou too depart, Ere on some secret mission thou too start With silent footsteps whither no man knows." Everybody knew how that call was responded to in that magnifinent speech delivered at Liverpool on the Armenian question. And now the great atatesman had gone with" silent footsteps" into the great unknown land. But he had gone with calmness hnd assurance. The hymns sung round his deathbed, the Prayers he uttered or to which he gave assent, gave witness to the faith he had in Christ and immortality. In this strong materialistic age it was not a little thing that this great Intelligence was found at last trusting in the eternal verities,and going forth with confidence into the future. For him also to die " was gain." The scene presented at Hawarden, in which when the statesmen was dying his favourite hymns were sung and his soul was com- mended to God, would sink deeply into the minds of all countriaa, and would not be without its lesson. The Dead March in " Saul " was played by the organist, Mr. Peter Harrison, at the close of the service, the congrega- tion standing. Mr. Gladstone's hymns were sung dur- ing the service. GOLBORNE.STREET BAPTIST CHURCH. At this church on Sunday morning the Pastor (Rev. James T. Schofield) • preached from the text, "The righteous hath hope in his death." He said : It is not necessary for me CO tell you what event has suggested this text to my mind. During the past week one of the greatest men, and in many respects the greatest man of o the present century has passed away. The message,"Mr, Gladstone is dead, has passed from one t " another wherever the English language is spoken, and the same massage has been repeated in many foreign languages. There have been two great men during this century who have towered head and shoulders above their con- temporaries, who stand forth as some tall tower or 'ofty mountain peak. These two men were not crowned sovereigns or titled peers. 'rhey were not ambitious for worldly honmrs ; their chief characteristie waa devotion to Hod and to their fellow, men. These two men are Charles Haddon Spurgeon and William E evert Gladstone. Both commenced public life in their youthful days, but Mr. Bourgeon had finished his lire's work at an age when Mr. Gladstone had a great part of his work before him At the age of 65 Mr. Gladstone tendered his resignation as leader of the Liberal party, feeling that he would vote the closing years of his life to religious subjecta. ' Little did he think that he had well nigh twenty years of public life before him, and that a great part of his political activity was yet to opine. He did retire from public life a few years before his death, and we learnt with admiring wonder that in his retirement he was de- voting himself to religious work. But now his long I I eventful life is completed. Whatever may be our non- tics,or our religious viewa,we admire and love Mr.Glarl-1 his strong senie ei Justice, his stone for his integrity, 4 earnest desire to advance human liberty, and above all things his devotion to God. As Bapriets Wit cannot agree with some of his views respecting the Church. But we are one with him in the great fundamental truths of Christianity, and it is not because we regard him as a great politick". hut because we regard him as a great naint,a servant of God and the Lord Jesus Christ,that we make special mention of his name from this pulpit. His consistent Christian life, in the State and in the family,in public and in private,his patient auffering and the calm confidence with which he received the an- nouncement of the fatal character of his malady have been strong evidence of the truth and power of the Christian religion. As I read the accounts of his illness and death the words of our text came into my mind, "The righteous bath hope in his death." _At the close of the service the congregation stood whilst the Dead March in "Saul " was played by the organist. LEGII-STREET PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHAPEL. At the close of his sermon in Legh-street Chapel on Sunday evening, the Rev. Cha.rles Finch said a prince and a great man had fallen.and it would not be inappro- priate, he thought, if he said a few words about him. The genius of Mr. Gladstone was acknowledged by all sections of the community. Ha was undoubtedly the greatest statesman of the century. His brilliant talents, mental force, extensive knowledge, and intense enthu- siasm or humanity enabled him to create and guide public opinion and influenee the passing of laws which have condoned to the good of the country and the stability of the State. When the history of the present century was written, the name of William Ewart Glad- stone would occupy no unimportant place. But on that day and in that place they wished to speak of him as a 'Christian. fll unfaltering faith In the Ohriti.l. their</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>TO WOMEN.</head>
        <p>TO WOMEN.</p>
