      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>CHRIST. CHURCH, .L ATCHFORD..</head>
        <p>CHRIST. CHURCH, .L ATCHFORD..</p>
        <p>Oa Sunday morning, in preaching from the teat I. St. Peter ii. 16, " As free, but as the servants of God," the R.v. Screeton paid an elequent tribute to Mr. Gladstone.</p>
        <p>PADG ATE.</p>
        <p>At the Wesleyan Cl-pi-rcli—c;li—Sunday morning, the preacher, Mr. Light, made special reference to the death of Mr. Gladstone. The palpit, communion table and organ screen were draped in black for the late statesman and special hymns were yen hired by the choir and con- gregation. At the end of the evening service the "Dead March" was played on the organ by Mr. Baker. The Rev. J. J. Langharn, preaching at Carist Church, Padgate, on Sunday morning from the words" It is good that a man should both hope and quietly wait for the salvation of the Lord," also made touching reference to the deceased gentleman, whose death he remarked was being lamented all over the world. Mr. Blackwell played the" Dead March " at the close of the service. STOCKTON HEATH MEN'S CLASS. At this class on Sunday afternoon, b.efOre commencing the usual lesson, the conductor (Mr. H. J. Westbrook) said it was almost a duty at a gathering of men like that to make some humble reference to the great states- man who bad passed to hie rest daring the week. There were four or rive points that stood ont most prominently in his life, which it would be profitable for them all to ' remember. They were, first, his steadfast faith in the Christian religion. The next point was his lifelong love for and attachment to the Church—the National Church —which he looked upon as the right and real represen- tative in this land of Corist'e Holy Catholic Church, whose birthday they were about to celebrate. The third point was the sanctity lof the home life, and the beauti- ful example set, hy him and his wife of what true married life really was. The fourth point was liii devoted and sincere obedience to duty. Differ as one humbly might with some of his schemes and proposals, one could not doubt that they were formulated in sincerity and troth. And, lastly, he had taught them how a true Christian could suffer and die. No one could follow the accounts of the past few weeks without noting the patient and peaceful resignation of the sufferer, and feeling that, in the words of his favourite hymn, he could " teach his brethren and Inspire, to suffer and to die." Special hymns were chosen and sung. SANKEY BRIDGES WESLEYAN CHAPEL. At the annivirreary services held in connection with the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Chapel at the Parr Gall on Sunday, the Dead March in " Saul " was played. At the evening service the prercher, the Rev. John G. Bin. net, connenianal evangelist, said he should like to pay his humbie tribute of pr lige to the memory of him who up to five o'clock on Thursday morning was the greatest living statesman. To-day, be observed, the whole na- tion was mourning tho death of Gladstone, statesman, scholar, and saint. Mr. Gladstone had seemed to belong to them all, especially since the old veteran's retirement —four years ago from the active warfare of life.—What was the source ft this universal Sorrow? Was it his statemanship ? Yes, in part ; for on the authority of the present Prime Minister " the most distinguished political name of this century has been withdrawn from the roll of E men." Was it his scholarship ? Yes, in part ; for he was one of the most brilliant of his day. But those two things combined would not of them- selves have won for him this universal sorrow. If he put the question to them," What think ye of his state- manship ?" they would be divided, or " What think ye of his scholarship ?" they would he still divided. But if he put the question " What think ye of his life 1" then he touched a chord which bound them together in tha common band of sorrow. For they were all agreed that his long and illu-trious life was one of the purest, most blameless and unselfish which had been lived during the century. They were told that his favourite hymn was the one So dear to them all, " Rock of Ages." That rock never failed him. It supported him in every arduous task of his life.and comforted him in every sorrow and trial. Gladstone was gone ! Earth was the poorer and heaven the richer. But the influence of Gladstone had not gone. Death could not rob them of that. It would abide for generations to come. Might they each catch up the inspiration of his life, and in some degree at least repeat it in their own.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <p>To NrOTHERS.—Mn. Wilmslow's Soothing Syrup bas,been used over fifty years by million; of mothers for their chil- dren while teething with perfect success. It will relieve the poor sufferer immediately. It is pleasant to taste ; it pro- duces natural quiet sleep, by relieving the child from pain and the little cherub awakes "as bright as a button," Of all chemists Is. ltd. per bottle.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>HILL CLIFFE Bap HSI' _CHAPEL,.</head>
        <p>HILL CLIFFE Bap HSI' _CHAPEL,.</p>
        <p>_ The Rev. J. Samuel Hughes, took as his theme "The fallen cel sr: howl, fir trea,for the cedar is fallea," Zech, xi, 2. Hi said: The teatime watched with strained eves and waited in awe-striken suspense by the bedside of the dying statesmen, and prayed for his happy release, yet when he peacefully passed away and silently crossed the bar the sense of relief gave place to one of astonish- ment,that he the toretnost Bliton or his day,the greatest character in contemporary history,the man of marvellous vitality, massive intellect and world-soreai influence, the hero of a thousand bloodless battles the world's greatest citizen—that he should have fallen a victim to insidious disease and cruel death. " A cedar " has falen, and there is reason for the nation.' tears and the nations lamentations. The great life that has just closed was a poem of wonderful symmetry, a song of charming sweetness, a battle cry in the warfare . against all wrong. Oppression, inhumanity and un- righteousness. The nation has lost her noblest son 'and the world.a true friend. The prowr attitude in the presence of sueh a bereavement as Chet we mourn to-day is one of reverence and submission of th.mkfulness that a life so pure and useful was eranted to us so long, of admiration for its shining qualities sad brilliant deeds, and of charity for any failings, which only brought so great and illustrious a life nearer our own, and made us feel that he was in reality one of us. In endeavouring to measure the personality, un- derstand the character' and contemplate the influence of Mr. Gladstone we fled he stands, like a towering cedar in the forest, preeminent from many points of view. Ile was pie-eminent, as a man of genius. Who can doubt it, with his splendid intellect, his expansive memory, his unrivalled eleqeence, his powerful mind could grasp any problem froth that of the most trivial agricultural question to the most momentous matters of the present or future life. Be was a mister of finance and a doctor of divinity, the range of his knowledge was marvellous, and his elecittence incomparable. The only man who could approach him in power of utter- ance was John Bright, but John Bright, when I heard hies, was cold, deliberate almost to painfulness, and had a total lack of fire, passion and gesture, although solid, stately, convincing, and beautiful in his use of pure Saxon. Mr. Gla4stone's eloquence on the other, hand was like a torrent ; there Wes the dash, and the roar, ' and the irresistibility, which by force of voice and ges- ture, nogent reasoning and terrible earnestness, carried you off your feet and compelled you to think with him. But what are we naturally most interested in is his re lationship to the free churches of our land. It would appear et first difficelt to understand how Mr. Gladstone in his lofty ',lsidore and with his pronounced High Church views could have captured the Nonconformists so completely as he did, and found amongst them hie most ardent, devoted, and enthusiastic admirers. To those who did not know the principles by which the Nonconformists are actuated, who do not weigh their motives, it would seem quite a paradox to 'find them trusting a public man so far removed from them in his religious convictions and acts. The Nonconformists looked upon Mr. Gladstone more as a God-fearing Christianlthan an Anglican Churchman, and they found in him evidence that he was attempting at least to apply the principles of the New Testament to national politics, and, further, they discovered that. It is for these I reasons, as well as that they found him always sincere, earnest, conscientious, approachable and respec- ted their appeals—that the great body of Nonconformists recognized in him a leader who was to be trusted, and their experience taueht them to love him with a passion of devoden rarely found between people and leader. As Neneonformists we owe him much. He was the fleet I great statesman candidly to acknowledge our existence as a factor for geed and a fotce to be considered in the councils of the nation. He understood better than any I other statesman ever had done our position and our principles, and he did more than way other to sooth the I smart of our wrongs and removeour disabiltties. Under him a Nonconformist became for the first time in our , country's history a Minister of the Crown and a member of the Cabinet—and that minister's name will ever shine illustrious on the roll of British statesmen,viz.John Bright. But whatever we think of his doctrinal beliefs, no one will for a moment deny that he was a Churchman of the hi/hest type,his love of God,his faith in Christ, his pas- sion for the Holy Catholic Church, his reverence and Ws devotedness were so real,sn intense,so beautiful, What- ever else men may forgot about him they will never for- get that he was a Christian. He wove his Christianity into everything he did,and he will be known by posterity as the Christian statesman. RaRLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>At one or twe of the places of *worship In Etrlestown reference was made on Sdnday last to the lamented death of Mr.Gladatone. At the Congregational Church, on Sunday evening Mr. Gladstone's favourite hymns were sung, and at the close of the service the organist, Mr. A. Knight, played the Dead March in "Saul," the congregation the while standing. The Rev. James All- att, who chose for his text, I. Cor. iv. 2. "It is re- quired in Stewards that a man be found faithful," preached a sermon, which in itself contained no per- sonal references, but whinh evidently outlined the ideal that the great statesman 111(1 sat before him, and to which through life he was loyal. At the closi Mr. All- att said there was awls in Mr. Gladstone that was re- markable beyond the 80 rears of labour (devoted to the service of his country. He was wonderful in the Wide range of his studies, and the variety of his interests; for his skill in mastering the details of complex querions and for the concentrati ,n of all the powers of its mind upon any one point until it was seen with micrascoPin clearness. There were occasions in which he was sur- passed by the oratory of Mr. Bright, hut taking into account the whole range of subjects on which it was ex- pended his eloquence was unequalled in our time. His Budget speeches havered even a I mass of figures and statistics, with a fascination and a charm. But great as Mr. Gladstone admittedly was in his intellectual gifts, and in his labOrioei cultiva- tion of them,so that even they should always be at their best, he would never have accomplished what he did apArt from the moral force that lay behind them. His career was long and his work in the House of Commons ar4uous and exacting, hut from first to last he rever- enced his conscience as his King. And it could be 'said of him with literal exactness that " he nothing common did or mean, upon that memorable scene." In the par' I able of the talents, We read that he that received the five talents " straightway went and traded with them." It was "straightway " with Mr. Gladstone. He began curly and continued to the end to devote his talents to the highest purposes. Lord Salisbury's words were as just as they were generous " men reuognised in him— men who did not share his oninions,a man guided in all the steps he took and in all the efforts that he Rivie, by a high moral idnal,and his leaves behind him the memory of a great Christian S'atesman." Whenever men or 1 nations are found faithful as stewards, ther achieve glory and honour and immortality. Men of all parties— Christian and non-Christian states—agree that that great ' distinction Mr. Gladstone has won. He has left a brilliant example. tie haa left a moral influence that will make for righteousness through coming years. HA was great in the eves of men and nations, but above all he was, through his steadfast faith " great in the sight of the Lord." At the Earlestown Wesleyan Chapel. the pulpit was draped in black, and the" Deal Mirth" was played by the organist, Miss Greenall. In the evening the Rev. P. R. Passmore referred in sympathetic terms to the death of the great Statesman. REFERENCE AT NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS. When the Court opened on Thursday. the Chairman (Captain Withington) said it was only fitting and proper that they shoald exrress their sympathy at the loss the "antrY generally had sustained by the death of Mr. Gladstone, who was to be buried on Saturday. They expressed their sympathy as a bench of magistrates. theMbrarßogilaeiyd, No fe wS tt.oHn eml eine sh,t ocnwhme ha thlef dof i thisi c n etoi colleagues at being associated with the most stirring partion of Mr. Glad- satpotnlye'ssaliid by fe.Altlhte Chairman, and they liked to be asso- ciated they thought had been fittingly and elated with the great volume of sympathy which was going out to Mrs. alldenne in her bereavement. RUNCORN PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>At the Parish Church, Runcorn, on Sunday, spneia sermons were preached in aid of the CheKter Diaresan• Finance Association, to good congrec.r,ntions. At the morning service the vicar (the Rey. Canon Wood), al- luded in fitting terms to the great loss the natieo ha d sustained through the death of that great statesman Mr. Will lan Ewart Gladstone. All the eyes of the world had been fired upon that death bed,as was shown by the touching sympathetic messages that had ficwed in front Russia, America, Italy, Gormany, China, and all parts of the known world, when the sad news became known. It would be art impertinence on his part to attempt to skotch the life and character of 90 eminent a man ; who haa entered into all the channels of knowledge given to mankind. But, whatever they mi.ht be with respect to agreement with some qoestions, none could but admire and acknowledge his PTP%t Christian principle. The " Dead March "in Saul was played on the organ by Mr. W.Johnson,the congregation standing with bowed heads , and reverent demeanour at the close of the service. ST. PAUL'S CHAPEL, RUNCORN. -</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. A. B. Malvern, 'preaching ac St. Paul's WeslPran Church, on Sunday morning referred to the late Mr. Gladstone as a mast a. cf assembhes " and as the Christian statesman who had gone to his rest. He was a man who, because he was a Christ!an.had thrilled the world more by his death than .by his lire. He had been possessed of a splendid constitution, a vast intel- lencmt,lnaniand with ittrhuloythsepriraitrueaalt m souln.. had sometimes Tie was a man who, in c hut also litre them had !often succeeded. On the recur- rection morn, the wornout frame would nut on more than sprinetide and the might,- h-ain, frozen by death's winter, would ba renewed in life and become once more a fitting vehicle for William Ewart Glad- stone's vaster soul—vaster because he had entered into fellowship with eternity.—At.the evening service at St. Paurs,the Rev. C. W. L,. Christien prefaced his sermon with a reference to the deceased statesman. He said that now the whole nation gathered around the mortal remains of one of her most prominent citizens. But it mattered not whether the servant of God was called from the castle or the cottage, each child of the Great Father passed to the same home of light and blessed- ness. And on such an occasion the mind of the preacher naturally turned towards the future home of the good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WIDNES.</head>
        <p>WIDNES.</p>
        <p>E In the canna of his sermon at St. Paul's Church, Widnes on Sunday morning, the Rev. 1. Adams reterred to the great loss sustained hy the nation in the death of one so aptly described as the greatest statesman of the greatest deliberative assembly in the world. Whatever their politics might be, all must bear record that Mr. Gladstone was a man of gigantic intellect, whi had riven is life to the servica of his country and his G3d. Mr. Adams pathetically appealed for the sympathy and prayers of the worshippers wino the beresvedwid ow in Ilawardea Castle. At the conclusion of the service, the Dead March waa impressively rendered by the organist, Mr.. W. Cooke.</p>
        <p>At the Victo—ria-roid Wesleyan Church, Widnes, on Sunday morning, the Rev. H. Snces during his die. owns feelingly referred to the late Mr. Gladstone's characteriatice, and touched upon his Christian-lilt 9 spirit. Mr. W. Stanley phayed the Dead March at the conclusion. References were also made at the various places of vorship iu the borough. Widnes Church Council have decided to hold a funeral Service on the frame day as the funeral of Mr. Gladstone, at which the Rev. Henry Scanes, president of the Coun. cil, will deliver an addr ss. In the event of the Mayor convening. a town's service to be held at S. Paul's Church, the members of the Free Church will join in that st:rvice instead of holding a separate one.</p>
