      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>IMPERIAL PI ItLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PI ItLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>ROUSH OP LORDS.—:/ZoNney.</p>
        <p>In the Haase of Lords on Monday the Royal Assent Wale) given to Miry-eve cubic and private Provisional Order Bills.—Lord SALISBURT ancoincel that he had received the ' I assent of her Majesty to the address of their lordship! that st I public funeral be given to Mr. G oilstone and a memoriah erected to his memory.—The Had of lIARDWICKE. In' intnrint the seconi reading of the Aliens Bill, explained that It pro- aided that an insipsctor appointed by the Board of Trade might boari Any vessel arriving with immigrant passengers at any regulated port and prohibit the landing of any alien who,in his opinion, was either an idiot insane, a pauper, a nerson likely tri become a publics charge or a person suffering from any dangeross contagious or infectious diseace. -Lo.d DUDLST Pptlka In fitvilne of the MIL Lord FIERSORCLL urged the necessity of an Ahaustive inquiry. Lor t MoNSSWELL I and Viscount CLUDICIP spoke In opposition. ant the Varqnis of SLLISBORT said he did not thistic that guilt !lent r•asort hat been given why the Governmeist should act in hostility to ' Bill. The Bill was read a seconit time by a majority of 62. A motion by Lori ciILISBURy that the House should attend , the fuceral of Mr. Gineistoce was ogreet to. The Merchant Shipping (Liablity of Sh'pownrrs) Bill. the Licensing (Die- qualification of Justices) Bill, and the Lunacy Bill passed through committee, and wore reported to the House.</p>
        <p>ROUSH OF CO IMONS.—IIONDAY. In the House of Commons on Monday. Hr. LLOYD GEORGS moved that the Rest an t West Wales Rai tway Bill be re- committed to the former committee. which had rejected it on financial grounds. He was assured by the promoters that if the bill were recommended they would be able in a few weeks' time to eiym satisfactory evidence on the financia/ part of the rcneme Mr. WILLILM .ToNEi seconded the motion, lea on a division it was rejected.--Hr. RITCHIE, in answer to Mr. Charles M'A.rthur. said he was unabln at pre- sent to say when the Merchant Thinning Mercantile Marine Fund) Rill as amended hy the Standing Committee, would be considered. 'dr. CHIPLIV informed Mr. Compton Rickett that ha hoped to introduce a bit: shortly dealing with the adulteration of food *ad drugs as mentions iin the Queen's Speech. In reply to Mr. lionised, Hr. BRaDRICE sad the peovlsion of tents and Sitars for the troops in the Son tan rested entirely with the Haptian Gove-nment. The arrangements had been left to the discretion of the Sinter. No complaints had realised the War Office He had no reason to suppose that a number of deaths from dysentery had occurred that might have been nreeented, but he would have Inv( V made. ThoDETCH informed Hr. Lloyd that the question of tak- ing s..ans to reatvent in the future the sale of honery colonel.. des of Volunteer regiment/ was under the consideration of the War Office. Replvln4 to Sir Janes Fergutson. Mr. Cunt -ix said the statement in reference to the acquiescence of the Govar .roent in the passport regulations imposed by Russia at Ta-Lien-Wan was entirely without foundation. The Russian °hero 4' Affalres at Pekin has blen told to instruct the Oonculs to withdraw the re nalstion Replying to Hr. Lahouchere tb. Cnanormon of the EXCHEQ TER said!! the House desirel that the Poreign Office vote should be taken on Pada.. the 10th of June, the government would fix It for thet day.</p>
        <p>HOUSE OF COMAIIII3.—TUESDAY. In theHonse of Commons on Toes lay Mr. On-size/(• in answer to Sir C Dilke, said he wag afraid he would not be I able to make a statement with r.rgard to the negotiation* with France as to West Africa until the negotiations were concluded —Mr. PROVLND asked whether the p,rt of Wel- Hal-Wel had ben leased for military purposes atone, as stated by Mr Balfour, or on the same terms as Russia had leased Port Arthur and Ta-lien-Wan, as ste.e.l by Lord -alishury.—Mr. CURZDN replied that both statemenrs were correct and there did not apnear to be any contradi •tim bs- tweenthem.—Asked what business wenn be taken after Whit-wwk Sir A. KICKS-REACH Paid that assuming the Committee stage of the Irish Local Government Bill to be finished b fore the erbournment. it was prnoosel to take the second reading of the Fin snce Billas the first order on Monday June 6, and he Reserve Forces Bill as the second order. if course if the Irish bill was not. finished before the edjounament it would be the first business on June 6. On the 7th of Jule the Indian Secretary would , Introduce a Loan Bill to provide for the red.mption r)f- certain dr Dentures, and proposed to make his alarm( al statement in moving the resolution necessary to set up that bill. The debate on the financial relations of Great Britain and Ireland would not ba taken before June 10, ant the 'I Sport stage of the Irish Local Government Bill might ; follow on June 21. The floosie •fterwards went Into Committee on tries Local Government Ireland) Bill, and shortly after three o'clock had passed all the original °leases of the bill. The new clauses standing In the name of the Chief Seuretary were next proceeded with, and agreed to. Mr. G. BaLYO a then appealed to members who had pntdown new clauses to let Jima stand over to the report stsge. The appeal was acquiesced in, and Sir M. 11, BRA.CII then said the course pro- pc sect by the G werntnfmt would permit of their giving due Heonsideration to the postsion.d clauses before the report stage kt nine o'clock the Committee entered upon the cora- -1 sideratlon of the echedules to the Bill. and at twenty-five Minutes to twelve the Bill, as amended passed through Com- mittee, and was ordered to be reported to the House. HOUSE OP COMNONS.—WEDNESDAY. The HonSe adjourned early on Wednesday and will not meet again till June 6.</p>
