      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>MAY 27th, 1898.</head>
        <p>MAY 27th, 1898.</p>
        <p>cjje eyaminer " Annts</p>
        <p>ON LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE COIINTT NEWBPA.PKELP. - •</p>
        <p>compri.e, Tint Warrington and Mid-Chealiire Examiner, pu Ilahed every Saturday morning. THE Earlestown and Newton .Examiner, published every Friday afternoon. THE St. Helens Examiner, published every Fridal A_ afternoon. I'HE Runcorn Examiner, published every &amp;tur.. day morning, and rrilE Wsdnes Axaminer, published every uturdey • morning.</p>
        <p>BEFORE adjourning for th Whitsuntide. Whitsuntide holidays,the House of Commons had not conclu. any business which the public can regard as par. ticularly interesting or important. Indeed, the session hitherto has been chiefly remarkable foi its extreme dulness. Ever since Easter the House has been occupied in considering the Irish Local Government Bill in Committee, and apart) f</p>
        <p>one or two points, the discussion of the Bill hi. been dreariness itself so far as the English publier are concerned. The committee stage has beet,' virtually completed, however, many points bein, reserved for the Report stage, and her Majesty* "faithful Commons" have taken a brief respit from their labours. There is, perhaps, a touch nef heroism about the legislator who sticks to his posSi while the deadly dull ork of committee is being' transacted in a cham r charged with an atme' phere of something like eighty degrees in tla shade. The misery of the situation is not my' terially relieved by the knowledge that the cool, and breezy terrace is close at hand, and that the) parks in their mantles of greenery, picked out b 1 blazes of blossom, are looking their very best. The' second spring festival of St. Lubbock</p>
        <p>falls well within the month of May; but this , it is shoved forward to the very end, the nett- consequence being that the holiday-maker 110 more chance of enjoying settled weather. Easter and Whitsuntide holidays are oases in wilderness of the stress and clatter of labour.' are the pivots alike of the pleasures of hope of the pleasures of memory, and a dismal Easter Monday or Whit Sunday is remembered with thc same feeling ot aversion which attaches, say, to excruciating toothache. This year we have st holiday times fared well. Good Friday and Easteri were more than tolerable, and a late Whitsuntide, eventhough there may be a passing shower or two, seems to guarantee a freedom from those aspen' ties which disconcert the holiday maker, and it the truth may be told, inflicts enormous loss 00 those classes of the community who make thea' harvest while other people give themselves up 0 a well-earned idleness and enjoyment. South of the Tweed the majority of people have probabli no serious fault to find with the system by which the great public holidays are regulated. It -</p>
        <p>sound strange to the ear of the Sassenach, ht ever, that north of the English frontier there I no Easter, Whitsuntide, or Christmas holida,</p>
        <p>The banks, of course, shut their doors for all some part of these, days ; but the people go abOI their avocatious.as usual. The popular holidi are mainly fixed by local bodies. Thus, it may</p>
        <p>a high public holiday in Aberdeen, while Dundee or Edinburgh, there is not the flutter of a holiday gown to be seen among the masses. Tht) system may have its advantages, but it is nc without its inconveniences. In England the holiday season is over within a specified There is complete interchangeability, and .3. body knows that everybody else who can afford )l is out and about, or otherwise on pleasure beet</p>
        <p>The Scotch take simultaneous action in the</p>
        <p>of holiday-making only once a year, and that __</p>
        <p>on New Year's Day, when the convulsion merriment spreads from "Grey Galloway" John O'Groats. Even the moveable fast-days' falling into disrepute, because the Scot began fall into a degenerate habit on those days attending much more to the pleasures of world rather than to the observance of the girlf. The Scot, however, has the advantage of able to fix his public holiday at midsummer, if b' will. He is free from the injunctions of Lubbock, and so long as his freedom is to mind it is not the business of anyl to interfere. At this time of day English would not take kindly to th</p>
        <p>bio ,boai the fixture.° corcov,i do') tbel</p>
        <p>Scotch system. An attempt to displace Whitsuntide holiday from the list of</p>
        <p>would be hotly resented by those to whom torn has become a second nature. Be the weal good or bad they will have the Whitsun festival at its appointed time. They look f</p>
        <p>to it months ha advance. In the sometimes</p>
        <p>and drab monotony of their every-day lives - are infinitely consoled as they look ahead to tP,, imaginary trightness of the Whitsuntide carlii,lo val. If some of the brightness fades with dawn of the auspicious day they will, no clOOl3O be disappointed; but they would probably is°l3, rather put up with the inconvenience of unseat° if) able weather than sacrifice the opportunity tho well within their grasp. An inclement Wh1t°11:10 tide hits the railway companies hard. It bitsz;ite purveyors of all outdoor rocreation,and the juice variety of caterors,high orhumble,for the ple*fiAoe of the multitude. Let us trust that it will 10 at least on Monday next. ululso</p>
        <p>SIM°. 00</p>
        <p>THE VERY LATEST DESIGNS and COLOt In Millinery. Dress Fables. Bilks, Blouses, Umbrellas am, &amp;.e —W.T.WILLtams, Bankey4tree9 Legh-street, Warrington.</p>
