      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>XEWTON URfs %_'NT DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>XEWTON URfs %_'NT DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>sPeeial meeting of the Newton in Makerfield Urban I,lo'Strict Council was held on Thursday afternoon. Mr. a presiding. There were also present : Messrs. T. Tinand, Dr. Patterson, J, ff.l.-.e J. Sitnpson, W. Beatty, ;v. Gilmore, W. W. Shirley (clerk), R. Brierley (Sur- and A. Eowes (assistant surveyor).—The Coals- 113lean said that during th. last few weeks their eyes had been turned towards Hawarden, viewing the dying t‘litateernals, the Right Hon. W. E. Gladstone who had Oated from this world. He would not enter into his it was the common, knowledge and talk of all a,Pee. His family life was one of peaceful felicity., ,44 his public life was brilliant and would • atl example now, and for generations to Be thm moved the following resolution :- 44• hat this Council in special meeting assembled, de- tilt" tO express their profound sense of the loss which vsAountry has sustained by the death of the Right Hen Cton"3 E wart Gladstone, in conveying to Mrs. Glad- ,tlo /led her family, their heartfelt sympathy and con- in• L. their bereavement. The Council trusts that 4,1"/ will feel their sorrow softened by the remember- after a long life full of great achievements for 41.1.1t,nd of mankind, the deceased statesman leaves e„ftLllti him an imperishable name, and the and all civilized nations =earn with in their bereavement. The Council 2aer,hipe thel his life as a Christian will remain tirnumant for religion to present and future genera- (llll9;"--Mr. Ormand seconded and referred Co Mr. to adatane's estimate of the nobility of George Washing- WasHe throught that that corinsate was what the world hanged thinking of Mr. Gladstone. Mr. Gladstone had mankind's ideas of political greatness, and ‘,9l;?:wad by his eansistent life that goodness could wed 11 greatness.-- Mr. Gilmore supported the resolution, alludeel to the scene in the House of Commons, on • t eception of the news of the great statesmans death, " also referred to the last dying moments of Nee states- at Hawarden.—TOo vote was passed in silence.</p>
        <p>4p. 1101 X HOLIDAY THE PADDLE SALOON -------</p>
        <p>--- FLYING FALCON It, Wig gall from the PRtfirCE'S LA.NDING-fiTAGE, "7.IIRPOOL (weather and eller circumstances permitting) !dONDAY NrErr at 1045 a.m. (55 minutes later Alta from New Brighton) for SOUTHPORT. tOing.from St Liftoff Pier at Spin, glviag_pisuttigers</p>
        <p>out six hours ashore. leeturii Pere,"2B. Xbefresbesertts on board ot moderate charges.</p>
