      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <p>The manufacture of criminals seems to be very aucousf ally carried on by amateur magistrates in 8913a0 parts of the country. An inattentive con- -B.lderation of a prisoner's plea—not infrequently the case by any means—very probably leads to a liuscarriage of justice and this fact ought to be rrle in mind. Minor offences are often sum- manly with in a busy court by unpaid ad- Itunistrators whose time is an important factor, 4,nd that such scant consideration of a case can go .ar towards increasing crime and actually adding to the ranks of criminals is shown by the following St°rY told to a c:ergyman who has recently been eon. ducting a mission in one of Her Majesty's I:ftlsorls, The prisoner's story to his interlo- Zitor, in his own words, is as follows I have been locked up three times. As long as I was in work I was never in trouble, then I got °lit of work, and everybody preferred putting on Zcaunger men. I was hungry—did you ever kla°w, sir, what it was to be right down unogry ? Well, I begged at a door. I knew it !as wrong, but I begged and I got locked up for `". When I came out, I said, "I won't do that aga. in-" So I got some leather and cut out a Pair of bootlaces, and tried to sell them. Then I got run in again for hawking without a license. WA hen I came out I said, "I wont do that again." 11(1 I walked and I walked, and could get no TWQ,rlc, nor find food either. I was dead beat, and lay down under a hedge—then I got locked up for sleeping out of doors.'</p>
        <p>--- There was not a little sarcastic comment aroused last week by the ineffective prosecution of a man for shocking cruelty to a rat. Students of the tlailY press will recollect that the offender suc- ceeded in shuffling out of the consequence of his barbarity by the advocacy of the legal anomaly that because the poor rat did net come under,the moraestic animals class, the torturing of it was not In offence punishable by law. What a preposter- "s Act! That a body of legislators should create a law imposin,.; the direst penalties on per- guilty of cruelty to a "domestic animal," and at the same time condoning the most re- volting treatment of beasts not in this category-, would almost pass comprehension were it not for the many other " red-tapeisms" brought t° light from time to time. Seeing the iniquity of this section of the Act,it is very pleasing to find that in the House of Commons so early as Mon- (laY,a hon. member,Mr. Luttrell, took the ques- tion up. He asked the Home Secretary if his at- tention had been drawn to the case here alluded to,and received the gratifying reply that the point • involved was one which he (Sir Matthew W. ?Adley) had already noted as a proper subject for ,egislation when opportunity occurred. Let us toLope,in the name of common humanity to dumb rniles, that this " opportunity " will not be so °..g in comity, as one instinctively fears. Pro- for the "punishment of people who will de- throrat°,lY torture an animal, and then creep not Such a loophole as at present exists, can- e made a day too soon.</p>
        <p>Something in the nature of an offensive and de- fensive alliance between Nonconformists has this Week been formed in London, i.e., the " Noncon- , formist Parliamentary Council." The object ot this organisation of forces is partially indicated by` the title, but for the benefit of our readers we may explain that primarily tho Council's aim is to Watch and protect in the legislature tize interests of Nonconformists, and promote effective legisla- tion for the removal of their grievances. The loss of the strength of unity has been felt by Nonconformists for some time, and organ- isation has at last been rendered absolutely neces- -4aryland has been carried into effect to strengthen the hands of the Free Churches and increase their ullinence upon the National Government, which was never less than at the present time. In the W°rrle of Mr. Lloyd George M.P., "Priesthood never had such a controlling voice in this country since the days of Archbishop Laud as now, and all the enthusiasm associated with the Fres Churches Is wanted for the purpose of fighting the great battle of religious freedom in this land." Though formed, the Council is as yet only in its elementary Btage, the meeting on Tuesday passing a resolution "Vowering a preliminary committee to prepare a Scheme for its constitution and working. Initial uifficulties are generally the hardest to overcome, and now that these have been surmounted and the Nonconformist Parliamentary Council constituted With such a sound basis and under the auspices of the most eminent Freechurchmon of tho day, action towards reform, and the suppression of aggressive sacerdotalism may be confidently `oeked for in the near future.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>1.„ We warmly congratulate Councillor James ,4vans on the brilliant success which has attended ilia candidature for the representation of White- Itoss Ward on the Warrington Town Council. though it might be said with some reason that he Stood little chan,e of defeat from the very begin- 41ntg, Councillor Evans and his supporters have !lot gone about their work trusting to any such nelief as this. Elections are at all times un- trustworthy affairs, and what a ward has done during the past is no guarantee that it will do the same in the present or future. In this instance t.he victorious aspirant for Town Council honours, ls not only the nominee of the strongest party in the ward, but he is a popular townsman of long standing in the district. His opponent also pos- -8888 es the latter claim to some extent,and with the solid phalanx of Warrington Conservatism thrust- ing llirnforward it was generally thought that he _,w°,llla Tun the issue pretty close. Mr. Bellian's defeat has been of that crushing nature character- istic of Whitecross Ward elections, and though it has not been openly fought as a party fight, the contest goes to show that Liberalism at that end of the town----aml as we believe in most other parts —la more than holding its own. The absolute sbortiveness of this attack on the Liberal citadel should convince our Tory friends once and for all „._that they have no earthly chance of success in w hiteeross so long as the other party enters the field with candidates of the calibre of Mr. trans.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>RUN riORN AND vir DNI ES.</head>
        <p>RUN riORN AND vir DNI ES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN HILL AND RUNCORN HEATH. The second report from the Select Committee on Gam- mons, published on Wednesday, contains the final word with reference to the Runcorn Hill dispute. The select Committee have considered the report made by the Rmrd of Agriculture,certifying the expediency of a pro- visional order f)r closing and regulating Runcorn Reath and the hill, and are of opinion that the same ought to be confirmed by Parliament without modifica- MOIL BRTHESDA SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY. _ . .</p>
        <p>The Bethesda Ocmgregational Sunday School,Runcorn, held tkeir anniversary on Sunday last, when the Rev. ELT. Spencer, M.A., B.Sc., of Wavertree preached two impressive sermons to large congregations. The scholars assisted by the choir rendered special music, to the accompaniment of the Runcorn Orchestral Society Band. The wh )le of tle meeting proved most successful and substantial collections were secured in aid of the school funds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>SEVERE WEATHER.</head>
        <p>SEVERE WEATHER.</p>
        <p>A severe thunderstorm pasaed over the district of Run- corn on Sunday evening last, accompanied by vivid flashes of lightning. At the Parish Church rain forced Its way through a defective part of the roof, whilst in Halton-road Chapel the rain forced its way in such quantities as to flood a great part of the staircase leading to the gallery. As the downpour of rain showed no signs of abating, a programme of music was given supplemen- tary to the service by the choir.—No damage of a serious nature is, however, reported.</p>
        <p>TRAP ACRIDENT. This morning (Friday), whilst driving in the vicinity of tho Railway Station, a horse and shandry belonging to W. Jolla Finch, farmer, Norton, met with a slight accident. The horse slipped and fell, one of the snatts getting broken, entangled the animal very mach, and at first it was thought that the piece of broken shaft had entered the body of the horse. On extricating the animal, however, it was found that no serious damage save the breaking of the shaft had been sustained. RUNCORN WESLEYAN CYCLE CLUB. O a fairly large muster of lady anda Wgenettdenmeesdnaz noohaefetleerrsbnalonging went tor a spirt to Helsby by way of to the Runcorn Wes- leyan Cycle Club, Ashton and Sutton-Weaver. On reaching their destine- the points of interest were visited and some ef the ntent- bore erat.loyed the time by a climb up the bine. The return journey was commenced about 7.30 and Runcorn reached at 8.45. BIWA 121/ HANDICAP . .</p>
        <p>On WednesdaY glietOe final in ih—e-lailliard handicap which bee been in progrees at the "George and a d Dragon Hotel," Egerton-street, trAi relayed off before compeny. There were about 78 entries in all,the game being 400 GM The sem;-duals reeulted :—W. Pealock, receives 260, beat W. Cooper, receives 200; P. guise, 00) beat J. Perkinson (90),—Final : P. Q nun beat W. Peacock. Seine of tho gatreee were close aad exciting especially the semi-final between Quinn and Parkinson ; the final however proved sernewhat tame. Toe priz se were First, a marble timepiece, valued at £2 103. ; : second, cue and case and a box of cigars, whittle the other two prizes were of 105 value. ACCIDENT AT War rose POINT. An accident of a distressing nature occurred at the works of Messrs.Caetner,Kellner and Co., Weston Point,by which a young man named Joseph Lunt, of Curzon-st., Runcorn, sustained serious injuries to his been. It ap- pears that Lunt was engaged at work upon tne founcia- tion of a building and was at the bottom of a deep cut- ting engaged with his work when a travelling crane eels- ' turbed some loose bricks and caused them to fall upon iefficting severe injuries. He was conveyed home and has been lying in a precarious condition; at preeent, hewever, it is pleasing to record he is progressing favourably. TERRIBLE ACCIDENT AT WIDNES. A shocking accident which almost resulted in the death of two men, occurred at Craig's Timber Works, Widnes.% ma Monday morning. It appears that the framework of the cask heading machinery, through some inexplicable cause, fisw off, and struck two young MB named Jabez kterrie.39, Frederick-street, and Ernest Caton,also a resident of Widnes. The latter sustained a broken arm, in addition to which the arm was almost torn from its socket. Dr. Hutchinsot was immediately summoned, and he, after treating the unfortunate man to the best of his skill, deemed it advisable to have him removed to the Royal Infirtnary, Liverpool. The other man had several of his ribi fractured, and he was con- veyed to the Accident Hospital, where Dr. Hutcuinson attended him. BETHESDA CONGREGATIONAL F.S.A. At the usual weekly meeting of this society on Sunday last a new feature was introduced, the children of the Sunday school rendering a part of the musical service by singing two of their anniversary pieces, which formed a very agreeable addition to the usual programme. Mr. Beck,who occupied the chair, expressed his gratification at being called upon to preside at a meeting winch combieed the two forms of Christian work in which he took most delight and which claimed his deepest sym- pathy. The address was by the Rev. H. T. Spencer, M. a., of IVavertree, who referred in very feeling terms to the loes the world had sustained by the death of Mr. Gladstone,basing his remarks on one of the great states- man's favourite expressions " Manhood." He gave a very inspiring address on some of the ways of acquiring true manhood. Mr. Spencer was listened to with great attention and his remarks called forth expressions of hearty approval. Miss Hultne,of Widnes, was the solo- ist,and gave with great taste " 0 loving Father," and "He shall feed his flock." The attenday.ce was good. A WIDNES BOY BIRCHED.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor and Alderman Sadler,Edward Farrell, 13 years of age, was chargeJ with stealing two pairs of children's boots,value 41., the property of Thomas Owen, sometime between the 11th and 15th inst. It appeared from the evidence, that the prisoner had been playing with the prosecutor's children all the week. Rose Owen saw the boots safe under the sofa in the kitchen on the 11th Inst., but she missed them on Sunday, he 15th inst. The prisoner pawned a pair of baby's boots at Harper's pawnshop in Ann-street,at nine o'clock on the 11t.h inst., he said they belonged to Mrs. Owen. He subseq tently pawned a pair of children's boots for is. 31. at Neil's pawnshop in Albert-road,then giving the name of Little- more. Both pairs of boots were those missed by the prosecutor.—The prisoner pleaded guilty, and said he wanted to buy a pair of trousers with the money that he got for selling the boots. —Prisoner's father stated that he had seven children, and the mother bad left them 'three years ago. There was no one to look after them but himself.—The Bench ordered the boy to receive six strokes with the birch. SAD DEATH ON A PIINLICAN'S WIER AT WIDNES. On Sunday morning Mary Taylor, 49 years of age, wife of James Taylor, of the Angel and Elephant Hotel, Appleton, was discovered lying dead upstairs. An in. quiry relative to the death was held on Monday after- noon, by Mr. S. Brighouse, in the Inn at Applcton,— James Taylor, hueband of the deceased, deposed that his wife retired about ten minutes past 11 o'clock on Saturday evening, together with the servant Kate MeCormick, with whom she slept. She WAS then per- fectly sober. About 8.30 on the following morning, lie found her lying on her left side with her head and shoulders on the landing, and the lower portion of her body on the stairs. Witnese inquired front her what she was doing there, but she made no reply. He put his hand oa her face and it was quite warm. He then called his two sons, who ran for the doctor, bat when he arrived he announced that life was extinct. Witness said his wife had been under Dr. Hutchinson's care for a long time, and she had been. of very intemperate habits for some years.—Dr. Hutchi son, who was sworn in the Scotch mode, deposed that he had attended deceased for years, chiefly for the effects of intemperance, but not since the 12th of last month. He was of opinion that death was due to failure of the heart's action, caused by the excessive use of alcohol. He had expected her death for some time, because she was unable to take sufficient food on her stomach to sustain life.—The Coroner, said in his opinion the only verdict to be returned, was that in accordance with the medical statement.—The jury, after due deliberation, returned a verdict accordingly.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. During the week the following births and deaths were registered :</p>
        <p>Births. Deaths.</p>
        <p>Warrington Registration District 27 21 The excess of births over deaths was 6. Birth Rate 22%3 per 1,000 of the population. The death-rate from all causes was equal to 17.3 per 1,000, against 173 the average of the previous four weeks, and against 235 the corresponding period of last year. The death. rate from the seven chief zvmotic diseases was equal to 33 per 1,000. against 9 6 the average rate of the previous four weeks, and against Nil the corresponding period of 1897. .The ages of mortality were- -0-1 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 5 8 0 1 7 0 21. The deaths included-3 from whooping cough, 1 from diarrbcea, 6 from lung diseases,l from injuries, 10 from all other causes. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Nil. Total number of patients in hospital, week ending 7th May-2. METEOROLOGr.—The mean temperature was 49 Fah., and therefore .1 a degree higher than the preceding week. The maximum temperature 63F. was recorded on the 3rd inst., and the minimum temperature 33 F. on the 2nd inst. The rainfall measured '33in. The general direction was S.E., the reading of the anemometer denoting 760 miles.</p>
        <p>JOHN H. GORNALL, Medical Officer a Health.</p>
        <p>Madame Meiasonier, woo died lately, has bequeathed to the Louvre the whole of the fine collections of draw. ings, sketches, and pictures by her late husband, the great French painter, from wheal she bad inherited theft.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>EX A MINER, FRII)A Y. MAY 27 1898.</head>
        <p>THE EX A MINER, FRII)A Y. MAY 27 1898.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND THE IRISH FAMINE ANNUAL MEETING OF THE 'RUNCORN FUND. RUGBY FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The antual meetir t! of the Runcorn Ruzbe Club wa,s held on Tuesday hiffht " Rilev's Cat.," Mr. P. Speakman. presiding over a full and enthusiastie meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>CONCERT AT TLib' PUBLIC HALL.</head>
        <p>CONCERT AT TLib' PUBLIC HALL.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the impromptu concert in aid of the distress in the West of Ireland came off at the Public Hall, Runcorn. In response to the appeal of the committee a number of local artistes had given their services gratis in aid of the cause, and a capital programme was prepared. Owing to the notice of the event being so short, however, there was but a meagre attendance,ancl the result of the venture has been a little disaupointing from a financial point of view. The following was the programme gone th roue b :—Part 1. Selection." Gems of Melody." Runcorn Volunteer band ; glee, " The Carnovale," Mr. Morris' glee party ; song, "Queen of the Earth," Mr. A. Shingler ; song, " Rory Harlin'," Miss Clara Taylor; cornet solo, "Rule Brittania" (en- cored) Mr. T. Knight; song, "Killarney " Miss Alice Taylor; song, " Terence's farewell to Kathleen," Mr. Dockery.—Part ll.—Selection, Runcorn Volunteer band; glee, "On the Banks of Allan Waters," Mr. Morris' glee party; song,"The Carnival," Miss Annie Johnson; song, "The Bugler," Mr. F. Shingler; flute solo, Mr. W. Craddock ; comic song, "That'll do," (encore " I cannot give the old seng "), Mr. Alf Grounds; finale, "God save the Queen." The effici- ent accompanist was Mr. W. Grounds. During the iuterval the Chairman of the Commit- tee (Mr. S. Taylor, C.O ) in a brief address intro- , duced the object of the fund, which was to assist in relieving the great distress of a people who were of the same flesh and family as themselves,. Ho would not take up their time in portraying the distress as he had to call upon the Rev. J. Ryan, a clergyman better acquainted with the actual distress, who would address them upon the subject. The Rev. Father Ryan, who was well received, raid he could not tell who was responsible for his name being placed 'upon the programme. He had come there with the intention of being a listener to one better able to describe the great distress which was being experienced in the affecte i parts of 'rebind. He felt the pain and humiliation of having io appeal on behelf of his fellow coantry- men,fer the menus of staying starvation and death from the homes of thousands of families in ttie west of Ireland. To accurately describe this distress would be a matter of imuessibility. He might in his imaeinatiou but it. wee beyond him or anyone else to overdraw the picture, Tee distress in Ire- land w e not, due to the ludo' rice of the people—tt was nut. due to any fault. of their own. It must. in- deed be a hard callous spirit that would scoff at the distress of its fellow creature. How many in that room would revolt at the idea of a cruel person ill- treat lug a starving dog in the streets—then how much more should they sympathise with their fel- low creatures. At length the speaker instanced the acuteness of the distress. Broad was an unknown luxury, nothing but Indian meal—and poor stuff at that—with a few discolored pota- toes was all that could be obtained and thousands considered themselves well off if they could but have that for once a day. His appealed to them as English people to do something for their suffering brothers and sisters in the sister isle. Irishmen were gratefel,large hearted, and loyal : they would never forget the many kindnesses England had ex- tended to them in their dietress,and he would point out that the kindness of England at this time was doing much to heal the breach that existed in that part between the two countries. If the people of England once realised the true state of the distress, be knew that practical sympathy would follow at once and something be accomplished. Already the names of Liverpool. Manchester and Glasgow were honoured by thousands of their fellow creatures in Ireland, and he hoped it would not be long before Runcorn and other towns would follow in their steps.</p>
        <p>Mr. Lightburn, said he had a pleasing duty to do in preposing a vote of thanks to the artistes for their kindness in coming forward so spontaneously. He could see that a half-filled room was not a grant encouragement to them, but still the concert might be the means of making the people more familiar with the distressing need in the West of Ireland, and when it was properly understood he felt sure that Runcorn would not be behind. (Hear, hear). Father Gore seconded the vote of thanks to the artistes, and also proposed that the chairman be accorded a like honNur. He would say that the people of Runcorn were responding most generously to the appeal for aid, and it the collections were re- ' ceived with the same kindness as had been accorded them so far, he was confident that a creditable sum would be sent from Runcorn. (Hear, hear). The votes of thanks were heartily accorded.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES. [BY A CORRESPONDENT]. The conglomeration of shops and private houses. places of worship, public buildings and public- houses that exists in Runcorn. is undoubtedly one of the chief causes why the little town has not the appearance of being busy, even when it is. There is scarcely a purely business street, or for the matter of that, a private street—in the sense of being restricted to private houses—throughout the whole of the town. It is throughout a mixture of shops, private houses, public-houses, public build- ings, and places of worship alternating with one another, True the English nation is a nation of shop keepers, but Runcorn has evidently more than a fair share of would-be shop-keepers, as can be easily instanced from a glance around at the numerous private houses that of late have been con- vex ted into places of business.</p>
        <p>Now, everyone in Runcorn gladly welcomes a new business or anything that can be looked upon as an incentive to trade. But it is a great pity that some effort is not made to confine a certain portion of the town into a business quarter, and develope that portion 'fret. Indeed, it is a matter that the Town Council might take a hand in, and try and encourage the development of such a centre, where the house-wife cou:d find all she re- quired in a certain area, and not be obliged to walk the whole of the town to accomplish the necessary week end purchases. Then again, who cares to see a brilliantly lighted shop in the middle of a row of nice bow-windowed houses in a quiet street?</p>
        <p>To the townspeople of Runcorn it must be very gratifying to learn (as they will from a perusal of the remarks of the Rev. Canon Wood) that they have in their midst a church so ancient in its his- torical recollections as the Parish Church has proved to be. I say proved te be, because, to me, it is suffi- ciently convincing when such people as Mr. John Littler and ldr.lmison (who I understand have been probing the question to its very depth), are satis- fied. Four churches have probably stood upon the site of the present building—the first of which was probably nothing better than a primitive shed— during a period of close upon 1,000 years; the exact figure is, however, not quite determinable, a fact that adds just a nice little " spice" to the whole thing. Once a year, however, future generations will be reminded of the fact when they look upon the "flag that carries the history of a thousand years"—in the shape of a picture of the Runcorn Parish Church—as it is borne through the streets at the bead ef the arm al procession ot the Runcorn Parish Church Sunday School.</p>
        <p>Of course a recreation ground and park is neces- sary for the inhabitants of Runcorn. But there are other things quite as necessary if not more so. The first and foremost neon is that of a public bath. To fully appreciate the blessings of a public bath one has only to have enjoyed the privilege of such an institution and then be suddenly deprived of it. During this summer the canals and other water- ways, as in past summers, will be requisitioned and made to serve the purposes of a public bath, or else the usual journey will be made to Widnes; both of which courses are, however, but illattrations of the desirability of meeting this pre,siog need.—Run. - corn can wait for recreation grounds; but there ts no reason why it should be made to do without a public bath. "Cleanliness is next to Godliness,"and that Runcornians would not be sorry to have some title to Godliness there can be no manner of doubt.</p>
      </div>
