      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>SEC RETA RY.S REPORT.</head>
        <p>SEC RETA RY.S REPORT.</p>
        <p>In submitting the report of the past &amp;towel, Mr. Eli. son, the fOsistant secretary, said it was with hrofourid regret t hit they recorded the removal of Mr. James Langiihaw, by death, from amongst them. Those who were associated with him whilst he regulated the affairs of the club were fully cognisant of the deep interest he took in everything pertaining to its welfare anti advance- ment. In the discharge of his official duties, Mr. Ltrig- shaw woe most untiring and painstaking, and his loss to them was a great one. Connected with the duo from Ito conception, he had in an official capacitv watched it rise from a fourth-ra'..e position to that of thr Champion of ClicsMre. His long experience and thorough grasp of the rules of the game eminently fitted him for the padtiou h4tt attained to on the Committee of the "Di or ther u I3nion, (Hear, hear). Turning to the affairs of the club, we have to report that the past season has been an unfortunate one from a sporting point of view, and comparatively speaking the club has deteriorated. The season opened favour- ably enough,but a change set in and after the defeat of the club by Broughton Rangers on November 27c1s a series of disasters betel the club and loss after loss was registered. They should not forgot the fact, however. that other clubs were somewhat strengthened by impor- t% ions from the larger and wealthier clubs. In the Northern Union Cup competition the players were to b- congratulated and commended upon their efforts, as the draw wag very much against them. Both snatches against, Ulverston and Brighouse were played away, but the match against 014 ham was a real " tit-bit," and al- though the home team were defeated they were not dis- graced.as those preient would recall the circumstances. In league games, 26, only 9 were won, 2 drawn, and 15 lost; 142 points against 186. In the Northern Union Cup Competition Runcorn scored 33 points to the 13 of their opponents. Of the total 36 matches played 14 were won, 19 lost, and 3 drawn. With a total score (at 31 g0a15,63 tries (242 points), to 34 goals, 52 tries '(224 points). A feature of the past season was the sports held in July, and the inauguration of the Inter-Works Competition, both of which aroused considerable interest in Ulf! nin , SO signal was its success that the committee feel justi- fied in making it an annual event. The r port of the doings of the " A. " team showed that nnti of 32 matches played, 12 had heen won. 16 oar, and 4 drawn ; the " A team scoring 24 goals, 54 tries (210 Poit;tN), against 28 goals, 61 tries (239 points).—On the motion of Mr. Speakman, the report wai unani- mously adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>TREASURER'S RV.PORT.</head>
        <p>TREASURER'S RV.PORT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles P)wnall (the treasurer), subm:tted the statement of accounts, allowing the work in Of the club. The receipts during the year were £B3l 173. 11d., and the expenditure £829 13.;. 21,, leaving a balanca of £2 43. 91. Subscriptions realised £75 2s. 01. ; sparts' profit. £l5 lie. 51. ; gate and stand receipts, £716 15. 111. the payments included, insuranc of piay, Lfri 154; ; lost time. £l3l 13. 61. ; hotel and travelling expenses, £B6 63. 11d. ; footballs, j.rseys, pants, boots, embrocation, and doctor's' fees, £llB 193. 33. ; salaries, £2l la;. The assets ware, cast, in treasurer's hands, £2 41. 91: amount of security refunded on behalf of the late sec- loan to Cricket Club, £25; retary (Mr.La.ngehaw), £25; value of stands, turnstilex and other club pronerts iPSI3I 20 nor cent depreciation deducted, £l6O 173 91.; total, £213 2s. 61.-1 n his report Mr.Powoall said the receipts trom players were 103. 6d. more than last year, hon. members subscriptions 17s. 63. lase, stand membns. £2 b. 61. less. The profit by the sports was £l5 lie. sd. and there were still on hand three prizes. (Flear, hear., The gate receipts showed an increase of £54 39.41. (Ap) plause.). The inter.worka competition proved to be moat- succeasful. the gross receipts were £62 104.81.; expendi- tin' tore £3O 75.11 d., thus benefiting club to the extent of £3o2e. 9 (Apnlauae.) He was pleased ta, announce that the committee intended continuinz then, and Mr. F. Norman (of the United Alkali Works) had promised them a handsome subscription towards the competition, and he had no doubt that when replies were received from other works they would be satisfactory ones. Amongst other items he explained that the biil for print- ing and stationer, was about £l5 more than last year owing to the fact that some of tip! 0 .ts,andine accounts of previous years had been met. They had this year paid £73 off at the bank, and he thought that there was omy about £23 outstanding for tradesmen's hill. Thus briefly summed up they were aheut £lOO better off on the working of the club than they were this time last year. (Hear. hear and applause). Mr. Speakman moved the adoption Of the report, and complimented the club on the successful fleoneisi year; be hoped they would still go on and prAaper.—Mr.Light- burn seconded and the motion was carried unanimously. - -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>TOTES OF THANKS.</head>
        <p>TOTES OF THANKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Speakman, in proposing a vote of thanks to the retiring committee and the players for their past ser- vices,said their thanks was especially due to the com- mittee for the way in which they had conducted the finances of the club as shown by the successful balance sheet they had just heard read. Though the players had not been so successful as in past years be was still glad to see se much goad feeling and talent left in Runcorn. (Hear, hear.) Mr. W. Carrel responded. If they had been anything this past year,it was a happy committes. The favour- able balance sheet was the result of very careful work. In acknowledging the vote of thanks ha wished to also thank personally those members of the committee who had so ably done their work. A vote of thanks was also accorded to the Works Com- petition Committee,on the motion of Mr.Carrol,seconded by Mr. Jones, who added if they only had a few more gentlemen like Mr. Moore and Mr. Wainwright they would do far better. He hoped others would gather round them and aid in getting the club out of debt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The election oi-oficers was then" proceeded with. There being only one nomination for the presidency, Mr. Lightburn formally proposed that Mr.P.Speakman be re- elected president for the ensuing season. Mr. Speakman was accordingly unanimously elected.</p>
        <p>. _ In acknowledging the honour done to him Mr. Speak- man expressed his pleasure at being once more elected president of the Runcorn Football Club. It was gratify igg to the members. who knew how low down on the scale the finances had been to note the improyement,and- he saw no reason why a special effort Rhould not bs made this season to for ever rid them of this encumb- rance. So far as the treasar.er's and secretary's reports went they were highly satisfactory. There had never i been a better feeling n the club than the feeling, shown at the end of the lest season. During the season they m i had a tajtvo a great loss n the death of their respected secretery, Mr. Langshaw. Now, more particularly, he wished to refer to the great loss sustained by the widow and child left to mourn a husband and a father, and he proposed that a vote of sympathy and condolence with Mrs. Langshaw in her deep inris be Rent from them, the annual meeting, es a token of the great respect they en- tertained for Mr. Langshaw.—Mr. Ellison seconded the vote of condolence, which WAN unahimnugly accorded.</p>
        <p>SECRETARY AND TREASI7RER For the first post there NVPre, two nominations:Messrs.J. Ellison and Alf Riley. Afr Alf Riley having' withdrawn, on the motion of Mr. Speakman seconded by Mr. Light. turn, Mr. J. Ellison was unanimously elected Mr. rownall was re-elected treasurer.</p>
        <p>TEM ASSISTANT SECIIETART. Out Of Sir nominations only two f•andidates stosd for election, Messrs. A. Abel and Rrown. Tbo votinz was as follows: Abel 43, Brown 18.—Mr. A. Abel was de- clared elected.</p>
        <p>TEAM OFFICERS. There were three candidatea for the poet of captain to the first team : Messrs. Butterworth, Fanlkner, and froughton. The resnit of the voting WRS Boughton, 22 ; Butterworth, 21 ; Faulkner, 19. Mr. S. Houghton was declared elected. For the sith-cantaincy the voting resulted : Mr. JAITINI Butterworth, 39; Mr. J. Faulkner, H. Mr. Butterworth being elected.—For the captaincy and sub-captaincy of the second team: Meesrs. Tavlor, VVarder,and Wilkinson were nominated. The voting. for the gantainCy was Mr. Warder.4o;Mr.Taylor,l2;and Mr. T. Wilkinson, 7. Mr. T. Warder heinq elected.—The voting for the sub-captaincy resulted: Mr. Alfred Taylor, 34; Mr. wiltiameon, 25.—The auditors Messrs. Edward Lunt, and T. C. Cooper ;were unanimously re-elected. THE COMMITTEE. Sixteen candidates stood for election on the commit- tee, the rinilint! being am fobowß :—G. linhineon, 55 Carroll. 54; P.Deakin. 52 ; G. Mac'rie, 52; W. Jones. 51; S. Abrams, 50 ; G. Wainwricht, 47 ; 11. Cawler, 42; A. Lightburn, 35; J. Cragg, 32: J Freser.3l; G.Moores, 27; R. Garnett, 20; D. Masser. 16; F.Hancook, 14; &amp;Sefton. 14. As there was only 12 seats the president declared the first twelve duly elected as the committee for the ensuing year. ------- -</p>
        <p>TUN ANNUAL sPORTa. It was decided to again hold sports in connection with the Football Club. and general satisfaction was ex- pressed with the rmsillt of the kat sports.</p>
        <p>PRACTICAL SYMPATHY.</p>
        <p>The President (Mr. Speak Tan) said while he had been ' sitting there there was a A9bret which WRS engaging his attention and that we with regard to showing a little practical sympathy with the widow of their late secre- tary Mr. Langshaw. It had always been a practice with the club, and it was a laudable practice, that in the case of death, those who were left behind rhould be cared for. If they (the Runcorn FM.) took the initiative in the matter he felt sure other dohs would respond. He did not want to make Mrs. Langshaw feel poor, hut he i thought that a little help n her need woull be a tangible token of the sympathy they all so freely expressed. He had pleasure in proposing that the Committee of the Runcorn Rugby Football Club be allowed to devote a sum as they think fi', to head the subscription list. (Rear, bear and nipleuse).--Mr. R. Garnett seconded the proposition, which was heartily agreed to. ' TUN QUERN's maximum In honour of the Queen's birthday the meeting joined in singing "God save the Queen," after which they dis- persed. _</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above was held at Dutton Work- house, subsequent to the meeting of the Board of Guar- dians on Tuesday- Mr.J. Koowles,J.P. presided ,there h'i,g also present Messrs. J. Stokes, T. Lewis, J. Mc- lotyre, T. Baster, W. J. Bagulcy, J. Tudor, G.C.Taylor, W. 'Talbot, J.P.. J.Weir, T. Darlington, W.Bradford,E. Brethercon, C. BoVianae, T. Wright, S. Woodward, S. Horton, Antwiss, J. Spruce, ifirshaw, W. Fletcher, P. Leather. E. Clartie, P. Gleave, Mawdslev, J. Edwards, Kydd ; Mr. J. Ashton (clerk), Mr. George' Ashton (deputy clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer), Mr. Dlggle and Mr.Swinton (surveyors).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>HELSBT ANS ITS SEWAGE.</head>
        <p>HELSBT ANS ITS SEWAGE.</p>
        <p>The committee ;Pponted to consider the mode of deal- ing with the sewage of He'shy met at the National Schools, Helsby, on Monday (May 23r3),—Mr. Diggle explained the scheme and plan suggested by Mr. Rad- ford, and Mr. Ashton produced the Manchescer Snip Canal agreement of the 15:h May, 1191, and read the clause therein with reference to Hoolpool Gutter, when on the motion of Mr. White, seconded by Mr. Collier it was resolved that an estimate of the cost of the scheme snonaitted by the Surveyor be prepared, on the assump- tion that instead of treating tho sewage as shewn on the plat% it be conveyed direct into Hoolpool Gutter at a point where such gutter may be held to be tidal,and that such estimate include not only the cost of construction hut also the cost of compensation to be paid for way. leave or otherwise.—On the motion of Mr. White, seconded by Mr. Collier, it was also resolved that having regard to the order of the Court in the action of the N -W. and Great Western Railway Cornpaniel v. tile Rural astricts Council of Runoorn, an estimate for the cost of the scheme with which the Railway Company wili be satisfied for treating the sewage by getting tanks or otherwise before passing under the Railway be pre- pared, and that it be shewn in such estimate what, in- stead of settling tanks, the cost ef conveying the sewage from the north-easterly corner of the Athletic Club field, by a pipe sewer parallel with the railway, in a Westerly direction, to and under the Railway Bridge near to Mr. Gu”st'entone yard and thence to the flushes on the Northerly side of the Railway, would be.—The report was received and adopted.</p>
        <p>STOCKTOH HEATH SWING lIRIDOR AND LATCHFORD LOCKS. The Clerk read the following letter from Mr. W. M. Huntei , engineer to the Ship Canal Compeny :—" Dear sir, I have to acknowledge the receipt of yours of the 12th inst., in which, on the part of the Rural District Council ot Runcorn, you presented to ms certain com• Plaints with regard to the operations of this company es ; Twenty Steps Bridge, Seacktuu [loath. It must he obvious to you that it is impossible for me to deal wi-.1-1 aerie:Al statement that this bridge "is frequently kept open for a !eager period than authorized by section 88 , (1) of ihe Ac; of 1885." I will be glad to be furnished with specific dates and hours at which it is alleged that the thrice during which the bridge was open were un- duly prolonged, alteugh as you are aware the section of the tee Act cf 1885 upon which the complaint was leased, was inserted for the protection of the Warrington Corporation and traders,and the District Council of Run- corn have therefore, no interest in it.—Moore Lane Bridge : I will be glad to have specific particulars as to the complaints about this bridge also ; I have no doubt , but that there are times when, u!ider en arrangement with nie residents in the district the bridge is epen in I the midnight hours when there is absolute-iy no traffic of any kind over the roads.—Footway across Latchford Lecke : I have nothine to add to my former communi- cation to you with regard to the ahoy). The footing across the canal is riot onle protected by suitable and sufficient band railings,but (in order to meet the views of the deputation from the different Councils which I naet ox the ground)additional band-railinge were erected on the piers wherever practicable, although there was no statutory obligation upon the Company to erect any handrailings, other than those on the footway across the canal. I presume that the suggestion that the lighting of the footway was provided for iu section 83(10) ot the Act of 1885 is derived from the letter to you from the clerk to the County Council of Chester, and that it i 9 not seriously advanced as being your own opinion en the matter. The question hardly admits of discussion as so clear a distinction between the bridges and the" foot- way "is maintained throughout the section referred to.—After some discussion the matter was left over for a fortnight.</p>
        <p>SATISFACTORY ARRANGEMENTS. The Clerk read a letter fro:n M•fSiCi. Walker, Smith. and Way with reference to the south road and a sewer- age pipe to run through a small pit belonging to Messrs. Walker. Smith, and Way, and explained that the mat- ter arose in connection with the sewerage of Weston. Some time ago it was arranged that the Council should pay an acknowledgemmt for the right of the pipe pass- ing through the property of Messrs. Walker. Smith, and Way, and in accordance with the resolution standing on the books he(Mr. Ashton)had sent the acknowledgement and submitted an agreemmt, which thoy had returned slightly amended. He suggested that the Council pur- sue the course he had taken which was unanimously agreed t,s. The costs for the paving of the South Road, ate, were apportioned to the respective property owners. RICIIIIVAY ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Diggle presented his estimate for the year 1898-99 for the Frodshatn section of the Council's dis- trict. Ordinary repairs were put down at £1,722 and and improvements at £270, inking a total of £1,992. The improvements included £lOO for Froieham and £2O for Halton, but the Highway Committee, after due con- sideration, decided to defer these two items for the present. This reduced the total estimate to £1,872. as compared with £2,070 last year. For Buiworth dis- trict Mr.Swinton estimated £,1860 for ordinary repairs, and £503 for improvements making a total lf £2,143,as compared with £2,188 for last year.—The estimates were paesed,with the exception of the Frodsham im- provements £lOO, which will come up at the next Coun- cil meeting. " FRODSFIAM REMAINS AS IT IS !" Mr. Reginald Potts, clerk to Cheshire Ctunty Council, writing with respect to the proposed formation of Fred- sham into an urban district, said he had been instructed Ito intimate to the Parish Council of Frodsbam that hav- ing regard to the absence of any support being accorded , to the mernoriil at the inquiry held by a committee of the County C tuncil,atirl to the fact that extensive urban powers have been conferred upon the Rural District Council for dealing with the parish, and alert to the further fact that the powers of the Rural District Coun- cil were exercised as reqoecred the parish by the Parish Council as a paroihial committee and bearing in mind that the parish is well supolied with water and effi- ciently drained the County Council was of opinion that no material benefit would acerue to the parish by the same being created into an urban district. The County Council were therefore not prepared to issue the order desired by the Parish Council.—No comment was made by the Council. This was all the business. OTHER MATTERS. The Clerk reported that with regard to the footpath at Preston-0%11-HW, he had gone into the matter and was of opinion the Council should take it over and re- pair h.—This gave rise to some discussion, but nothing was done in the metter.—The reports of the Medical Officer of Health, Inspector of Nuisances, and the Sur- veyors reports for Budwerth and. Frodsharn were also eubmitted.—lnstructions were given to advertise for macadam. FRODSHAII.</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM BRIDGE MISSION PrIIIRCII.-1 rummage sale O f a sucecsaful nature was held in the Town Hall, Frod- sham, on Wednesday afternoon last, in aid a the Bridge Mission Church, PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>WEDNESDAY' —Refoie :11 888,1:8. p.. Reynolds (in the chair)</head>
        <p>WEDNESDAY' —Refoie :11 888,1:8. p.. Reynolds (in the chair)</p>
        <p>and J. Ockleston.</p>
        <p>DRIVING WITHOUT A LlGHT.—Jarnes Knight. junior, Overton, was summoned or driving a horse and lorry a proper lamp, through Main-street, F r,dsh im at 11 p.m. on the 28th of April a light —PM. Price proved the case. A last without having attached to thc.vehi•-le, and for not having fine of 5s including costs was imposed. DRUNK. AND DISORDEHLY. —For being drunk and disorder- ly In Main-street, Frodshom, on the 12th May, Richard Houghton, Main street. was lined 5s including costs.—Ser- grant Clays preyed the escie. OBSCENE LANGUAGE IN THE STREET.—George Twaddle, Alvanly road, If elsby was summoned for using obscene language in Alvanly road on the 14 of May.—P.O. gave evidence to heiring the defendant using very disgust- ing language, and evidence was given by another witness that It was a usual oocurrerre to hear the defendant use bad language.—Fined 10s aud 18s 6d costs.</p>
        <p>ST. MARY'S, GREAT SANKEY. On Sunday the Rev. J. R. Jones conducted the services at St. Mary's Church, Great Sankey, and during hie sermon stated that during the past week there had passed away from our midst on who for the greater part of a century had stood out among the great men of the Empire as a statesman, a schriar, a gentleman and a Churchman. William Ewart Gladstone had gone hence amid a nation's lamentations, nay more, amid a world- wide grief. He said they had witnessed the sunset of a glorious life, beautiful at its rising, resplendent at its zanith, magnificent at its decline. The world could ill afford to lose in these days of self-interest and self-indul- gence a public man whose life was an object lessors to every department of A. WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>The Rector, Canon Penrhyn, preaehed in the morning in Winwick Church on our Lord's ASC9RBiOfI into heaves; and after epealeng of the assurance which we have therein of the way being open to all of us to heaven, he concluded : This was the hope which was held firmly by that great statesman whose loss the whole nation is now deploring ; this it was which enabled him so calmly to await his summons, and when the time came te depart in peace. Endowed as he was with the rarest gifts of intellect and eloquence, he has used them dligently and conscientiously for the higher inteleste of his people; and while much of his timeand his thoughts were necessarily taken up with high attaire of State, yet he never allowed them to interfere with his religious duties or make him forgetful of his God, or of that rest wilds re- maineth for the people of God, to which he has now oeen called after his prolonged and arduous la boars.</p>
        <p>WATIRTNG}TDY BO RD OF GUARDIA.NS.</p>
        <p>A meeting of this Board was held in the Board-room on Wednesday morning. Mr. G. Pilling presided, and there were also present :—Mrs. slay, Mrs. Edelsten, the Rev. W. H. Drummond, Rev. J. El. Rid(lett, Rev. T. R. Matthews. Messrs. J. W. Knowles, A. J. Jolley, A Jolley, J. Clarke. J. Billinge, T. H. Ashcroft, S. Davies, T. B. Malkin, P. Cuuliffe, J. Dignum, Forshaw, H. Thornton, William Houghton, J.P., A. Brooke, William Hickman, J. Ball, Dr. P4tterson, T. Halton, with Mr. J. C Sutton, (clerk), Mr- S. Sutton (assistant clerk), Mr. Ward (Workhouse master), Mr. Wright (superintendent Pad- gate schools). TEIE MINUTES OF THE PREVIOUS MEETING.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Board were unusually long, con- taining a great many financial details. After twenty five minutes had been spent in listening to the Clerk reading the minutes Mr. DALES interrupted and said : lam sorry to in- terrupt you, Mr. Clerk, hut our time-- The CLERIC : You are interrupting me. (Laughter).</p>
        <p>Mr. DAVIES : But cur time is being--- The CLERK : You are responsible if the Guardians say at some fteure period that important information of this kind is not disclosed to you. Mr. HICKMAN : It won't be remembered. The CLERK: If you put me bstween two tools I don't know which to sit upon. (Laughter). Mr. SUTTON continued the reading for several minutes more, and when he had concluded Mr. PILLING remarked : We understand all you have read, lam sure. (Loud laughter). The minutes were approved. PAUPER NURSING AT THE WORKHOUSE.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. JOLLEY moved " That as by the Medical Officer's report the addition of two nurses will dispense with pauper help to the Nursing staff the Board resolves upon such increase." In doing so Mr. Jail," referred to the action of the House Committee in the matter. When the new Nursing Order was issued by the Local Government Board the Board of Guardians approached thern,and asked whether they would allow them to go on the old lines until tho new hospital was finished. The Local Government Board declined to do so and insisted on some change being made in the nursing staff. One step was insisted upon by the Local G...vornment Board and that Was the appointment of a superintend- ent nurse. That had born carried out, and at the time it was thought, if practical, that the Board should try to do away with pauper help in nursing. About the general principle of having treined nursea in place of pauper nurses be thought there could scarcely he two opinions. Before the last seleetion of the Board of Guardians, the house Commit- tee asked the Medical Officer to report to them as to the practicability or doing away with pauper help. Some time elapsed before the Medical Officer made his report. He went carefully into the subject and at a recent meet. lug of the Reuse Ceminittee the doctor attended and told them of the irquiries he had made, and the conclu- sions he had arrived at. The Hoes,. Committee consi- dered the matter and decidel to recomrnerd for tbe ac- ceptance of the Beard several nurses who were to have appeared before them that morning. The nurses had not attended,and itshowed theie was a big demand for nurses at the present time. The recently ap- pointed nurse—Miss Wilde—gave in her notice, and her time was now sip. She would not stop any longer. He ventured to hope that with the Superintendent nurse they had got they would be able to keep their nurses, and carry out some reforms. He thought the Beard ought to make an effort to put down pauper nursing. He was of the opinion that it could be done, eltheuele not immediately. From the report of the doctor he was of opinion that if they could de away e ith pauper nursing they could do sway with ten wardsmen and wardawotnen. Mr. KNOWLES (interrupting) D you want us to be- lieve that two paid nurees can de as much work as ten pauper aesistanta ? Mr. JOLLEY : The addition of two nUrsAs will enable you to do away altogether with wardsmen and wards- women. Mr. KNOWLES : What is the minimum number of nurses that can do the work ? Mr. A. J. JOLLEY : Dr. Braid e says six. Mr. KNowinn : That is what he thinks and when we have got them I suppose we shall find that we want an- other six. Mr. A. J. JOLLEy (cnntinuirm) said if members of the Board allowed themselves to be got at by paid officers of the Union and allowed officers to buttonhole them outside, to argue with them, and talk them round, the work of the Board would nor he well done. Ile had no hesitatien in saying that if this reform of naid nurses was carried oat it would improve the discipline of the Rouse. IF they got rid of ten men and wereen from the hospital and out of the house, the saving in the e.eaL of maintenance would pretty nearly counterbalance the cost of two acldttiflii nurses which they would have to get. At present in order to g.t the work done in the hospital by pouriers little privileges had to he given them, and these privileges interfered with the discipline of the house. At the present time the four nurses had to be on night work half their time,but if six nurses were engaged four could be engaged on day-work and two during the night. This would greatly relieve the night duty for each nurse, and the Board would probably have less changes than they had had in their nursing staff. Mr. KNOWLES What is going to become of these pauper as-istants ? Mr. A. J. Joeter : They will have to go outside and earn their own living. The CHAIRMAN : Most of them are able-bodied per- sons. Mr. Karowtes : Have we power to turn them out. If they can go out why dent they go now? Mr. A. J. JOLLEY : That matter will be dealt with by the House Cr mmittee. Mr. KNOWLES said when the subject of nursing was before the Board on the last occasion they appointed one superintendent nurse and one asaistent, and it was then understood that these would be sufficient to comply I with the requirements of the Local Government Board. It was decided to make ule of pauper nurses until such time as the new infirmary was ready to be occupied. That met with the approval of the Board because they could not get out of it. If it took ten or twelve men and women in the workhouse to assist in nursing, the Board would find that they needed an in- creased number of efficient nurses. Two ad- ditional nurses would not be sufficient. He did not see why an able-bodied pauper was not quite as able to do the ordinary nursing of a sick person as a skilled woman. These trained nurses would require a large salary, and a considerable amount of money would have to be expended in finding the necessary accommo- dation for them It was true that a new Board had been elected, and perhaps seine of the new members were not as enthusiastic in saving the ratepayers money as the previpus Bard was. He should certainly vote against the prnpr•sitien. Mr. PILLING'S seconded the resolution, and Mr. Hick- man supported it. Mr. JOSHUA BALL said he should vote against it. The Rev. W. H. DRUMMOND said in the interests of the ratepayers and a true policy of oublic economy, he shnuld sunpnrt the resolution. The people who were in the hospi'al ought to be treated with every considera- tion that the law allevred them. It was in evidence in the recent Nursing Order that the Local Government Board did not consider it was desirable to have pauper nursing. Mr. Knowles wished only to go as far as the law compelled him to go, hut there was another policy, and that was of acting in the spirit of the law. (Rear,_ hear). Pauper nursing had been proved not to be desir- able, and they wonld onle he complying with the spirit of the Local Government Order, and consulting the trup.t interests of the ratepayers. and ef the inmates in the hospital, if they increased the nursing staff. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY haying replied to the criticisms, a vote was taken when the following voted in support of the resolution :—Dr. Patterson, Revs. T. R. Matthews, W. H Drummond, and J. H. Riddette, Mrs. Clay and Edeieten, Messrs. A. J. Jelley, A. Jolley' Dienutn, Forebear, Malkin. Billinge, Cunliffe, Ashcroft, Hickman, S. Davies, and T. Flatten. It was not considered neces- sary to take a negative vote, and the resolution was carried. HOUSE COMMITTEE.—TFIE CLASSIFICATION OF</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>PAUPERS.</head>
        <p>PAUPERS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of this committee on the 29th April, Mr. A. J.Jolley was re-elected. The committee decided that it is desirable to extend the existing classfication of in- door poor according to sex and age by a further classifi- cation according to the conduct and antecedents, and a small sub-committe be appointed to inquire into and re- port upon the practicabili-y of such a reform, the mm- mittee to consist of the following :—Mrs. Clay, the Rev. w. H. Drummond, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, A. Jolley, sad Mr. Knovrtns said the subject of classification was discussod at a meeting of the Board some months ago, and the members decided against it. That being so he Ithoneht it ought net to come again before the Board without special notice having been given. It was so im- portant a matter that it ought to have come before the Board and not before the House Committee. Mr. A.J, JOLLEY said the special resolution passed at the annual meeting of the Board empowered any com- mittee to appoint Fuh-committees. That was all the House Committee was doing in this instance. There was really nothing for the Board to consider at present. The minutes were then adopted. THE MASTER'S REPORT. The MASTER rep,irted that lately the vagrant wards hall boon overcrowded and on one occasion some of the mon had to sleep on the floor. He acknewledzed the re- ceipt of parcels of Papera from Mr. Crosfield. Walton Lea ; from Mr. Barrett, 91, Bewsev-street; and from the Rev. T. R Matthews. and Mr. Ashcroft and Mr. Harry . Toft, of Sinkey-qtreet ; seriptural pictures for Use in the schoolroom and infirmary from the 11,v. J. H. Ricldette, and throu2h the Rev. W. H. Drummond dowers for the hospital frnm Cairo-x.reet school. AN oFFICFRS COMMITTEE TO BE FORMED. Mr. A. J. JOLLEY moved the following resolution of which he had given notice That a committee of nix Guardians to be called the Officers' Committee be cansti- tuted,and that such committee be empowered to meet</p>
        <p>as they may decide for the following purposes : to con- sider and, when necessary, to report upon the duties of any officer or servant of the Board or upon any applica- tion made to them by any such officer or servant in ra- tion to such duties ;to make from time to time any recommendation they may decide upon in regard to the salary or other remuneration of any officer or servant of the Board, provided that so far as any such recommen- dation relates to an officer or servant employed at the workhouse or at the Par ?gate schools the same shall be first submitted to the Workhouse or the School Commit- tee ; that any application by any officer or servant for increase of salary or any other remuneration be first submitted 0 the Officers Committee ; that proceedings by statutory committee, as regulated by public statute, be excluded from the foregoing resolution." He Said the resolution was framed in tolerably wide terms, and they bad considerable powers in the matter of initiation. A. strong Committee should be formed ; if a weak one was appointed it would probably do more harm than good. A strong committee would be benefi- cial in many ways, and could make itself fen; amongst the officers—a committee prepared to take ry.tice of any laxity of adminiatration, and to encourage goad work. (Hear, hear). Mr. BILLINGS seconded, and several members sup- ported the motion, which was carried. Eventually it was decided that the following form the committee :—Mrs. Clay, and Messrs. Billinge, Davies, Houghton, A. J. Jolley, and Pilling. This woe all the business.</p>
