      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>BEFORE THE MA.GIST&amp;ATEe.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MA.GIST&amp;ATEe.</p>
        <p>YOUTHFUL GAMESTERS AT WARRTNOTON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington dorough Police Court on Monday before Mr. F. Monks and other magistrates,George Wat- son (17), and John Pemberton (17), both of Watkin-st. were charged with tessing for money on Sunday. the 15th inst. Both pleaded not guilty.—P.C. Whittaker said about 11.15 a.m, on the day in question he was on duty,in plain clothes, in the neighbourhood of Clegg-st. Be noticed a nernber of boys in Back Watson-street playing a game of chance. They saw witness and P.C. Carter who accompanied him, and dispersed. The two defendants were caught. Watson said he had not played for a month and Pemberton said "All right."—P.C. Carter and P.C. Smith corroborated the evidence.— Watson now denied that he was tossing; he had not been out more than five seconds. Pemberton also said he was not playing. A Mrs.Selly said Watson had not left the house more than a few seconds before he was ar- rested by he policeman.—The Bench fined defendants 53. each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUG El POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>PRlDAT.—Before the Meyer (ha the chair), Councillor Brost hurst, Mr. James Hepla•rd, and Dr. Corns</p>
        <p>TEMPORARY AurrroarrY.—Henry Hayes, applied for tem- porary authority to sell at the Boar's Head.r_own Hill. In lien of Joseph Caslin t —Granted. DRUNH ArD DISORDERLY.—Robert Mclfinnion Berwick, Coffee House, Horsemarket-street. for this offence in Sankey. street. at ten minutes past nine on Thursday night. proved by PC Dawson, was lined ss. Annie Grry, of no settled abode, for this offence in Scotland-road, at twenty minutes past n ne on Thursday night was fined ss. or seven days in default. MownlY.—Before Mr. Fred Monks (in the chair), Councillor Shaw Green, Councillor Smethurst. Alderman Blackhurst, Mr. Thomas Birchen, and Mr. S. Wright. DRUNK AND DISMIDERLY.—Henry White, labourer, 29. Iloyd-streed. was charged with this offence on Saturday night. The bench dismiss-fl the cose ;Al 13E1 Ann Needham, widow from O'dham, was charged with the same offence on Sunday night. PC. Rerldington said it occurred in Butter- market street at twen.y minutes to nine. On the defendant promising to behave herself and go back to Oldham,the ben .h dismissed the case ; Patrick Burke fish hawker,3 Glasshouse Bow. for this offence in Scotland-road on the 16' h May at 940 a.m. proved by P.C. Edwards, was fined 5s and costs. Dx—wir IN CHARGE —Hearry Bamford, carter. 19 Foundry- street, was charged with being drunk in charge on the 14th May. The evidenca was that the defendant on the day in question was drunk io ohs roe of two horses and a lurry on School-brow The bench inflicted a fine of 5s and costs. OBsCENE LANGUAGE.—Winifresi Cosgrove, b s'riars-lane was charged by P.S. Evans with making use of obscene language on the 16th May. The officer's evidence was that on the day in question in Friars-lane the defendant was mak- ing use of obscene language for shout twenty minutes. The language w s not fit to repeat.—The bench fined her 5s or in default seven days. limitrzzt,sm DOGS.—Samuel Cross, butcher, Market-place, for having two dogs unmuzzled in Bold-street on the 12th May was fined 2s 61. DAMAGING IIaOWiNG POTATOES.—MIchaeI Haves and Salll,.el Annock. both of Dale-street. Sa key Bridge., were charged with damaging growing potatoes belonging to Mr. Thomas Lowe. farmer. of Sankey.--P.C. Wool gage evidence to the effect that he cautioned the lads about this. on the leth of Mey.—Mr. Thomes Lowe said tie saw the lads on the 20th committing the damage, but he could not catch them. He got their names from some other lads.--The bench fined the lads Ts. 61. each or the alternitive, If their mothers would consent of them being bir,hen DEPHRTERa.—Thomas Hewitt was charged with having ! deserted from the Ist Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, ! stationed at Cork, since the 28th February. 1R95. Prisoner gave himself up at the Barrack.—Martin Hogan was charged with having deserted tram the Ist Battalion Royal Munster Fusileers stationed at Fermev. I-el ,nd. Hogan presented himself for enli.tinent in a Liverpool regiment, inquiries were made which elicited the fact that he was a deserter from another regiment. —Private Perrey proved both cases. —The two prisoners were remanded to Strmageweys WEDillisDAY.—Before Mr. John Patrolenigh (In the chair) Mr Thomas Birchall. and Mr. Geddes. HER 73m) APP TAR ANCE.—Eliza Smith was summoned lir drunken and disorderly helm, lour in the Workhouse—Pri- mer pleaded guilty.--Mr. Shearer on behalf of the Union authorities. said the prisoner was an Innate of the work- bonse. continually rotor, nR and leaving. She took her dis- charge from the workhouse on Friday and on Sunday evening she came hack *bout eight o'clole and carried on to such an extent they had to hand her over to the pollee. —The Chief Constable This is her 73rd appearance When as' ed, she had nothing to ssv.—The Bench committed her to prison for 21 days with hard labour. THURSDAY. —Before Messrs. Chandle7 in the chair) and . .</p>
