        <p>NEWTON PETTY SESSIONS. FIRST COTIRT.</p>
        <p>InttaSDlY.—Before Captain Withltudon (in the chair),l Colonel Woo.i, Rnd Mr. T. J Gillespie. TMUNIC AND DISORDERI.7.—The following persons were fined for this offenee. B41:7 Peers. 2s t3d, and costs, and Ann Coyl. Harle^tawn. ss. and costs. CYCLES WITTIonT LIGHTS —Mark Callon and Thomas Turton. were each rimed Is. and costs, for riding bicveles at Wit:wick, on the 19th May, without having light after Sunset. Doo TINMITzZrEn. —Dr. II inlysile, of Barleetown. Wa fined 31. lid neluding costs. for having his dog insecurely maze&amp; in SAnkm -rt., itarlestown, on the 213th May. A W.LTMINOToN HAWKER FINED FOR ()RUMAT. —John William McCormick, hawker, of Warrington. was summoned for illtreating a horse by beating it on the 17th May, at Newton.—lnspeetor Oreatrix stated he saw defendant on the date named in charge of a horse at Newton horse fair. It was In poor con.ll,lon, ant defendant heat, it ten or fourteen times with n whip stodr. Witness drew his attention to it, and defendant said he was very sorry for what he had done. There were lumps all over the horses abonlder.—lnspector Lucking. corroborated.— A fine 0° 10., arid costs was imposed Catrwry To HOASF.9 --Joseph Pickering of Ash.on ws summoned for working a horse whilst in an unfit state at Haydock on 13th May.—lnspector flrentreT said on the date named he saw defendant in e,,arge of a horse and cart near the race course. on toe nits ler sd trank leading to litydock. Iv/taw ermined the horse and toned it In very p -tor condi_ tion. It was very lame and was suffering from AlTtSrllB. He ordered the man to take the Ireele home. Later in the da; he again saw defendaot in charge of the lame horse. Ile was tgoing in the opposite direction to his honsc._inspector 1. sckings COro'te4 and said the animal appeared in gle,t pale :—Threnas Rigby, coal' see notoinco wrong collier, of Ashton, said he with tho , the animal—A fine of 20s. end costs m131:99;0rArthur ked Grundy and Wilbrahtm Martin both of We o sthooghton e- was i n .71 summoned, the former for causing a e to the latter for working the same whilst in an unfit spate on the 14th of May.—lnspector Luckings roved and said the animal was nothing but the ea ekin_ _s. and o,ats.and rone swdbi gitartin 20 Grundy was fined 3i., and costs a hares, In an unfit —James Marsh pleaded guilty to working f state on the 13th Hay at Ne am t.— A line o Ios. and costs was IMPme&amp;—Robert Hamann wa: fined 209. and costs far &amp;similar offence at Newton on the 14th 31 '9- CRUELTY TO A COW.--Jobn Horrock was minim lned for ornelty to a Caw ors t,•ff 17th Hey. at Newton Cattle fair. • Inspector Lnckinga said his attention was drawn to defen- dant's cow in the Newton cattle fir on the date named. It was suffering very badly from bang overstoosed with Milk. The wider was very hard end the animal w *tanling With its lege wide apart. Defend-ant said the cow was milked that monslng,—A line of 20s. and coats was imposed. THE ALLFGED WIitANING CAS. AT NEWTIV.—ChO.I4OI Smethurst was charged on remand with stealing 108 by</p>
