      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <p>means of a trie-c from J. Wor'hington.Of Warrington.Similar evidence to that which has already been given at the War- rington Court aria published in these Columns was tendered. The magistrates considering that prisoner was not th 4 prin- cipal and had not h.ndled the rao,ev and that he had been been in custody a fortnight d mlded to di-charge him. A DANGEROII9 MANI AT iANKRY.—WiIIiaM Moody, a ticket of leave man. was charge] with loitering with intent to commit a felony at sankey on the 23rd May.—P.O. Du.s- dale said early on the morning be saw the prisoner coming from the direction of Whittle Hall Farm. Witness stopped him and on s-archint him f mai a gimlet and a piece of wire with string attached in his cap. He also found a box of matches and a small piece of black machin- tosh. The gimlet was for the purpose of boring holes 112 windows and then the wire and string were inserted in the holes to pull the catch of the window back. On the 23rd February Sankey Bridges Station was broken into and also on the 26th, March Mr. Wright's house was entered Entrances to these places had been effected by the boring of holes into the windows and the catches being drawn back by the !insertion of wire and strlnt.—Prisoner was also charged with failing to report hlmself.—Prisouer's license was revoked and he was ordered to comolete his term of imprisonment. SECOND COURT.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. T. D. Grinatch Drayton (in the chtir) and Mr. C B F. B ayon.</p>
        <p>Dausirot Liercreso Barkhead on the 13th May. drunk and disorderly at Haydock and refusing to quit the premises of the " Vfa4on and Horses." The case was proved and the mazistrstes fined him 21 6d and costs. DOG AT LAR6E.—Levi lialtonshaw for having a dog at large on the 19th May at Golborne was fined 31 6d,</p>
        <p>A TRIVIAL ASSAULT —William Tongan summoned Edward Finley for assaulting him on the 11th May at Croft.— Defendant pif aded guilty.—Tunstall said he went into the public house to hava a !lass of beer when Pfnley hit him.— Finley when asked what he had to say, said they hod some words about a hedge and he jest struck Tunstall with his open hand.—The Bench said It was a trivial case hut the defendatr, had pleaded guilty, they would therefore flee him the costs. _ _ .</p>
        <p>TB/MT Op felonry* BY BOVI —Joseph Beswick (14) and Harold Twist (10). were charged with stealing £3 12e., the money of Adam Unsworth, on the 15th May, at Golboree.— Mr, E. A. Whle of Werringtoa, appeared for Beswiek and Mr. it Stant for the bay Twist.—The first witness called for the prosectition was Mary Bosworth. wife of Adam Bosworth. resid'ng at 10i, High-street, Golborne.—The evidence was to the effeet that she left her house on the morning of the 15th May. When she returned the same night she found the bedroom upset. drawers pulfed Out and clothing pulled about. She missed £3 from the mid tie drawer of a washstand. She also missed half a sovereign out of a purse and two eh Mugs out of a sitting room.—Margaret Mather. 103, High-street. Goiborne, said her back door over- looked Mrs. Unsworth's yard Between 3 and 4 o'clock on holi- day afternoon she was in he- bedroom,and saw Harold Twist In that backyard sawing a box. She keiacked at the back door and he went away. Later she saw him with the bey Joseph Beswick in Unsworth's yard. They were both smoking cigarettes. When they went away Beswicklfastened thegate and climbed hver the wall. She only saw Helmick at six o'clock in the afternoon.—Mr. Stuart cross-examined her, and she said the boys had been In the habit of going to Uneworths.—Samuel Collier, engineer, 88 High-street, Golborne, said that about 7 o'closk on the 15th inst. the boy Twist came to his shop. He want id an air-gun, said it was for his father, and tendered a 10e piece for It. He gave him Sc. change. The boy Beswick came In for some cigarettes, and gave him Is. to pay for them. He gave him 6d. change He asked him who they were lot and the lad said for Adam Unsworth. PC. Wild stationed at Golhorne said at 4-30 on the 18th May. he apprehended the two prisoners, and he charged them with breaking end entering the dwelling house mentioned, and stealing £3 12s Beswlek said in answer, that when he was with Twist in Uns worth's yard, Twist opened the back window while they were there, Twist took a shilli,-g out of his pocket. He (Beswick) asked him where he got it He said he hal won some money betting. After he said he was " colding "as he had saved it. Twist went upstairs in the house and brought down a parse with money in it. They spent the money. Twist said in answor to the charge that he opened the window the first time. and Beswick the second time.—Mr. White said he would prove that the boy Beswick was not there at Ike time it was Slew&amp; the theft was committed. He called James Doyle aged 15, who said on the Sunday he went to school with Beswick and afterwards to church and then they went a walk and arrived back at 4-45.—Mr• Stuart said he did not purpose calling any evidence for his client. The probability was that if this money was taken the smaller ; and younger boy would be under the control of the larger I boy. His client had never been in the home and there was; no evidence to prove it. He had got the 10s. from the boy Beswick for the gun, end before they could convict they must be perfectly satlsflad that Vie money spent belonged to Adam 17nsworth. He would like to euglest thst the boys were young and should be bound over.—Mr. White for If client said he could not a Tee with Mr. Stu .rt about the younger boy being under the infloence of the other One boy was trying to shUt the blame es another. The only boy found to be spending any money with the exception of a shilling was Twist.—The Be ich of ter considering the case said they were of opinion that. the case was pro :tad and the lads' fathers were bound Over in the sum of £5 in their recognisances to come no when called upon. INTENT TO COMMIT A FELONY.—Samuel Bradford, labourer. Ed no fixed abode was charged with being found in a building belonging to R. Evans and Co. at Haydock, and with entering a school at Havdock hat-wean the 19th and 20th inst., with intent to steel a sum of money.—Prisoner pleaded gnilty.—The evidence was that prisoner had broken a win- dow in the school and was evideeVy looking for money.— P.C. Harrison proved apprehending Brad'ord in Church-rd., Herlock. The prisoner had made a statement which was taken down in writing- In it he said he was pass.ng the school about a week ago at 3 o'clock. He broke open the window with his hand covered with his cap He went in and broke open a desk. On the Friday morning following he went in again and broke open a cuphoard.—The Soperintendent said the prisoner had been in the Asylum twice.—The bench committed him for 14 days Dlal/NIC AND A.IA.IILTING A POLICE CONTABLE.—John Flaherty was charged with being drunk and disorderly at Croft on the 23rd Mey,ftrid also with assaulting P.C. Calla- ghan at the same time whilst in the di-charge of his duty Prisoner pleade 1 guiltv.—The evidenee proved that, at ten o'clock on the M ,ndiy night, 23rd May at Heath-lane,Croft. the defendant WAS ejecta i from the Ploisith Inn. He was very rough. The constable said he would have to take him into custody if he was not quiet. Flaherty said na one coillo take him. He then gruel( the constable.—The Bench fined him 51 and costs for being drunk and disorderly and 20e and Wets for the assetslt.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN YE r MoSIDAY.- Before Mem* IF tfortnin (In the aver), and</p>
        <p>Jahn Oekletton.</p>
        <p>I TEMPMI &amp;RV TRAVSFER. The temporary transfer or the license of the Botthons - Inn, Mersey str et. Runcorn, Wee granted from Agnes Jane Hewitt to Alfred Tames. ADJoUrnexn.- This was a case In which Annie iloyle, of husband Peter Coyle. for Weston Point summon sd he- assault at Weston Point. on May 9th. -Mr. Chnrtost. Chester. represented the defen Sant. and Mr Burton the eompisinant. Mr. Churton at the outset. asked that an adjournment of the case be granted owing to one of the witneilleA. who wits 17:ng merlons'), 111 at Chester Infirmary being unsh'e to attend, He produced a medical certificete, and ask-d that then se he adjourned until June 7th. Mr B.srton said he had no serious objections to an adjournment and the bench granted the ap plicetion Me INDIGVANT DEPTIVTANT.-John Kiloarif/ of Weston, was summoned for being drunk and -iisorderly in Heath- road. Weston on the 14th May last -In answer to the "barge the defendant pleaded guilty, but contradicted the statement of P 0. Morris as to being drunk and disorderly In ste th-road ; he was In kis own house.-The Magistrates Clerk : If you want to ask questions or made a state-nent, why do you plead vilify -Defend ant Ido plead ently. -The Magistrates Clerk: Then why esy anything more about it? -Defendant I was in M 7 own house.-The Magistrates Clerk : Then you had better plead " not gulity."-Defeniant : Very well.-Rvidence was then given by P.O: Morris that on the night in goestion defendant sr s drunk and disorderly in Heath-road end using abominable language. Tha officer in company with P.O. Gregory took the man home but it was no use, and the defendant con. , ducted hinegelf in a disgraceful manner.-P.O. Gregory corroborated.-Defendant began to make a statement that the police had entered his h ruse and struck him with a stick. -The B meh : Asir the question ; 70.5 can make a statement afterwards-The Police denied doing any such thing -Sergeant Lee gave evidence as to hearing* row and seeing the constables taking • man, who he believed was the defendant. home--Del end an' (to the rnagistretet) : "I don't see how they can stand there and take the book and tell sneh Iles, sir. I see its no use me saymv anything.- Defend ,nt was fined 5s and Its and costs or Ewen days in defenit. FiLucvit CAPVIEM oF • Twirl BY TWO Won r:N.-Henry Tedow, a sailor, was charged on remind with the larceny of la table cloth, the property of Mrs Jones. Volunteer Inn. aranviile-street, value-1 at 41 Gil. on Friday laet -Mrs. Annie Jonee, wife of Mr. J dward Jo as. the proprietor of the Volunteer Inn said she was in the house and heard a tap at the front window, tint took no notice of it. after that she ' heard someone in the reed, and on going to the front door saw the prisoner going off. Tha table cloth (produced) was hanging en the ninth ,a line in the para. When she missed the tablecloth she went, In company with Mary Blien Sant- ley, after the man. whom she had never seen b,fore and caught him in High-street. They got the sloth from him. It was ueder his resat -Mary Allen leantley corroborated add- ing that they watched imismer out of sight. She missed the table cloth first and told Mrs Joaes.-P.O. Rills deoa.ad to beinl on duty about 12 midnight on Prlst ay. In High-et-asst. He saw the two women come up to the man. ge to'lk prisoner In charge tor stealing the table cloth, ts which he made no reply.-In answer to the Bench prisoner sdd he knew nothing about the offenses Ste could not unientanl anything about it; but avossid plead guilty an-I e'e't to be summarily dealt with.-Sent mead to Insprisenment for seven days with hard labour. IMMORHAUTY.—Msry White,an aged woman, was mulcted in a fine and costs amounting to 40s with the alternatly of one months' Imprisonment, for soliciting In Runcorn streets. —pi). Wylie proved the ease. .</p>
        <p>CHILI' 4WD PLZ&amp;SANT Ss&amp; TRIPS. —FOr a p'easant ex• cumin° to El glywell, St. Winifridefia eell, Pantasaph, Prestatyn angl Rhyl, one of the best routes is a cheap sea trip from Liverpool to Mnatyn by the fast paddle salooe steamer "Flying F,l-on." Tie vessel makes the ret,Xn journey the cam. day, an.l the aysrage palsagn ,is one hour and 45 minutes. Toe fare br the return Journey is deck 2i. bridge N This is nno, of the cheap- est and least tedi ,us era trips out of Liverpool, and en. shies the passengers to it one of the prettiest spots in N irth Wales. The times of saillins sell be futind in our advertising column. Another eejoyable sea trip hv the came steamer is that between Li vrepeni .and Southport: On Monday (Sank Holiday) the steamer will leave the Princes' Landing Stage, Livernool at 10 45 a.M. The vessel Will leave Southport at e p m the same dap, thus e,iving passengers about six hegira ashore. The return are is 2s.</p>
