      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>1 111</head>
        <p>1 111</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, MAX 27, 1898</p>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL VACANCY IN WHITECROSS WAH,D, • WARIIINGIrON.</p>
        <p>A GOOD FLGITT AND A. SPLENDID VICTORY.</p>
        <p>Monday was an exciting day in Whitecrose Ward Warrington, when the electors were celled upon to elect a Councillor to represent them in place of Mr. Roberts, who, as is now well known, is an Alderman of the Borough. The election was keenly contested by both sides, and it is questionable whether any previous election in the ward has aroused so much interest. Mr. James Evans,of Ehzebeth-street,baker and provision dealer, was the first candidate in the field, and he was supported by the Liberals. Ile was opooeed by Mr. Williamn Bellian. tool manufacturer of ‘Lovely-laue, the nominee of the Conservative party. Both sides were well supported by energetic workers, and a number of vehicles were placed at their disposal to bring up the voters. Mr. Evans had the largest number of werkers reiident in the Ward, whilst on the 'other hand, a number of mem- bers connected with the junior Conservative A.,•sociation assisted Mr. Bellian. A motor , cycle run in the interest of M. Bellien attracted a great deal of attention. The walls were blazoned with a plentifol supply of mural literature., One placard posted on benalf of Mr. Evans read as follows :, "Electors of Whitecross Ward. Read this!,! Bridge-street Improvement. "Feathers Inn" Arbitration, a big win for the rate- payers The owner's claim was £15,800. The Um- pire has awarded the sum of £4,610. The sum thus seved to the ratepayers upon the claim is .£11.190. Alderman Sutton valued the property at £8,855, or ' nearly double the amount awarded by the umpire. Is this what Mr. Sutton considers workinglor the linterest of the ratepayers? If you value the good work done on your behelf vote for James Evans on Monday next, and so return a man to the . Council who will siwengthen the hands of those who are de- termined that your money shall sot be wasted.' Another placard advised the electors to vote for James Evans, "the best man." • On the other hand a great quar.tity of bite ink had, been used in requesting the' electors to support Mr. Bellian. Spanning Bostock-street in two places, in blue let- ters on a white ground, was the request "Vote for Bellian, the popular candidate P wnile on the walls were the requestkalso in large blue letters, " Who is the best man? Why, William Ballian." Some amusement, was created by the circulation of a email blue card in black letters which read:— " Whitecrose Ward election. Good morning. Have you voted for Bellies? You won't be happy till you do." . _ _ _ . _</p>
        <p>'I- When the poll opined at eight o'clock, rain was falling heavily, and this somewhat retarded the efforts of the workers. But towards noon the weather was aU that could be desired, aril considerable activity was shown in bringing the voters to the poll. That they succeeded in their effmte may be judged by the fact that nearly 000 polled out of an electorate of 1080. In the Liberal Committee Room Alderman Roberts, assisted by Mr. William Hayes and Mr. Clayton superintended the work of bringing up voters, and the checking off of those who had al- ready voted. A number of Liberal Councillors visited . the Ward during the day. St. Barnabas School, in Borock-street was used. as the polling booth, where there Were two stations. The presid- ing officers were Messrs. Stanley Wilson awl T. Spinks, and ths poll clerks Messrs. A. Starkey and E. Rough, while Messrs. W. E. Roberts, R. Burrows, Walter Bell ian,and R. Y. Woollacot offi- ciated as personation agents. After tea, large crowds assembled in the vicinity of the polling booth, and cheers were raised for the respective candidates, but the utmost good humour prevailed. Promptly at eight o'clocit the polling booth was closed, and the ballot papers immediately conveyed to the Town Hall. The result of the poll was declared at twenty minutes to nine by Aider- man Hutchinson, the returning abet for the Ward. The figures were as follows : Mr. Rvans 579 Mr. Belllan 313</p>
        <p>Majority for Mr. Evans ......... 268 Prior to the announcement a vote of thanks was accorded the returning officer, on the proposition of Mr. Evans, seconded by Mr. Bellian, the latter observing that although Mr. Evans and himself had been fighting that day they had been good friends all their lives. REJOICINGS AT THE LIBERAL CLUB. .</p>
        <p>Councillor Evans's supporters afterwards escorted him to the Liberal Club, where he was most enthu- siaeticelly received in the large billiard room. :aldermen Ronsarrs said they all knew the result of that da:.'s election, and it was exceedingly satis- factory to them from every p tint of view. At any rate, one thiug was quite certain, and that was that the electors of the ward knew a man when they saw him. (Applause.) He thought they knew the differenee between wnat they might call reseectable mediocrity in the shape of ability, and real genuine ;.;,3crl stuff. (Laugher and applause.) It was due to him to say this, es Chairman of the Ward Committee, that the committee and officers hid worked hard—quite as hard as if the issue of the election depended upon each man. The way they had winked that day surpaesed anything he had ever seen. Nothing had Seen too much trouble throughout the time during which the poll had been open. (Hear,bear.) Ile had never seen such devo- tion to a candidate; he was a good man, and that was what had secured hie return. fie would not say a single word against their opponents. The only difference was that the Liberals had the better man of the two, but they certainly owed their thanks to a number of discriminating Conserve- rib esivelp had Preferred to vote for Ifr.Evans rather than be bullied into supporting the nominee of the Conservative Club. (Hear, hear, and applause.) Re had now very much pleasure in introducing his friend of many years standing—Councillor James Evans. (Loud applause.) Councillor JAMES Flvsiqs, who was cheered again and again on rising to address the meeting, said he would only speak a word or two to them, as he telt like an °le dog run down and wished for rest after a hard day's work. He felt very mach indebted to all present for their exertions on his behalf. He had w„rkei hard, and so had his friends, and he believed that all had had an eye to the cause. (Hear, hear) They fel. that they had seen fiehting for the right, and they believed in right triumphing. ( tlear,hiser). He also wished to way a few words about his good friends the Conservatives in Whitecross Ward; there were a number of Conservative friends of his who had done well for him that day. They had, in fact, risen above party p )litice and feeling, and. when he knew who his onponent was to be he determined that no element of a party character should be introduced into the contest. (Applause) tie hoped the electors would never be ashamed of his character and hie career in tlie Town Council. There w an old eillee which sAid that if a man was known anywhere it was where he lived. He lived in the midst of the electors who had conferred such an honour upon him that day, and no one knew better than they din what he was. If they were satisfied with their choice—and it wiesevirlent they were—be also was quite satisfied. The fight was over, the election won, and he could promise them that he could do hie duty faithfully. In pro- posing a vote of thanks to Alderman Roberts, the committee, officers, and other workers in the ward, Councillor Evans referred to the arduous work which had been undertaken by them,and also spoke of the ..,ervicee of Mrs. Roberta. (Applause.) Mr. HATAY, in seconding the resolution, said he dii not think they had had a better committee in Whitecrose ward ; every man had stood shoulder to shoulder, and fought nobly and well for the candi- date. (Applause.) They had all felt that they had a tree man in Mr. Evans and one who was an exponent of the views and desires ot the people in Whitecrose ward, and the community at large. The resolution was carried with enthusiasm' Alderman POnanTa said as chairman of the com- mittee he would like to acknowledge the valuable help that had ben given by gentlemen connected- with other parts of the borough. They, had come from their several districts at great inconvenience and loss of time and business, to rally to their sup- port in Whitecrcee ward in a manner most praise- worthy. He begged to return his best thanks to them for the handsome way they had eupported thorn. Whitecroes ward had always been ready to simi workers to other parts of the town, and that I help had hen reciprocated that day. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Twomes HAwrironx in acknowledging the resolution said he could add his testimony to the valuable work done by the Committee of Whitecross Ward that day. He had never seen them work harder. The extra efforts put forth by the Conser- vatives instead of damping the ardour of Mr.Evane's workers had spurred them on to greater eff ots,and they now saw the result. (Applause,) They knew they had a man whose character would stand the test of examination,and whose interests were not for self but for the good of the whole town. (Ap- plause.) There were calls for Councillor Plumpton.,. Alder- man Roberts explained that he' was not present, but he (Alderman Roberts) would like to say that Coun- cillor Piumpton had worked hard all day and he was personally very much indebted to him. The proceedings then terminated.</p>
      </div>
