      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. sourEry.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. sourEry.</p>
        <p>ANNUeL MEETING.</p>
        <p>At the meting of the Wycliffe P.S.A.. Society, on Sunday afternoon, the annual business was transacted when the reports were receivei, and the election of officers took pisce. The 11. iv. I. Youge presided,and he was accompanied within the c ommunion rails by Messrs. Thomas Wallington, B. Farrar,R. Atkinson, A. Lang,and R. MUM. THe LATE MR. GLADSTONE—. A VOTE Or 52111FATHT.</p>
        <p>On the prop3sition oi Mr. Crosskill, seconded oy Mr. Whetnall, a resolution of sympathy with Mrs. OLadatone and family, was passed by the members :standing. The Chairman's:allusion to the stateman's death will be found in another column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>REPORTS.</head>
        <p>THE REPORTS.</p>
        <p>The Cu/murex after stiting the business of the meet- ing said it was most undesirable that the management of the society should get into the hands of a small corn: pany of men. Flcibelleved in every man having a share in the management, and was most anxious that all the members should feel a growing interest !n its various matters and details so as to give them a personallaterest in.the</p>
        <p>. - Mr. THOHAi WALLINGTON said he had great pleasure in submitting the fifth annual report. Be th,ught they would agree with him that althoup the past year had not been brim full of encouraging signs of pro4ress and prosperity,yet it had been a year marked in many ways by degrees of success for which they should all be truly grateful, and for which fresh inspiration eh mid be gathered for a stronger effort being made to make the present year of the society redound with those successes which characterised it three years age. They were not as many prophesied five years ago, numbered among the many institutions whica only survived 12 months, and then decayed and ultimately died out of existence. They were a long way frem that unhappy posi- tion yet. and he was certain that all . present hoped that the time was far distant when the public of Warrington shoeld have heard the last reports of the do- ing of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. (Hear, hear.) The year closed with an enrolled reetribership of 935. Of this num er there were many who took their cards be t had no: made a single attendance during the year. The weekly average attendance for the year of members and non.rnembers had been 756, which included the attend- ance at all the open meetings. Eight hundred and eighty-eight b3oks had been gained as prizes during the year,4l4 for the first six months and 474 for the second. The total number of prizes dietribute,d since the cam. mencem eat of the society was 5,634. The work done by the members in introducing new members resulted in 64 being added in September last. (Hear, hear) The members of the committee had. ever been careful and vigilant in watching over the transctions of the society and helping on its highest welfare. Referrin4 to the absentee visitors work and that of the Benevolent Fund Cominittee'he stated tint these two branches of work were of paramount imp irtance,and unlees each of Miens received the assistance necessary to meet their 'lemands their !sewers of usefulness and help were crippled, and the society suffered generally. Those in charge of the work deserved their heartiest thanks for they had stuck loyally to their work in one MP, under the difficulties of a very limited band of visitors, and in the other. of dimini had pecuniary help. In oconclusi +a, Mr. Wallington said their thanks were due to all who had helped to make the meetings joyous and profitable. They had been helped by many earnest men, either as chair- wen or speakers, who had spoken brave and encouraging words which had greatly helped and stimulated them. They also thanked all who ha 1 placed their musical talents at th. Society's disposal, and every ome who had assisted to help on tho work in any way. They had had abundant seasons of great rejoicing during the year that had gone, an i there were also abundant signs that the present year would be asihappy ;and profitable as its predecessors. (Applause). Mr. RICHARD ATSINSON then presented the annual finenc;al statement, which showAl the receipts to be £127 61. 6 1., and the expenditure £l3l 15i. 61., leaving an adverse balance of £4 lie. Ha explained that a col- lection which was usually taken in the month of May had not been taken, and that accounted for the adverse balance. . . . .</p>
        <p>r.'iLt submitted a detailed statemont of the visits paid by tho Visiting Committee, from which it appeared that the Committee had attended t 0.1,031 oases of re- ported absentees. Mr. LANG gave an interesting rep )rt of tho work of the Benevolent Fund Cornmittqe. Re p ointed out that calls upon the fund had h.a, n very light during the year. The amount disbursed was £7 173 0.1, and there was still a balance in hand of £2.</p>
        <p>The reports were adopted, and the firmiciAl state- ments ordered to be printed on the proposition of the Chaim Ln, seconded by Mr. Jogeph Owen.</p>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The election of mifieers Wai thsa proceeded with, and resulted as follows :—President, Bev. J. Yong s ; gener secrettry, Mr. Thom ts Wallington ; astistant secrettry, Mr. B. Fsrrar; treasurer, Mr. R. Atkins 3u; re4istration gecretary, Mr. Alfred Knowles ; absentee visitors secre- tary, Mr. Richard Mille; litnevolitut ful 1 secretary, Mr. A. Lang; Benevolent fund treasurer, Mr. William Hick- man. A general committee was also appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>VOTES OF TH,LNKS.</head>
        <p>VOTES OF TH,LNKS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Guoaus Trio:grams in proposin;• that the best thanks of toe committee be given to the officers and friends for their work during the past year, said it had given him great pleasure to hear the report read. The society was very fortunate in having a president like the Rev. John Yonge. He did not know anyone who Wa4 mono fitted for the posirion. He also paid a tribute to the services of the other officers, and said,it was the least they could do to propose a hearty vote of thanks. The resolution was unan!mously carried, and the Rev. J Yonge, in responding, acid it bid been a great &amp;light to him to s,.rve the society in the pist. He was willing to give them his service in the future. The m-eting concluded with the singing of the Doxology.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>SAD BOATENG FATALITY AT</head>
        <p>SAD BOATENG FATALITY AT</p>
        <p>WA RRINGTON.</p>
        <p>About eight o'clock on Saturday night a dis- tressing boating fatality occurred on the Mersey at Warrington—or to be more particularly correct at Paddington—in which a young man named James Sankey,' clerk, 25 years of ageovas drowned. It appears that Thomas Finnigan, clerk of 26, slar- son-street, and James Sankey, clerk, of 174, Long- ford-street, hired a rowing skiff from Hr. Platt at Howley Quay,about eight o'clock. When they had proceed d about 500 yards. Sankey commenced " larking" and rocked the frail craft until it cap- sized. Both the occupants wets thrown into the river, and although he was an excellent swimmer. Sankey became exhausted,and Finnigan,after mak- ing a plucky attempt to rescue him, found it be- yond his power, and to save his own life had to make for the side. Information of the occurrence was immediately given, but it was not until half- past seven on Sunday morning that Sankey's body was recovered. Deceased was a clerk in the goods department of the Cheshire Lines Committee at Warrington. and was held in high respect by all who knew him.—The inquest was held on the body by Mr. Brighouse on Nlonday afternoon, at the Borough Police Station.—Deceased's father identified the body, and said he last saw him on Friday night,—Thomas Finnigan, deceased's companion, said that he aad deceased decided at eight o'clock on Saturday night to have a row on the Mersey, They hired a boat from Mr. Plw.t, and after they had gone about 500 yards Sankey commenced "rocking " the boat, and although witness asked him to desist he did not do so. The result was that the boat capsized, Finnigan attempted to rescue his companion, but after grab- bing him twice by the collar he had to let him go to save his own life. He had great difficulty in get- ting to the si le. He gave an alarm, and the bo i was recovered the following inorning.—George Platt owner of the boat, F.aid that Finnigan and Sankey hired an out-rigged boat, with single pair ot oars, on Saturday night for an hour. Toe boat was a heavy one. He always warned people about changing seats in the boats, which he had owned for eight years without having an accident. Fin- nigan informed him of the occurrence, and he re- covered the body at ten minutes to seven on Sun- • day morniug.—A verdict Of "Accidentally drowned" was returned, the Coroner expressing the sympathy of the jurymen and himself with the father upon the loss of his san.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <p>SYSTEMA.TIC SHOPLIFTING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>TWO GIRLS sEsr TO gngON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Tuesday, before Mr. Robert Garnett (in the chair), Councillor Shaer Green, Mr. Thomas Birchen, and Mr. E. J. Guest, Phcebe Clarke, (18). and Mary McGrath (21), were charged, Clarke with stealing three shawls valued at les from the shop of Edgar V. Morten, pawnbroker, Bridge Foot. on Saturday night, 'also a skirt and two silk hand- kerchiefs from the Co-operative Stores. Winwick-street, a scent bottle and silver fruit knife, frees the shop of Eustance' and Co., jewellers, Pankey-street : and a set of artificial teeth, the property of H. W. Griffin, Bewsey- street, and the girl McGrath with aiding and abbetting the thefte.--Prieoners pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable ,said the charges disclosed a, System of systematic shop robberies on the pare of the 'two prisoners. The circametances came to the know- , ledge of the police in this manner. It appeared that on Saturday night a number of shawls were banging on a , rail inside the shop of Mr. M . 3rton Bridge Foot. They , were seen by an assistant named Conway. and about ! an hour and a quarter later were missee by him, and in. 1 formation was given toehe police. inviries were made ! I and on Monday morning,Detectives Barlow and Meacock I visited a lodging-hense in Bettermerkst-itreet where the two prisoners were just preparing to leave with a large ! box. The officers examined the box,end found amongst a number of other articles,two of the shawls whice bad been stolen,two silk handkerchiefs, a skirt, and a scent bottle,and silver fruit knife. The scent bottle and fruit knife were identified as having been at den from the I shop of Messrs. Eustance and Co., jewellers, by Clarke, who one day went in to mike a small purchase. !There was also found a set of artificial teeth,whi'rdi belongedeo Mr. GrifEn,cd Dewily-street. The prisoner Clarke went to Mr. Griffins business place for some teeth, and the, stolen teeth were on the table in a waiting room. 539 had taken advantage of the opportunity of being left alone to steal the teeth. To prisoner McGrath had bele living with Clarke and acting in concert with her. At the time the officers visited the hoese,they want Into a room occupied by the prisoner McGrath, and caught her in the act of concealing one of the shawls under her clothing. The girls were brought to the Police Station and charged with the thefts. Clarke admitted the offence. The other prisoner said she only stood out- side the shape when the thefts were committed. That of course was the usual modus operandi of sheplifterce Both girls had been living a very sad life for some time. Clarke was a confirmed thief and shoplifter. In De- cember last.there were three charges against her of shop thefts. For them she was sent to prison for three months. one month on each charge. In May 1893 A conviction was recorded against her for robbery from the person. She had lived the life of a fallen woman since she had come out of goal. The prisoner McGrath was impri- soned for a manth in 1895 fsr vagrancy. The two girls were at present he was afraid, utterly beyond redemp- tion. In answer to the Chairman, the Chief Constable said Clarke had narents but they would have nothing to do with her. McGrath had a father in the Workhouse. The Chairman said the case was a very bad one and the prisoners did not seem to realize the position they were In. Toe judgment of the bench was that Clarke should be sent to prison for six months, with hard libour, two months on each charge, and McGrath for four months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>DRAUGHTS.</head>
        <p>DRAUGHTS.</p>
        <p>YORK3IIIRE v. LANCASHM.</p>
        <p>The sixth match between these counties took place on Saturday at Leeds. After a few preliminary remarks by the Yorkshire president (Mr J Uuodall, Mr F 11 Booth. of I an- cashire, offered to give any work on draughts to the winner nearest the top board who won both his games. This proved to be Mr W Blakelev,of Mancoeiter, who defeated Mr Kear in the resent English tournament. After air Frank Dunne, cm behalf of the Thxzllsh Association Committee, had presented Mr W Gardner with the board On which he won the English cham- pionship, play commenced. After several games had been adjudicated by alessrs W Gardner and F Dunne, the Yorkshire victory by 14 games to 7, and 16 draws, was an- nounced amidst great applause. This brings the records co the two counties to three wins each, the home county in every histatve having proved the victors. Score : YORKSHIRE. W. L ANCABHIRE. W. D. W Gardner (clot), Leeds 1 F Dunne (capt) W'gton 0 1 C Horst all, Halifax 0 A Cain, Liverpool 0 W J Wray, Halifax L Renton, Bradford Sunderland, Leeds ---------0 W Bakelev, Ilechester 2 0 J Bosworth, Leeds 0 T Atherton. Li,,erpool 0 F Womersiev. Bradford 2J W Weir, Manchester 0 0 S Willans, Keighley A Boothroyd, Wm.ndwike 1 T Bolen, Liverpool... 0 1 B Lord, Halifax W Gould, Middlesborough 0 3 H Holt. Bury 0 J Petty, Bradford H Wheater,Jfalifax</p>
        <p>_ II Brown, Keighley 7 Gooda11. Leeds it Hunter, Halifax 1 W Dyer, Liverpool ... 0 1 3 0 Moss, Leeds OW 11 Waller, Rochdale 0 2 T Goldshro, Bradford I Bratntner, Leeds 0 T Lightfoot, Liverpool 0 2 J Kenton, Bradford</p>
        <p>Total tone game adjudicated. *only played one game.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WA.RRL3UTON.</head>
        <p>WA.RRL3UTON.</p>
        <p>TIER ALLIGED AssetrLr ON A COUNTY COURT BAILIFF. . _ _</p>
        <p>At the Warrington i.);;;;:io-nal Ceurt on Tuesday before Mr. Claude Morris,Michaal Knight and Mar- garet Knight, of Legh-street, Earlestown, were brought up. The defendants were remanded on Wednesday, the 18th, from the Warrington County Petty Sess'ons ou a charge of assaulting Walter Newton,a Warrington bailiff, on the 13th May at Earlestown. Tney were n&lt;,w charged with doing malicious bodily harm to Walter Newton on the 13th May. Mr. Browne,for the proiecutor, said he would now ask for a remand as his client would be unable to appear for a week.—Dr. Bowden was called and said he had seen Mr. Newton that morn- ing. He would not be lit to lea .'e the house for a week. The two defendants were hound over in two sureties of .£25 each,to appear to answer the charge against them tt 7.henext_Cluntv Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>SJOELSIOR and NIVT HOWR CYCLII3.—C4Rimtn</p>
        <p>Ironmonger, VVarrington. TR&amp; WARRINGTON Vocurrnms AT THE PARISH</p>
        <p>CHunca</p>
        <p>The Warrington Volunteers attended divine er - vice,at the Parish Church on Sunday morning. They paraded at the Drill Hall at 930, and at ten left for the Parieh Church, headed by the regimental band, under the direction of Mr. Thomas Armstrong, and the Bugle band, under the direction ot Bugle- Major Plinston. The men, who numbered between 200 and 300, looked remarkably well, and the parade was witnessed by large crowds. The officers present were Colonel Ridgway (commanding), Major Buckton, Captain and Adjutant F. M. Roome, Captain J. D. Pairclough, Captain F. M. Appleton Csptain R. W. Baynes, Captain J. H. Ridgway, and Giptain J. A. Edelsten. A poropriate hymns were sling. accompanied by the Volunteer Band, and the service throughout was a very impressive one. This was the annual church parade prior to the week's annual training when they go into camp on Sunday next at Rhyl.—An advance party under the command of Captain Baynes, left by the 95 a.in. train from Bank Quay Station on Wednesday, for Rhyl, for the purpose of pitching the tents and making other preparations for the arrival of the main body of the battalion. Captain Baynes was accompanied by Captain B. Fairclough, and the following non-commissioned officers :—Colour-Ser- geant James Wilcock, Sergeant W. Taylor, Sergeant W. Dutton, Sergeant J. Millett, Lance-Sergeant Whitfield, and Armourer-Sergeant Davies. There were 22 privates. To-morrow (Sunday) morning the men will fall in at, ten o'clock, and proceed by special train at 11.20 to 1141,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CORN MARKET. There was a fair quintity offering at this market on Wednesday. New wheat (white) fetched 63 8d to 63 10d (70Ib to the bushel); red wheat 63 7d to to 6s 91; new oats. •3301t0 33 2d. Flour best seconds, per 240!13.3, 353 01 to 38s al, Bran, 101 to ls Od per 20Ib. NNW RAPID COLS.—GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street. Warrington. 'NWT/SST AT WINWICK ASYLUM. Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquest at the Winwick Hall Asylum,on Monday afternoon,touch- ing the death of Daniel Kelly, fitteen years of age, an inmate of the asylum, who was received from Whittingham Asylum on the 28th September last. Dr. J. GUPSL Gornall stated that he was a visiting medical officer of the 'asylum. The deceased was a complete idiot, and nothing could be gained from him. He was in the habit of eating anything he came across. He was taken ill at the beginning of last week with vomiting,but later on he improved. On Friday be was taken worse, and died suddenly in the evening. He made a post-mortem examina- tion on Saturday afterti3oll. There were no exter- nal marks of violence. Internally all the organs were healthy. except the brain, which was slightly congested. He found no foreign substance in the body of any description. Collapse was the imme- diate cause of death,but what Was the cause of the collapse he was quite unable to say.—The jury re- tirned a verdict in accordsnde with the medical evidence. •</p>
        <p>PRIIMIHR COLS, special terma.—GAntann, Agent Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL. The anniversary services of the Cairo-street Chspel OA place on Sunday. A children's service was held in the afternoon whm an interesting and instructive ad- dress was given by Mr. Samuel Ashworth. of Hyde. In the evesing an anthem by the choir "Suffer little child- ren to come unto me," was very ably rendered. The preacher was the Rev. J. E. Odgers, M.A., Hyde. After the sermon the reverend pteacher spoke in a very high manner of the late Mr.Gladstents. Personally he did net agree with Mr. Gladstone in politics, but he pointed out that he (Mr Gladstone) had raised the whole tone of pelitics since his entry into Parliament. At the conclu- sion of the servicl the hymn was PUIZ " I have fouxhe a good fight, I have finished my course." The organist then played the " Dead March" whilst the audience were standing. There were very good congregations at the different services, and the collections during the day realisNl £44. BUCKLEY STREET METHODIST FREE CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY. At the Sunday School anniversary of the above place ef worship on Sunday two sermons were preached by Mr. John Wright, of Glaz-burv, near Manchester. Suitable hymns and anthem.; were sung by the choir and great p-al se is due to Mr. J. U. Cooke, the leader of the choir for the manner in which the anthems were rendered, and Mr. Albert Trow, the organist, deserves the highest credit. Ia the afternoon the P con- nected with the school held a sacred concert when 1 several well known gentlemen gave their service,. istr. lAlbert Trow was the accompanist. Mr. Jahn Wright gave a short address the subject being" Sslf Respect." The chair was occupied by Mr. Alfred Scott.—On Mon- day evening Mr. John Wright gave a lecture on " presi- dent Garfield," whica was listened to by the audience ' with great attention. Mr. Arthur H Cresfield occupied the chair, but throne!' another engagement had to leave before thelecturer closed his address. A vets of thanks was p, ()posed to Mr. Joins Wright by Mr. Alfred Scott seconded by Me. Owen and the meeting was brought to an end. ' ST. JOHN'S PRESBYTERIAN CHURCH. The anniversary services were held at this church in Winwick-street on Sunday. The Rev. J. Warnock. S.D., preached in the morning. At the conclusion of his sermon the preacher made a touching reference to the death of Mr. Gladstone. The Rev. John H. Scott of Manchester occupied tho pulpit in the evening. There were good congregations and the collections during the day were satisfactory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>COMMON-SENSE DIET. BY A MEDICAL MAN.</head>
