      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 27 198</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, MAY 27 198</p>
        <p>Et?lterstr thr Mittor.</p>
        <p>TILE NSED OF TRAINI.M.* Lid.oUNDS AT WARRINGTON._ . .</p>
        <p>Sir,—There are raOt.;;;;-to-w-n-s-Where sports are held twice a year, where they have not got any training grounds. It is so, however, in our town. We are not now alto wed to train on the Football Field, and if we , want to run at all we have got to go in the country lanes (perhaps walk miles before we can get a suit able place), and then risk being caught by the police and fined. See- ing what good athletes we have had, and have got in Warrington, it is rather surprising to see a lot ot them standing:about with;their bags,not hnowing where to go. Surely in a town like ours we could have grounds like Wigan, Rochdale, and Broughton. At the last named place they charge a penny per night e let' person, and I think it pays very well. Cannot anyone in Warrington or just outside do this ? Or cannot the Corporation spe their way to make a training ground, at' one of the new parks, and charge 90 much a year P—Thanking you in anticipation, and apologizing for trouble.—Yours truly, RUNNER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>NOTES ON -OA AIES AN!)</head>
        <p>NOTES ON -OA AIES AN!)</p>
        <p>ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>It is psrhaps as well that the v. Nlrthern match was notcompleted on Saturday owing to the rain. It did not augur well for the final result for the local men, when two wickets were taken for four balls with no runs. The condition of the grouad was no doubt against batting. The professional was bowled of his pads and J. Callon followed a minute or so after him. Interested followers of the game, will feel it a pleasant surprise when Warrington began to play up to th ir form of two or three seasons ago. Probably a little new blond would do good, as there is plenty of room for improvement.</p>
        <p>A different method of choosing the second eleven would work advantageously in my opinion. What is to prevent the committee from securing some of the most promisinii members of our junior teams ? Tnere is no lack of promising players, and most of them would be only to glad to have their merits tested in the premier eleven it the method of selection was not on su..h exclusive lines. It is natural that we should look to the second eleven to feed the first eleven, and if the qualities of the second eleven are not up to the average, where are the vacancies to be filled up from ?</p>
        <p>Progression would be surer and sustained, if the corn mittee would be less conservative in choosing the mem" hers of the junior teams. Lt personal merit, and tot influence secure the vacancies. Good gates will con Untie to be a rarity if matches are not won, and a ern' perison between the present avail Able players and the list of fixtures does not suggest that many will be won.</p>
        <p>The match,Peter Walker:and Son v Runcorn on Satur- day was a very creditable victory for the Brea cry. Run- corn playing on their own ground were tot out for 45 runs. Runcorn made a poor show. Midge and A. Abr4ms respectively compiled the most runs for the horneatera. Arbor and E. Allen for the visitors were got out for 17 and 7, the only wickets which toll. 13Ailmv and Ashton b ;.ttei well for 22 and 18 not out. The total with extras made 65.</p>
        <p>The game llstween Cheshire Lines Engineers and Pad- gate on Saturday was not completed. The En :ineers %frit in and three wickets fell. Brown and Lawle43 scored 13 and 10 not out. Total for three wickets 54. At this pint the rain otopoe I the game. The match ty-• tween the second teams of these clubs was cancelled in cone:queue@ of the wet weather.</p>
        <p>We have received a copy of Brookfield's Cricket Annual for Liverpool and District which includes ChEs:er SoutliPott, St. Helen, Runcorn, Warrington, Widnes, and Wirral. It di a veritable rode mecum to all intermted in cricket, and may be obtained for the popular penny. The editor has arranged a competition in which he pre- s-nts photographs free of charge to the members of the team which wins the most matches between May 7th, and August 27th. I is interesting to note that last year's winners were the morph rs of the Sankey Wire Mills C.C., and a portrait of the team appsars in the handbook,</p>
        <p>St James' A, just managed to score a win azain st Hatton on We around of VIA latter Oa Saturday; of tit. total so^ra of 30 for the viaitors P. Knight compiled 21 not out.</p>
        <p>A win by 28 runs for Backley-street was the result of matm Buckley-street Sunday School v. Sankey Wire Mills (2nd Elevrn). Tile game took place on the ground of the latter. Jolley for Buckley-street took six wickets for eight runs.</p>
        <p>Warrington Co-operative just scored a vicory of one run over the S. Helens Co-operative on Tuesday at Warrington. Ford for the locals hit up 11 runs and Johl- son took eight wickets for nine runs, both of inestimable value to the winning side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>CYCLING.</head>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>The following is F. T. Bidlak3's opini In on georiaz : High gears for the strong, th 3 short-distanc , Inv]. th condenser of his energies into a small space of time ; low gears for the longer distance riders and for continua exertion. My own opinion is that 83 is the to..st go sr for persons of average strength for road work ; that oven the strongest need nothing above 70. except for racing ; and that 55 is a go xi gear for the average woman, whose strength is as a rule below toat of the average man.</p>
        <p>Cyclists are an ingenious lot. They are of scoring of the p.dice or other opponents of the wheel. There was an instance of this at the Altrinchaso Police Court last week, when is cyclist named Ernest Baum, Winton• road, Bowdon, was summonoo for riding a bicycle with- out having alight attached af:er hours. He was seen he a police officer in Wintoo-road. at 840 p.m. on April 3, and had then no light.—The defendant pointed out that he was within the hours. Tne inn set about 16 onintres later at Bowdon or Minchester than Greenwich, and that the more westerly and northerly a town was situated the later the sun set. It was impossinle for two places to h lye the same sunset owing to the isclinatim of the earth's ans.—The bstsch found there were no "cases" on the point, and dismissed the summons.</p>
        <p>Three hundred and sixty.seven miles in 201 hours. Averagn pace, 17irnie an hour. Wind, mud, add r.tin. Rivierre Ist, G siin 2nd, Rohl 3rd. Motor-cAr p wing no great success. Such 13 a s:kort resume M Ur., great annual Bordeaux-to-Paris race, run last Sunday.</p>
        <p>-- The easiest way of mounting a lady's hicycl3 13 to stand with the left foot on the ground in front of the Twdal, and place the right foot on the right-hand pedal, which should be in position slightly forward of the top of the stroke, and, having adjus'.ed your skirt so that it may fall evenly on either side of the frame, take a firm grip of both harylle?. Flavin,' put yourself in posi— tion, you tv?xt proceed to use the right hand pedal as a step by which you lift yourself on to the saddle ; the pressure thus ext'utmi ad y Jur will start the maceine, n left foot will drop naturally on tO the other Waal as it comes up.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT</p>
        <p>COUNCIL. At a meeting of the abavo, held in thy Bevraoy-stree: Chambers ou Werlneiclay. there aver,. preient Mr. J. Bail (chairman) M."Eliri. IV Knowlea,W. Houghton, Erookeq, J. Dignom. T. Halton, R. Forahaw, a9•l H. Thornton, with J. C. Sutton (clerk) Dr. S•phtonlrnadic ^rficer). F. Karfoot (inspector) and D. S. Stone for Mr. W. Owen (surveyor). _____</p>
        <p>FINANCIAL. rie CLERK reported hrrhe balanco in hand on the acc,unt. was £3lB 8, 2,1 and nn the Buttonwood Sewerage L')Atl account £1,046 19s 7d. MEDICAL OFFICERS REPORT. Thl MEDICAL OFFICER von ,te I 'hi'; during 01., meth the de ithq were eqdal to 15 p,r 1000 ayainet 136 the average ef the last 'bile monl.t..s. and 132 the cor- responom month of last year. The deaths from 7,7m0: ic diseases were nil per 1,000 against •6 the average of the last three m ml.hs And .8 the corresponding ni 'nth of 7r.nr. Daring the month 12 cases of sc udet fever had barn rcp.;rted an th.y were thought tn be CARS° by the epidemic at Nowehurch. There was alio one death cluaed by puerperal f..ver.—Tho reportorial that: of the Inspector was adopted. RE-APPOINTMENT OF MEDICAL OFFICER AND INSPECTOR The CHAIRMAN moved that Dr. Sldatori bo Ti'- appointed. Mr. D;gnuin seconded and it was carrittd unanimously. The CLERIC then read to the meeting a letter nf appli- cation from Mr. K.rfoot for an increase of salary. Asked how lone Mr. Kerfont had been inspector, tri Clark said since 1888 Ells original salary was £3O, and it had been inmeased at various limes. It was now £llO. It was pointed out that there was a difference oh £2O between Mr. get fres present salary and tits prede- ecessor's, nut in the case of his pre lecesaor that salary had been reached by gradual increases. Mr. Frounirrox moved the re-appointment of Mr. IK,,r- -foot RS inspector at an increase of salary of £lO. H. had been in their service for eleven yeare,and they could not have R more efficient man.—Mr. Dignum seconded the resolution, Mr. Thornton supported, and it was carried. This was all the husines.</p>
        <p>FOR ATHLETES, AND ALL OTHERS interes,, d Milli:tic:4, cycling, gulf, tennis, riding, sparring, or any sport, • ty,tu With CTricurre.ScaP is 4,n0 of tho luxuries of modern c,vWzation. It eleanses,purifies, and beauti. fies tne thin, ecalp, and hair, soothes and prevents chaf- imt, ito!ir,, irritation, and influnmatien, and when hv a gentle annointinff with CCTICURA, pidrest and ..scp.(e,t of emolients, proves most grateful in re- lieving and s!rengthening tired, lamed, strained, and pam'ul =Kies.</p>
