      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>FARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>FARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>VULCAN WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCIOOL.</p>
        <p>In all of the tends of the above school, special srrices were preached on Sunday afternoon and evening in the -Vulcan Wesleyan Meeting.room. Sermons were preached by the ;Lev. G. Lnrin, and special antherw and hymns were sung by the c'aoir and children.</p>
        <p>EARLESTONvIst MAY QUEEN. In consequanca of the very inclement state of the weather, on Saturday, this festival which should have been held oa that day hid to ho post- poned, much to the disappointment of hundreds of children. Given a fine day the festival will be held to-morrow (Saturday). SUNDAY SCHOOL PROCESSION.</p>
        <p>The annual Whit Monday demonstration in con- iwtion with the various dissenting Sunday schools will take place on Monday nest. The schools will assemble on the Market ground at 10 o'clock, and after singing several hymns will parade the town in procession in the following order :—Sutton-road prize band, Welsh Wesleyans, Primitive Methodists, Parr Temperance prize hand, E%rlestown Wesleymis Salvation Army and Baptists, On this occasion the Wesleyan Sunday School will display its new silk banner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>HOPE OP EIRLESTOWN LODGE 1.0.G.T.</head>
        <p>HOPE OP EIRLESTOWN LODGE 1.0.G.T.</p>
        <p>The above society held a successful gathering in the Co-operative Hall, EarlAstown,ort Monday even- ing, presided over by C.T, Bro. J. Taylor, and there was a large attendance, Bro. Woods gave a report on the Grand C.T Circular, stating that there was an increase of 2,712 adult, and 1,206 juvenile rnem- bars, giving a tetal membership of 57,512 in 1,371 lodges, and of 52.367 in 703 juvenile Temples giv- ing a grand total of 109, 879 members in 2,074 branches,urging all lodges and Temples not to close during the summer months. Bro. J. Warburton gave the report of the picnic which showed a sue- cess,and thanks were accorded to the committee,and it was arranged to visit the Ilaydock Lodge, and give an entertainment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>EARLESTOWN CONdERVATIVE CLUB.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN CONdERVATIVE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the tnembers of the above club was held in the billiard room of the club on Tuesday evening. Mr. T. J. Gillespie presided, and in opening the proceedings he referred to the death of Mr. Gladstone,and paid a glewing tribute to his character. The committee's report and balance sheet were unanimously adopted, the financial statement showing the club to be in a prosperous condition. Eight vacancies on the committee were filled by the appointment o the following : Messrs. George Jones, W. A. Mawdsley, J. Catterall, J. Howe, James Smith, W. Pilling, Joseph Grey,and T. Holliday. Mr. Gillespie was heartily thanked for past services as president and again elected to the office. Mr. J. W. Thorpe was re-elected treasurer, Mr. T. littselden secretary, and Messrs. Ma wdsley and ilevve,auditors. Ok the proposition of Mr. R. Stuart it was decided that it be an in- struction to the committee to invite competition for the Legh Billlard Challenge Cup. A vote of thanks to the chairman terminated the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>MARLESTOWN BAPTIST SUNDAY SCHJOL.</head>
        <p>MARLESTOWN BAPTIST SUNDAY SCHJOL.</p>
        <p>Special services in connection with the annivers- ary of above school was held in the chapel on Sun- day. The pulpit was occupied by the Rev. A. Tildesley, of Sunnyside, Rossendale, who at the morning service chose for his subject "An unwel- come visitor," and in the evening " Fire Baptism." At the morning service the anthem l'Hoiness be- cometh Thy house for ever," was sung, and at the evening service "0 come, let us worship"the solo in which was sweetly rendered by Miss Jones. Special hyrpns were also sung by the choir and children. In the at ternoon the cantata "A day of Praise" was very efficiently rendered by the choir, the soloists being Messrs.T.A.Limand,Peet,Godbert,A.Limond,K. Limond, Roberts, mime, Misses E. Partington, L Holt, M. Fail, Grissold, Id. Partington, E. Wright, Drummond, &amp;c. There were other selections by individual members of the choir. Mr. Harvey con- ducted, and Mrs. Harvey presided at the piano. and Mr. T. A. Limond at the organ The collections throughout the day amounted to £33. ST. Joust's CHURCH, EARLESTOWN. Special services in aid of the Distr.,ct Day Schools were held in St. John's Church, Earlestown, on Sun- day. At the morning service the sermon was preached by the Rev. Morley Stevenson (Warring- ton), who during his address referred in touching terms to the loss sustained by the country by the death of Mr. Gladstone. In the afternoon an organ recital given by Mr. Henry W. Radford, organist and choirmoster, assisted by Mr. J. H. Peplow (violinist), and vocal items were given by the school children. After the opening hymn the organist played the " Dead March " in Saul, and the follow- ing programme was afterwards rendered :—" Organ Cone,-,rto " No. 2 in B flat; violin solo " Canzona," Ms. J. 11. Peplow ; organ, " Romance Affetnoso " ; anthem Tn. Lsrd is nay Shepherd " (children) ; organ " The Pilgrim's Song of Hope," " Introduc- tions and variations on a Theme " violin solo, " Cavatins," Mr. J. H. Peplow ; organ " March Nlilitaire " ; and after the prayer and Benediction the congregation sang the " National Anthem."— At the evening Pe rvice the sermon was preached by this Rev. B. W. Whittecbury-Kaye, rector of New- church. The collections throughout the day amounted to over £7.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>ST, HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST, HELENS.</p>
        <p>ASSAIILTV:76 A WIFH AT ST._ HELENS.</p>
        <p>Edward Bate, ziassmaker, of 148, R aglan-street, was fined 10s. and costa• f4r assaulting his wife, who was rant-,I a separation order and 101. a week alimony. The parties had been separated before.</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT AT A ST. FIBLIIN9 MINERAL WATER MANUFACTORY.</p>
        <p>About fivo on Tuesday afternoon a marri d man named John Woods who worked for Mr. W. Smith, minf,ral water manufacturer. George-street was coming up the cellar in the yard with a box of mineral waters on his back, when h 3 slipped and fell down the stairs. Dr. Jackson was sent for, and he found that his back was crush A. He was taken to Providence Hospital. ' EXPD9O7O A ST. BELTN9 FTSTSR PATIRNT.</p>
        <p>Mary Ashcroft, a-marrie-d-W-onia-r;:;r13:0"-r-r-e-1-strest, was charged at S. Helens on Monday witn exposing a scarleN, fever p +tient in the public streets. Chief Con- stable Wood 9,:Atd that Mrs. Ashcroft had two of her children ill with scarlet fever. The children were never properly ioolated, and on May 9.11 Inspector Costes found Robert, aged eight, who was in a very infectious condition, playing in the streets.—Dr. Harris, medical offi,:or, proved the offence.—Mrs. Ashcroft said it was oniv four days short of the six weeks when the boy was caught in the. street. She had eight of a family,and had to do everything herself. Sne had only gone to the baker when the boy ran out„—The Chairman said that the Fa nirAry regulations must be obeyed. She would be fined 104 lFiitiu.ll cests.—lrs. A.4nrolt (bursting out crvin) : Well, you will have to take me, because my master is out of work,</p>
        <p>INCIIICASE OF TIIE4 HIGHWAY Rare AT ST.IIELENS. Ti- ',inane() Gialalittea ,Jf the St. Helena Corporation Werio'silav aftertmon held their annual meeting for the consideratinn cif the estiinates of the several corn- mii:tees, sod to determine the amount of rates to be !late d !luring the Prl4lling. year. Toe estimate of the Hhzhwav Gamnittoe, it was found, indicated that the comtn'ttee .anticipated a ziteiatly increased expenditure on tla, maintenance and repair of the roads. Conse- quen ly the highway rate had to be increased by Id. The committee -fixed the rates as fnll9,VB :—HighlraF rate, 91 iri tine pound ; and the borough rate 2i 7d. The "'ers h i I isid the DJOr rate at Is in the pound, the total rates of the b irough for all purposes will be 4s 4110 the pound, against 4i 3d last year. INuErmsr ox A Sr. 11RLENS BOY. Mr. S. flrienoir-e, county coroner, held an inquest en NI-MdaV ork :JO body of Johre Pickett, aged four years, son of Henry Plekett, a labourer, of 7, Napier-street, whl afternoon from injuries received on. Friday afternoott.—Eliztb2th Pickett. the mother, s'ld 811'3 saw her son !idiom a quarter to four on Friday afternoon, Waling in the street with other children. About a qaartor of an hour later he earns running to the don! crying, and sail " Its broken," and pointed to his stomach. She sent for Dr. Dsw, who attended him. Be- fore he died he said he had fallen out of a waggon in the entry. The child died between twelve and one on Satur- day—Dr. J. H. D-icv said there were no external signs of injaries,snd no signs on the side where he complained of tno pain. flo was suffering from concussion of the brain. He died from some injury combined with his weak state, The coroner and jury exonerated the boys WI)) were paying with the deceased from all blame,and a verdict of t• Accidental death " was returned.</p>
        <p>TOorslay wag thn 31st anniversary ,f the birth of the Dtrhass nt Y.)rk. that evpnt having taken place at Ken- ein2lon Palace on May 26.1857. 13t.r marriaze with tne Om Yark took place at St. James's Palace on July 6 1893</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <p>NOTES BY TITS WAY .AT EA IILESTOWN. I [Br AN OCCASIONAL CORRIeSPONDENT.I It was most unfortunate for those who had charge of the arrangements for the May Queen festival, that the weather last Saturday was so very inclement. Everything foreshadowed a successful result, and there cannot be any doubt that the en- forced postponement means a considerable loss to the Committee. For such an affair as this every- thing depends upon the weather, and should it be anything but bright and warm, the out of-door celebrations must necessarily suffer. Much dis- appointment and inconvenience was caused by the postponement, but the Committee cannot be held responsible for this, as they did the very best thing possible in the circumstances. Given fine weather the festival will take place to-morrow.</p>
        <p>On every band the expressions of sympathy and regret at the death of Mr. Gladstone are general, and on Sunday several of the ministers at the various places of worship paid tributes of reverence to his memory. There were exceptions, and in one or two places not a single reference was made, and there are some who ascribe the omission not to for- getfulness, but to political feeling, if such be the reason it is a pity when in the face of a nation's calamity, for such Mr. Gladstone'e death must be considered, that all party prejudices, cannot for. a time be sunk and in unison with the majority honour done to the memory of the greatest states- man of the century.</p>
        <p>The storm of thunder and lightning which broke over the district on Sunday night caused many people a great amount of inconvenience and alarm. Luckily those who had attended divine worship were safely sheltered during the deluge of rain, but many who had taken advantage of the pleasant evening to enjoy a stroll in the country,wished they had obsyecl the command to " keep the Sabbath day holy." As usaul when there is an unusually heavy fall of rain, the roadway and footpath under Wargrave-road bridge were flooded to a depth of several inches, and as a consequence residents of Wargrave and oth3rs had to retrace their steps or climb the railway embankment in order to react dry land. I have on several occasions had to draw attention to this state of things but at last I hear the Council intend doing something to remedy the evil. Of course it is not wise to burry over such matters,but they might be expedited if some prominent member of the Council got stranded some night by the flooding of the bridge.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PA !USTI CHURCH SUNDAY</p>
        <p>SCHOOL. UNFURLING A NEW BANNER.</p>
        <p>_ On WednQsday evening an interesting c,remony was performed in the National Schoolroom, Church-street, when a new banner, for use in the Whitsuntide annual parade of the scholars of this school, wao formally un- furled by Mr. E. Irnison. There was a fair attendance of the Sunday School teachers and scholars, and great interest was evinced in the proceedings. After the sing- ing of a hymn the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, M.A. (vicar of Runcorn) called upon Mr E. Irnison to unfurl the banner, a duty which Mr. Iruison successfully per- formed. The banner proved to be a most exquisite work of art, and excited the highest admiration on all hands. It is about BC. square and suspeaded from a brass mounted cross-piece, which is attached to two beautifully finished carrying poles, surmounted by brass spear-heeds. In the centre of the front of the banner was depicted an admirable picture of the world renowned statue of Christ, by the Danish sculptor TtlOrWalti Sea, and is possibly the only banner in the United Kingdom that possesses such a picture. This is on a circular ground herdered by a wide gold hem and enclosed in a pretty fresco w.:.ak of red on blue silk, which in turn is surrounded by a wide border of rich old gold coloured silk. The centre-piece on the reverse side is a faithful portrait of the Runcorn Perish Churcri as it stands at present, and is a really creditable tribute to the banner-maker's art, and one in which the artist has excelled himself. On the front and obverse sides of the banner are the respective inscriptions " Runcorn Parish Church Sunday. School " and " Rancorn Parisn Church, consecrated (circa) A.D. 917," Thera is a deep fringe at the bottom of the banner which sets it efT in an effective manner. The designs are the work of the local committee, and the banner was prepared by the well-known firm of Mr. George Tutil, 83, City-road Lon- don.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Canon Maitland Wood said he should like to say a few words about the banner which was the moat beautiful banner he had ever seen. Though the banner itself was not made in Runcorn he was pleased to be able to say it had designed and thought out in Runcern. Referring to the selection of the subject fer the front of the banner,he said that when some time ago he wee travelling about the continent he secured a copy of a photograph of the ramous statue by the great D uaish sculptor Thorwald Sea, and it was when they were look- ing for a suitable subject for the front of the banner that he bethought of the photo he had,and the commit- tee decided it wee just the thing. On the original et atm) however, there WM no inscription at the base, but they hid added " Come unto me," as appropriate inasmuch as the banner was to be taken as a rally- ing point. With regard to the picture of the Parish Church they could see for themselves how well the artist had done his work. In fixing the date of the first consecration of the Parish Church they had had some difficulty, but had added the word circa which meant "about," thong they' were sure that they had not been far from the exact date in doing so. lie did not mean that that church as they knew it at prei- cot had remained the same all that time, but upon the same spot there had stood at different times, four churches. Possibly the first building was of wood and plaster, which in turn had been replaced by something more subst,ntial, but they were convinced that they could claim for the church in Runcorn that it , had a history of close upon 1,000 years. He wanted them to bear in mind that the par- ish church was a symbol of the parish people. I Great credit was due to the oemmittee for carrying out the work so successfully, and it also showed what a great interest the old scholars took in the Sunday school. He asked them to show their appreciation of the work done by giving Messrs. Imison, J. Littler, Dudley, and fey, a round of hearty applause,—whlch was duly accorded. Mr. unison said he unfurled the banner with same misgivings as he did not know bow to feel on an occasion like that. It was a matter to congratulate one another upon, as the response made to the appeal of the commit- tee was such an encouraging one. He hoped that the banner would recall to th-m the days they had spent within the school walls, lemming the lessons that made them become good men and women. He also hoped that Monday would be a fine day so that the whole world mieht look upon the beautiful banner. (Laughter and applause). The singing of the hymn " Brightly gleams our benmer " and the hiseedic-,ion prop-Juiced oy the vicar, brought the interesting ceremony te a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The timid fortnighdy meetinv of the above Board was held at Dut•ton Workhouse on Tuesday last, Mr.J.Stokes presidinii. There were also present the Rec. Dr. Gastiliti, Messrs. S. fat hr J.P. T.T.zsa, A. R. N 'man, and the membriri of the Rural District Council ; Mr.J. Ashton (clerk),hnd Mr. (*.Ashton (deputy- clerk),</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT.</p>
        <p>At this court on WednestiriTy-b-71-oreTl;.-FTA.Fr3st, Edward Holland, of 6t. Delens, was fined ss. and costs for having been drunk at Woolston on Tues- day. P.C. Bellinger proved the case.—John Don- aghy,who described himself as a barber and said he came from Chester, was charged with having been drunk at Rixton on Tuesday. P.C. Clarke proved the case. Prisoner was discharged on promising to leave the town. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT Ifoigfrla Somas'.</p>
        <p>This eociety held their second old-bird race of the season on Saturday, from Swindon, a distance of 132 miles, Twenty-five members sent 419 birds, which were liberated by Mr. J. Buckley at two p m. with tbe wind blowing in a southerly direction. Scores Lamb 852, H. F. Greenhough 756, P. Lester, 753, J. Edgelev 743, H. Clare 740, H. Mostyn 710, J. Edgeley 676, J. Clare 672, Miller 667, and G. Moncrieff 638. Owing to the disastrous weather only ten birds returned on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>TRU rINANCILL STATEMEN:r.</head>
        <p>TRU rINANCILL STATEMEN:r.</p>
        <p>The financial statement submitted showed that the b4lance in hand on the 10ch of May was £2,714 43 receipts £1,328 153. 101.. makinv a total of £4043 0..21. The payments included that intere•t and instalment of the loan on the workhouse of £B7 61. 21., amounted to £222 6s.2l,leaving a balance in band of £4,044 4s. 81. Tne pr,neet for the county rate was ah folio w county rat" £2.465; 10s. 7. constabulary general exn-editure, £224 2+.ld ; clnerabulary local expenditure, £719 5 i.B I. The prPcmt last year was county rate, £2,017; con• stabulary gener4l expenditure, £112; constabulary local expenditure, £715. The bridge rate 5r..., £2O 0.3.91. Tiv total moue,' called for was £3,439 53. is. as against £2,935133. 61.1ast half year.—tilr.vito,d ward mov"cl the adoption of the minutes, which was seconded by Mr. Wright and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>TUE 114D17STRIAL TRAINIM,</head>
        <p>TUE 114D17STRIAL TRAINIM,</p>
        <p>The Clerk read a letter from the L &gt;cal Olvernment Board sanctioning ,the appointment of John Mandl.rs. as industrial trainer at Dutton Workhouse at a salary rd £25, with allowance for rations and clothing as the Guardians of the Board thousht Norman alive notice of motion that he would move at the noat meet- ing of the Board that the salary of the iadustrial trainer be increased by £5.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>TIIE LADIES VISITING COMMITTEE,</head>
        <p>TIIE LADIES VISITING COMMITTEE,</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward, in accordance with his notice of motion, moved that Miss Anderson, of Runcorn, ho elected on the Ladies Visiting Committee to fill the vacancy caused by the resignation of MiSti MaiiinStln, of Froclsham.—Mr. T. Buster sec -Tided the proposition, which was carried unanimously.—The report ot the Ladies Visiting Committee contained n aliusion to the great loss the committee susrained by Miss Msllinson's resignation, and also a minute rnording appreciation of her high sgrvices whilst acting as sectotar7 on tbs corn- , mittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>NURSES AND THEIR MATHES.</head>
        <p>THE NURSES AND THEIR MATHES.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. R. Norman said it seemed that it was his duty to move in the matter of raising salaries all round. (Laughter.) Be now proposed to increase the salaries t oie two nu:ses. This was in truth a saving to the Board of a salary that hitherto went to a third nurse. It was not being done without an understanding witb the nurses who were willing to do night work and were agrceablo to the term; that the salary, which was now £25 per annum be increased by £1 per annum until the sum of £3O per annum was reached.—This was unanim• onsly agreed to.</p>
        <p>INDUSTRIAL TRAINER TOR THE GIRLS. On the S,lggNstiou of Mr. Norman it was decided to defer the question of an industrial trainer for the girls until the next meting ef the Board. • This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <p>CYCLING and DAT ANC! ROAD mAes.-7 IHN 8. Gal. EN. Slat it):10:, se., 34 Bridge-street, Warrington</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>SPIRITUALIST SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>THE SPIRITUALIST SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>At the usual meetings on .`unday and Monday Mrs Porter,of Patricroft,was the speaker. She delivered two addresses on the Suuday,which made good im- pressions upon the audiences.especially in the even- ing,when the subject was," Can man by searching find out God ?" At each service she gave clairvoy- ance. On Monday evening.after a short address on "Is Spiritualism of the Devil or of God ?" the re- mainder of the evening was occupied with pyscho- metric delineations. THE P.S.A. AT THE Y.M.C. , ROOMS The Lecture Hall was crowded at the usual P.S.A. meetiag on Sunday afternoon. Quarter- ma.stPr Sergeant Roberts, who was in the chair, gave an interesting speec'i on " Nobility of Charac- ter." In consequence of a disappointment, the Presi- dent (Mr. William rfickman), gave an impremptu address on the :-,hree " WV—watch, work, and war, which was listened to with great attention. Miss Lavvton was the vocalist, and tastefully rendered two solos. Mr. Clough, Mr. Taker, and the Secretary took part in the meeting, and at the close the or- ganist, Mr. G. B. Belford, played the " Dead March" as a token of respect to the late Mr. Gladstone the whole of the members standing with bowed heads.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>OHMY'S CIRCUS.</head>
        <p>OHMY'S CIRCUS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Ohmy,who ii an old fs-;;;;:i.te in Warrington, has returned to the scene o his former triumphs the circus in Queeen-street. Although the com- pauy only arrived in the town the previous Wednesday, thanks to the hard work of Mr. Ohmy and his staff, the interior of the building has been decorated, and plants laid about the stalls. The whole produced a most pleasing effect on Monday evening, when the first performance was given. There was a large audience which included the Mayor (Alderman J. E. Wright), Councillors Smethurst, Woods, and Wilkinson. Punctually at half-past seven the band struck up the overture,the leader being Captain Norman, and shortly after- wards the programme which was a lengthy and varied one, was proceeded with. Miss Ohmy, a daughter of the proprietor, is a very clever eques- trienne,and Miss Allen won applause for her skilful handling of her performing horse, while Mdlle. Wentworth, described as Bernum'sgreat lady rider, who left 'the greatest show on earth "on the ter- mination of the Liverpool engagement on Saturday, is an accomplished rider. She was not, however, accustomed to the ring, which was somewhat smaller than she has been in the habit of using and this accounted for several falls. She fully atoned for this, however, by the last part of her per- formance, which was very daring and clever. Miss Frederica earned applause with her troupe of performing dogs. Another skilful performer on horseback is Claude ()limy, who is more daring than ever, and who also appeared as an English jockey. Mr. Obrey's two performing ponies, Douglas and Blackpool, delighted the audience, and wonderful exhibitions of skill were given by the" Human White Snake" (who is able to twist his body into almost any shape), by the Olmar troupe of acro- bats, by Mons. St. Clair in his high trapeze perform- ance, by the Leotard Brothers in a tumbling act, and by the Cardeuo troupe of bar performers. Other novelties were the riding dog Billy and the comic donkey race. Ihere was a large number of clowns, and they kept the audience intensely amused throughout the evening. Some new " business " was introduced, the rocket scene being particularly effective. Special praise is due to Tony and Crukk- shanks, the latter being described as "the prince of all clowns." The entertainment is one which should commend itself to all, and no one will regret a visit during Mr. Ohney's stay in Warrington. A ' change of programme will take place weekly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1898-05-27_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>PRESENTATION TO THE REY. S. J. FLEmING</head>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO THE REY. S. J. FLEmING</p>
        <p>On Monday evening several members of the con- gregation and friends of St. Peter's Church assem- bled in the Club Room,John-street,to witness a pre- sentation to the curate (Rev. S. J. Fleming), on his 11 removal from St. Peter's perish, of a handsomely fitted dressing bag, and a marble and bronze clock, bearing the inscription :—" Presented to the Rev.S. J. Fleming by the parishioners of St. Peter's Church, Warrington,May 1898." He was also presented with an umbrella from the Sunday school teachers, and a silver pencil case and pen from the members of the Band of llope,as a token of regard and esteem after 2 years work in the parish. The rev, gentleman will shortly take up duties at St.Petere,trairfield,Buxton. —Mr. J. Berry,who presided,called on Mr. Hands to make the presentation.—Mr. Hands expressed his regret that the Vicar was unable to be present to occupy- the position which he (the speaker) had I been called upon to fill. He was not afraid to ask Mr. Fleming to receive the beautiful articles. He was thankful for the generous way the parish- ioners had subscribed to the presentation. He thought Mr Fleming was well worthy of the articles they were presenting to him, for they were giving honour to whom honour was due. (Applause.) lie asked Mr. Fleming, in the name of the parish- ioners, to accept the timepiece and bag. (Applause.) Mr. H. Taylor, P.C.M., said the children of the Band of Hope did not like to allow Mr. Fleming to leave Warrington without showing some regard and esteem for him. The Band of Hope were losing a good friend, and he said that on behalf of the teachers and children. They could not have anyone who took greater interest in the children, and he asked Mr. Fleming to accept the gift on behalf of the Band of Hope. (Applause.)—Mr. Williams . presenting the umbrella from the Sunday school teachers,said they would all feel his loss very much.l They were losing a good worker. (Hear, hear.)— Mr. Brierley said that personally he would feel the loss of Mr. Fleming, whom he had always found to be straightforward— in spiritual and in temperance matters. He did not know one individual who did not speak highly of Mr. Fleming. (Applause). He bad done his 'level best," not only f.r the parish but for the town.—The Rev. S. J. Fleming said be felt un- able to acknowledge their kindness. He was sorry the Vicar was unable to be present, but he trusted that his good wishes might to some extent go with him where he was going. He was also sorry that Mr Roberts was unable to be present, because be had done a great deal for St. Peter's Church, and the officials of the church and he (the speaker) had always been on friendly terms.. He took great interest in the Band of Hope work as he had seen the evils of intemperance. He thanked the Sunday school teachers for their kindness. In concluding, he said he would not forget them and asked them to accept his hearty thanks for their kindness, and also for their prayers. (Applause). Votes of thanks were passed to the Chairman for presiding,and after the hymn" God be with you till we meet again ' had been sung, cheers were raised for Mr. Fleming, and for the Parish of St. Peter's.</p>
      </div>
