        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>THE WAIL</p>
        <p>News is still very scant wi h regaul to War operation!. The tone of the telegrams this morning is far less pos.,' tive than it was yesterday. On Wednesday everyone la America had persuaded himself that the Spanish fleet had been sealed up in Santiago. Yesterday the blockade was a pious belief rather than an ascertained fact, The Spanish fleet was probably securely caught in Santiago harbour ; it might be inferred from the Madrid tele- grams that it was still in Santiago ; and it had Bet been seen elsewhere. In other words, it is all certain as anything can be that Admiral SchleV haa not seen the Spanish flvt nor obtained any conclusive information as to its whereabouts. Nor are the movements of the American fleets very clear. The only ships that are certainly off tne south clear, are those of Admiral Schley's rquadron. Admiral Sampson is at present off the noith coast of Cuba, while Admiral Schley is off tho south coast. This movement makes it pretty certain that no attempt is to be made to force the passage into Santiago harbour and bring the Spanish fleet, said to he lying there, to an engagement. ANOTHER FORT SEIELLED.</p>
        <p>The' Heraldo " publishes a telegram from Efavana of yesterday's date stating that some of the American war- ships shelled Fort San Hilario, sluated three miles front the entrance to the Port of Nuevitas. ()then of the enemy's ship reconnoitrM the eutrenco to CartleoaS Bay,with the intention of ascertaining whother any tor- pedoes had beea laid, and of eventually forcing, an en- trance. 'the inhabitants of Cardenas are fortifying the place strongly.</p>
        <p>'THE FRANCEISE IN JAPAN. YOKOHAMA, THURSDAY. The Premier yesterday introduced iu the Janauesa Diet a bill intended to lower the standard of the fran- chise, and thereby increasing the number of voters five- fold. It is also propos-d that the m Tnbership of the Lower House be increased from 300 to 472. TuE SIERRA LEONE TROUsLES. FREETOWN. SIERRA LEONE, THURSDAY. A naval brigade landed this noorning,and after a short route march returned on board. The demonstration pr 4.. duced a good effect on the natives. The troops wider L'eutenant Colonel Cunningham have arrived at Banda- , jrimah. Captain Carr, the district commissioner, who with his small force Was hemmed in in the Upper Mendi district. is now reported safe. The C,alabar arrived from Sherbro this morning with fugitives and three wounded man of the West India Bevirn,mt. Captain Cave Brown Cave, who was wou aclecl in the eneouuter the in. Furgents, WES brought down from KuellA on Fri lay 'Asc. Ho is doin,z, well and will leave for England with Major Tarbet on Saturday next SINKING OF A BRITI3II INDIA STEAMER. OVER FIFTY LIVES' LOSE.</p>
        <p>CALCUTTA, THURSDAY. The British India Company's s7eamship LinOuta while on the voyage to Rangoon broke her sh itt,and was takea in tow on Monday by the same Cornpany's steamer Mecca. Oa Tuesday the hawser parted,and during the mateceuvriog which followed the Mee.la erosoel the bows of the Lindula. A collision 2n6uM,:ind the Mecca sank. Her captain, two engineers, and atuut fifty others were drowned. BRIGHAM YOUNG'S SON IN TUE BANKRUPTCY</p>
        <p>--- COURT. In the London Bankruptcy Court yesterday a receiv- ing order was made against John Willard young, des- cribed as of Sydney-street, South Kensington. The petitioning creditors are the Trustees. Executors, and Securities Insusance Corporation, Limited, and the amount of the debt is £333,000, being upon final judg- ment for money lent and advanced, ii respect of which they hold as security shares valuei at £l,OOO. The debtor, a son of the late Brigham Young, the Mormon, was a railway contractor. MR. GLADSTONE. A vast concours- of persona anxious to pay a last tribute of reneect to Mr. Gladstone paaried through West- minster Hall yesterday. From six in the morning until eight at night tho stream fl iwed on unbroken, and it is estimated by one authority that 170,000 people walked past the simple catafalque on which the body lies. To- day it is anticipated that an almoat equ thy large num- ber of persons will visit the flail. Mrs Gladstone is re- ported to be in fairly good health, though sornewoat exhausted by the trying ordeal of the last week. Accompanied by the other members of no family who have remained at Flawardan, she will travel to London by special train to-day.____</p>
        <p>CRICKET. Lancashire in the opening day of the match with Wart, wickshire at Aigburth yrsterday did nt fare very well. The visitors went in first and scored 178, end Lancsahire; then lost seven wickets for 67. All day, hewever, were hard to obtain, as the wicket had not fully re" covered from the effects of recent rains. Mold's bowling was very successful ; he took five wickets for 20' Surrey and Gloucestershire began a match at the 1 Gloucestershire were bating for most of the day, ! thanks largely to a stand at the nnd of the inniags ! Ilale and March, they scored 205. Lock wood took wickets for 56. Surrey had alieut half an hour's bat and 41 were put on without loss. Leicestershire against the M.C.C. scored 185, nod rhe Club made 125 for six wickets. Somerset, playing ag4inst Oxford University, made 193, and th 9 University put on 149 f u five wickets. Oarnbridpe University met an elev-n captained bY A. J. Webber at Cana bridgn. [he visitors scored 113ur and the Light Blues made 163 for three wickets. No play was possible, unfortunately, at Southampton yesterday in the Hants and Yorkshire match, which is for the benefit of H. Ilald win. THE COAL STRIKE —PROBABLE EARLY SETTLEMENT.</p>
        <p>At a conference of colliers' delemates st Cardiff,yest," day, plenary powers wen' given to the provisional cow' mittee. A contemner, with tr e employers will take place early next week and an earl, ssttlement is now - si dered Certain.</p>
