        <p>varaonna Villa, and 00th spoke of it, and of tbesa whom they loved. A barking collie, with erect bushy tail, and the shepherd returning from his flock interrupted the conversation. A. stranger paced alongside the young shepherd. Ile was a middle-aged Spaniards with frank, rough face and bright, engaging eyes. His eyebrows were knit a little, his face furrow with hard, deep lines, the corners of his eyes a . mouth were puckered by age, and a deep seal beneath his left ear marred that side of the fegr sufficiently to make it appear ugly. He was a native of Northern Spain, and his name was Vigo. At the dinner table he was free and jocular' but his face was grave at times; still, a pleasant boar was passed in exchange of thoughts on their native land. Then the three Spaniards left for IleswieL where their tedious journey back to London bega4. The journey was a pleasant one in point, of coal' panionship, and the scenery agreeably beautiful ti eyes unused to such verdant fields as those O$ England in spring. The three Spaniards occupied the top of 010 coach throughout the journey, and, getting quite confidential, Vigo Lleren gave himself out as 8 man of means, his father living in retirement ofteS acquiring i good deal of wealth as a merchant, fill which business Vigo had first joined and Lila' mately succeeded him. He had been holida' making in France, Italy, and England, and wee staying at Grasmere when he heard of the when' ture of the two Spanish gentlemen, and hastened 0 the Cottage to congratulate them on their escape. (To be continued.);</p>
